    <node id="ETHNICITY">
      <data key="d0">ETHNICITY</data>
      <data key="d1">CONFOUNDING_FACTOR</data>
      <data key="d2">A sociodemographic variable included in regression-based normalization to reduce demographic bias in cognitive performance estimates. | Demographic variable proposed for future subgroup analyses and psychometric monitoring as the sample grows. | A demographic variable defined using census-style categories: Asian or Asian British; Black, Black British, Caribbean or African; Mixed or multiple ethnic groups; White; and Other ethnic groups. | Demographic factor central to the referenced cohort study on ethnic differences in SARS-CoV-2 infection and COVID-19-related hospitalisation, ICU admission, and death. The Scotland Census ethnicity reference provides population ethnicity context. Within the available schema, ethnicity is represented as a potential confounding or stratifying factor rather than as a disease or outcome. | Covariate included in logistic regression models, accounting for demographic or social differences that could influence symptom reporting. | Race or ethnicity categories, including African Americans, whites, Hispanics, and Korean elderly participants, considered in relation to brain-behavior associations and brain structural aging. | Self-reported demographic characteristic collected from participants; ethnicity may correlate with social, environmental, or health-related variables relevant to outcome comparisons. | Ethnicity was reported as a demographic characteristic, including Hispanic and non-Hispanic identification. | Ethnicity is identified in an article title as a demographic factor interacting with deprivation in relation to COVID-19 mortality risk. | A demographic factor assessed for possible influence on post-COVID cognitive trajectories. | Patient-reported demographic characteristic collected via REDCap that may be considered in analyses of cognitive outcomes. | Participant characteristic used for matching infected participants and controls to reduce demographic confounding.</data>
