    <node id="THALAMIC FUNCTIONAL CONNECTIVITY">
      <data key="d0">THALAMIC FUNCTIONAL CONNECTIVITY</data>
      <data key="d1">BIOLOGICAL_MECHANISM</data>
      <data key="d2">A thalamic node was identified as exerting a critical role in predicting individual differences in depression during the COVID-19 pandemic. | Functional connectivity involving the thalamus, described in prior multiple sclerosis fatigue research as increased in fatigued patients, potentially reflecting a compensatory response to alleviate fatigue symptoms. | Dynamic functional connectivity is mentioned in the cited sleep-deprivation article title as a brain-network property showing abnormal temporal variability after sleep deprivation. | Thalamocortical functional connectivity is identified in a cited resting-state fMRI study as a brain connectivity mechanism modulated in major depressive disorder during body-mind relaxation meditation. | Thalamic functional connectivity is highlighted as relevant to ME/CFS pathophysiology, particularly because thalamic hyperconnectivity normalized after HBOT and was associated with clinical response. | Functional connectivity derived from seed ROIs in the bilateral thalamus and thalamic subregions, analyzed before and after treatment and in comparison with healthy controls. | A neural connectivity pattern investigated in patients with chronic fatigue syndrome in a referenced arterial spin labelling fMRI study. | A time-varying neural connectivity pattern investigated in patients with chronic fatigue syndrome in a referenced arterial spin labelling fMRI study.</data>
      <data key="d3">0.95</data>
