    <node id="MENTAL HEALTH PROBLEMS">
      <data key="d0">MENTAL HEALTH PROBLEMS</data>
      <data key="d1">CONFOUNDING_FACTOR</data>
      <data key="d2">Psychological and psychiatric problems discussed in reviews of mental health during the COVID-19 pandemic; these may act as contextual or confounding factors in studies of COVID-19-related health or cognitive outcomes. | Psychological or psychiatric difficulties mentioned alongside cognitive decline as outcomes that may be influenced by social connectedness and pandemic-related restrictions. These problems may also complicate interpretation of cognitive changes. | Mental-health outcome domain assessed repeatedly in all participants at T0 through T3. Relevant as a possible confounder for cognitive complaints in other contexts, though no cognitive outcomes are reported in this passage. | Broad category referenced in the review on the epidemiology of mental health problems in COVID-19 and in the article on post-COVID-19 pandemic mental health challenges. These symptoms are domain-relevant as potential confounders of cognitive and neuropsychological outcomes, although no specific cognitive test or cognitive effect size is reported in the excerpt. | A systematic review and meta-analysis is cited on the prevalence of mental health problems during the COVID-19 pandemic. This broad category includes pandemic-associated psychological symptoms that may complicate interpretation of cognitive or functional outcomes. | Mental health problems are mentioned as outcomes predicted by childhood maltreatment among adolescents and young adults. | Mental health factors cited as commonly disrupting sleep in incarcerated populations.</data>
