      <data key="d2">Psychological condition reported in long-term mental health assessment of patients recovering from COVID-19. PTSD can affect attention, memory, and executive functioning, so it may confound interpretation of post-COVID cognitive symptoms. | Psychiatric condition referenced in relation to general intensive care unit survivors. PTSD can potentially confound interpretation of post-illness cognitive or symptom outcomes because trauma-related symptoms may affect perceived or measured functioning, although the excerpt does not directly link PTSD to COVID-19 cognitive outcomes. | A psychiatric condition mentioned in a cited article title. It may be relevant as a non-infection-related factor associated with cognition and alcohol dependence. | Psychiatric condition discussed in the COVID-19 healthcare-professional literature. In infection-cognition research, PTSD could confound associations between respiratory infection exposure and cognitive complaints or performance, although the excerpt itself does not report cognitive measures. | A psychiatric condition referenced in relation to WWC1/KIBRA. In cognitive studies, PTSD may influence memory-related outcomes and therefore can function as a potential confounding factor. | Psychiatric condition cited in relation to smaller hippocampal volume in a multisite ENIGMA-PGC study. PTSD may independently influence cognition, stress physiology, and hippocampal structure, making it a relevant potential confounder in infection-related cognitive or neuroimaging studies. | A trauma-related psychiatric condition referenced as post-traumatic stress disorder and PTSD symptoms. In cognition-focused studies, PTSD symptoms may be relevant as a psychiatric factor associated with selective cognitive impairments, including cognitive flexibility. | Excluded psychiatric diagnosis. PTSD may affect memory, attention, sleep, stress physiology, and arousal. | Psychiatric condition mentioned in relation to working memory. In studies of cognition, PTSD may independently influence cognitive performance and therefore act as a potential confounder. | Psychiatric condition examined alongside neurologic symptom complexes in Gulf War era veterans and potentially relevant when interpreting biomarker differences. | A neuropsychiatric condition listed as an adverse posttraumatic sequela after traumatic stress exposure. It may independently influence cognitive or neuropsychiatric assessments, so it is typed as a confounding factor in the available schema. | PTSD diagnostic/status variable that may influence observed immune-system and microbiome differences. | A psychiatric condition referenced in the cited study. PTSD may influence inflammatory biology and cognitive or neuropsychological outcomes, so it can act as a confounding factor in infection-cognition analyses. | Listed among exclusionary psychiatric diagnoses. PTSD may independently affect sleep, arousal, memory, attention, and brain function. | PTSD was associated with greater insomnia prevalence in the Chinese carceral study.</data>
