    <node id="COVID-19 PANDEMIC">
      <data key="d0">COVID-19 PANDEMIC</data>
      <data key="d1">RESPIRATORY_INFECTION</data>
      <data key="d2">Pandemic context associated with reported longitudinal and cross-sectional changes in public mental health, including increased anxiety and depressive symptoms compared with the pre-COVID-19 period. | Mentioned only in a cited reference title about chronic health crises and emergency medicine in war-torn Yemen. The passage itself does not describe COVID-19-related cognitive outcomes, neuropsychological testing, disease severity, follow-up, or biological mechanisms. | Pandemic context associated with COVID-19, during which surgical facemask use became widespread. The passage does not report cognitive sequelae of infection itself; COVID-19 is mainly the public-health context for increased mask exposure. | The pandemic context that partly motivated the remote study design because of travel restrictions and concerns about older participants attending hospital. | Mentioned only as the infectious-disease context for a cited study on pressure perception and anxiety in hemodialysis patients. The excerpt does not report cognitive outcomes, healthcare-worker cohorts, neuropsychological tests, severity strata, follow-up timepoints, mechanisms, or effect sizes related to COVID-19. | Referenced as the coronavirus pandemic context for healthcare workers facing outbreak-related occupational stress and possible post-pandemic PTSD symptoms. The excerpt does not report direct neuropsychological testing or quantified cognitive outcomes specifically attributable to COVID-19 infection. | Referenced as the pandemic context in a cited study on the global prevalence and burden of depressive and anxiety disorders in 204 countries and territories in 2020. The excerpt does not report cognitive domains, neuropsychological tests, healthcare-worker cohorts, disease severity, follow-up timepoints, effect sizes, confounders, or biological mechanisms. | The pandemic context in Taiwan in which home gardening began receiving attention as a health-enhancing activity. | The pandemic context in which mandatory quarantine policies were implemented, creating potential social isolation for cardiac patients after intervention. | COVID-19 is referenced in the title of a scoping review about psychological impacts and online interventions related to social isolation among older adults during the pandemic. The excerpt does not report specific cognitive outcomes, neuropsychological tests, effect sizes, healthcare-worker cohorts, or follow-up timepoints. | The COVID-19 pandemic is mentioned as having continued effects on the physician-scientist workforce. | The COVID-19 pandemic is used as a temporal context for a nationwide United States survey comparing college mental health indicators before versus during the pandemic. The passage does not report individual infection status, disease severity, cognitive outcomes, or neuropsychological testing. | Mentioned only as the pandemic that disrupted normal activities in the country and delayed the study timeline. The passage does not report COVID-19 infection status, disease severity, cognitive outcomes, healthcare-worker exposure, neuropsychological testing, biological mechanisms, or follow-up assessments. | Pandemic context associated in the cited work with circadian disturbances and sleep difficulties. The passage refers to pandemic-related life changes rather than directly measuring infection-related cognitive impairment. | COVID-19 is mentioned as a public health emergency context in which a mobile, telemedicine-compatible diagnostic system may be useful, particularly for remote administration. | Pandemic context in which several cited studies examined associations among screen time, physical activity, sitting time, and mental health outcomes. | Pandemic context for the study design; participants had UK Biobank assessments before the start of the COVID-19 pandemic, enabling longitudinal comparison after SARS-CoV-2 infection status was determined. | Pandemic period referenced as an exception during which R