    <node id="RESTING-STATE FUNCTIONAL CONNECTIVITY">
      <data key="d0">RESTING-STATE FUNCTIONAL CONNECTIVITY</data>
      <data key="d1">BIOLOGICAL_MECHANISM</data>
      <data key="d2">Functional brain-network measure reported as consistently altered in long COVID and discussed in relation to cognitive impairment or cognitive slowing. | A neural connectivity-based biological mechanism reported to prospectively encode or predict individual differences in pandemic-related psychological symptoms. Relevant contributing regions included temporoparietal junction, right inferior temporal gyrus, bilateral angular gyrus, temporal pole, fusiform gyrus, bilateral precuneus, and middle/inferior temporal gyri. | A neural connectivity measure discussed in reviews of resting-state neuroimaging studies in psychiatric disorders. | Resting-state striatal connectivity is referenced as a neural connectivity measure related to task-induced subjective fatigue following traumatic brain injury. | A brain-network measure discussed in relation to neuropsychiatric disorders, reflecting functional coupling among brain regions during rest. | Functional connectivity pattern assessed by resting-state fMRI and reported as altered in association with retinal vessel diameter and cognitive performance. | Neural connectivity substrate examined using resting-state functional connectivity; graph metrics from the resting-state functional connectome were used to identify neural correlates of executive functioning and their association with recent suicidality. | Intrinsic functional coupling between brain regions at rest; the text reports both weaker and stronger connectivity patterns in subthreshold depression compared with healthy controls. | A neuroimaging-derived measure of intrinsic brain network connectivity discussed in relation to mindfulness across clinical and non-clinical contexts. | Patterns of brain activation and connectivity observed while a subject is awake but not performing an explicit task. | Functional connectivity of cortical networks and subcortical regions measured using infant fMRI scans to characterize early brain development. | Brain network connectivity estimated from rs-fMRI as Fisher-z-transformed Pearson correlations among 216 cortical and subcortical ROIs, with within-network connectivity summarized as mean ROI-pair z-values. | An altered neural connectivity pattern reported in patients with chronic fatigue syndrome in a referenced arterial spin-labeling fMRI study. | Resting-state fMRI time-series measures used to characterize activity or connectivity in brain networks, including frontoparietal and default mode networks. | Brain functional network measure reported to decline in association with vascular risk factors among cognitively unimpaired individuals at risk for Alzheimer's disease.</data>
      <data key="d3">0.95</data>
