      <data key="d1">CONFOUNDING_FACTOR</data>
      <data key="d2">Population racial composition is identified as a possible contributor to inconsistent results across studies of post-SARS-CoV-2 outcomes. | Participant race was included as a control variable in analyses when it was not a primary factor of interest; recruitment constraints led to uneven racial representation. | Demographic covariate included in the model as a relevant adjustment or predictor variable. | Recent myocardial infarction or heart failure were exclusion criteria because they could affect study classification or outcomes. | Demographic covariate controlled for in the cited older-adult sample with diverse ethnic origins; relevant as a potential confounder in analyses of sleep and mental health outcomes. | A demographic factor assessed for possible influence on changes in cognitive scores over time after SARS-CoV-2 infection. | Patient-reported demographic characteristic collected via REDCap that may be considered in analyses of cognitive outcomes. | Participant race, including comparisons between NHW and B/AA participants. The excerpt reports race-related differences in women for hormone-associated regression results, while noting no race effect on plasma E1 or E2 levels in men. | Black and White race groups are explicitly referenced in the study of long-term plasma proteomic changes and may modify or confound observed profiles. | Stratification factor in the analysis of associations between childhood experiences and mood challenges.</data>
