    <node id="SEX DIFFERENCES">
      <data key="d0">SEX DIFFERENCES</data>
      <data key="d1">CONFOUNDING_FACTOR</data>
      <data key="d2">Known biological sex-related differences accounted for in analyses; the cingulum association with depressive symptom severity was reported specifically in females. | Biological demographic factor referenced as influencing or differentiating neuroendocrine adaptations relevant to chronic fatigue syndrome. | A demographic/biological factor mentioned in relation to differences in episodic memory performance during early midlife. | Biological demographic factor discussed in relation to depression and treatment responses. | A biological and demographic factor studied in relation to cardiac biomarkers and sex-specific clinical decision limits. | Male-female differences relevant to pubertal timing and maturation status in the study sample. | A demographic factor referenced in relation to examination of the orbitofrontal cortex and potentially relevant to variation in neuroimaging findings. | Biological sex differences considered as a modifying or confounding factor in studies of brain structure, depressive symptom trajectories, and female depression risk. | Participant sex was examined as a key factor that may influence BNST connectivity and neurobiological outcomes during early abstinence. | Male-female differences relevant because females typically begin puberty earlier than males, potentially confounding developmental and sleep-related findings. | Potential confounding factor that researchers are cautioned to consider in future studies of anger and emotion regulation. | Demographic factor examined in relation to psychological distress and other neuropsychological or biological outcomes. | Biological sex is highlighted in the cited literature as a factor associated with variation in brain structure, function, chemistry, and grey matter atrophy patterns.</data>
