health during the pandemic. The passage does not report specific cognitive domains, neuropsychological tests, effect sizes, severity strata, or cognitive follow-up outcomes. | COVID-19 is the respiratory infection driving the pandemic context discussed in the passage. The text specifically notes concerns about preventing its spread during clinical trial participant recruitment. | Respiratory infectious disease referenced in the cited studies. The excerpt links COVID-19 infection to postdischarge assessment, persistent post-infection somatic symptoms, rehabilitation needs, and outcomes after hospitalization, but it does not specify cognitive domains, neuropsychological tests, effect sizes, mechanisms, or healthcare-worker cohorts. | The infection caused by SARS-CoV-2 that is the focus of the systematic review and meta-analysis, with emphasis on its long-term health consequences after recovery, discharge, or survival. | Respiratory coronavirus infection discussed in relation to endothelial and cardiomyocyte invasion, vascular inflammation, cardiac dysfunction, post-recovery microcirculation disorders, endothelial dysfunction, autoimmune phenomena, antiphospholipid antibodies, vasculitis, blood clots, and thrombotic complications. | A respiratory infectious disease referenced in the citation title asking whether patients with alcohol use disorders are at increased risk for COVID-19 infection. The excerpt does not report cognitive outcomes, disease severity, healthcare-worker cohorts, effect sizes, or follow-up timepoints related to COVID-19. | PCR- or antigen-confirmed SARS-CoV-2/COVID-19 infection required for study inclusion. Participants had infection at least four weeks before enrollment and persistent symptoms afterward. | Prior SARS-CoV-2 infection forming the clinical background for the study; participants had COVID infections that were not severe enough to require hospitalization. | Respiratory viral infection from which the 62 pulmonary patients were recovering. The study examines its post-acute psychological and cognitive sequelae, including PTSD symptoms, cognitive symptoms, and post-traumatic growth. | Respiratory infection status reported among participants at three study waves. Participant contamination was 4% at T1, 4% at T2, and 3% at T3; analyses were also rerun excluding participants with infection at any time point, with highly similar results. | Primary infection context for the described post-infection rehabilitation, dyspnea, exercise-capacity, DLCO, and cohort-timing findings. No cognitive domain or neuropsychological test outcome is directly reported in the passage. | SARS-CoV-2 infection referenced as the acute respiratory viral illness after which post-acute sequelae, long COVID, and prolonged cognitive or psychological symptoms are assessed. | Respiratory viral infection reported in the passage as causing olfactory dysfunction, including hyposmia and anosmia, and as being associated with persisting auditory and visual hallucinations. | Repeat COVID-19 infection referenced in the title 'COVID-19 reinfections among naturally infected and vaccinated individuals.' | Respiratory infectious disease referenced in multiple article titles, including detection from chest X-ray images, persistent pulmonary pathology after infection, and infectiousness monitoring in patients. | Respiratory infectious disease referenced in the cited article title, 'Assessment of cognitive function in patients after COVID-19 infection.' It is the infection after which cognitive function was assessed. | Respiratory infection mentioned in the cited article title: 'Impact of dementia on risks of COVID-19 infection and outcomes among older adults in Sweden.' No cognitive domains, neuropsychological tests, effect sizes, follow-up timepoints, or biological mechanisms are reported in the provided text. | Respiratory infection explicitly cited in the reference title as being associated with electrophysiological abnormalities. The excerpt provides bibliographic evidence only, not detailed study results.</data>
