    <node id="SYNAPTIC PLASTICITY">
      <data key="d0">SYNAPTIC PLASTICITY</data>
      <data key="d1">BIOLOGICAL_MECHANISM</data>
      <data key="d2">The capacity of synapses to change in strength or structure, referenced in relation to neuropsychiatric disorders. | Synaptic plasticity is mentioned in a cited title as a possible neural mechanism related to mask effects and face recognition, but the passage does not provide direct mechanistic evidence. | A mechanism for adjusting synaptic connectivity between participating neurons, required for establishing memory neuronal ensembles. | Biological pathway category altered in dorsal hippocampal gene-expression analyses after androgen deprivation therapy and relevant to learning and memory. | Underlying plasticity mechanism potentially induced by non-invasive brain stimulation, although the text notes that direct evidence in humans is indirect and based largely on animal and pharmacological studies. | Exploratory plasma biomarker domain associated with synaptic function and plasticity, assessed using BDNF. The text does not connect this mechanism to infection-related or cognitive outcomes. | Neural adaptability process reported to decline during the perimenopausal aging transition in the female rat brain.</data>
      <data key="d3">0.96</data>
      <data key="d4">9fd2d2b7-7479-41aa-88bf-84e0286f9506_chunk_37</data>
      <data key="d5">9fd2d2b7-7479-41aa-88bf-84e0286f9506,a31e145c-fb56-4bde-99e3-fe50e28f9c77,c1bfbf3a-d728-4b3c-b369-eb64b5b9e128,ed93f40c-bf1d-4c99-b4e6-3ee0682b88c6,d9e6bfa7-e0c7-4041-82d3-54049860c2bf,edc67c59-b539-46b8-9064-e280c6f86904,3e3acef8-1a7d-425a-b577-e9cb913726f4</data>
      <data key="d6">SYNAPTIC PLASTICITY</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SLEEP DEPRIVATION">
      <data key="d0">SLEEP DEPRIVATION</data>
      <data key="d1">CONFOUNDING_FACTOR</data>
      <data key="d2">Sleep deprivation is cited as an exposure associated with reduced resting-state EEG power spectra and functional connectivity after 24 and 36 hours. It is relevant as a potential confounder for cognitive or neurophysiological outcomes because it can independently alter brain function. | Sleep deprivation is explicitly mentioned in the cited article title as a condition associated with abnormal dynamic functional connectivity. Within cognition or brain-network studies, it can act as a confounding factor because it independently alters neural connectivity and cognitive performance. | Condition named in the topic on neurocognitive effects; represented as a factor that can influence cognitive functioning. | A sleep-related factor referenced in the article title 'Sleep deprivation and neurobehavioral dynamics.' Sleep deprivation can independently affect cognitive and neurobehavioral performance and may confound interpretation of cognitive outcomes in biomedical or occupational studies. | Sleep deprivation is described as negatively affecting multiple health outcomes, including memory, mood, cardiovascular health, hormone regulation, metabolism, immune health, and cognitive function. It is a non-infectious factor that could independently influence cognition.</data>
      <data key="d3">0.9</data>
      <data key="d4">e7fe57d5-6381-4c3f-b922-ec8893231f33_chunk_108,e7fe57d5-6381-4c3f-b922-ec8893231f33_chunk_109</data>
      <data key="d5">e7fe57d5-6381-4c3f-b922-ec8893231f33,050a4d09-f879-4946-af0d-10d9e1e68967,9fd2d2b7-7479-41aa-88bf-84e0286f9506,432eead5-bd19-425c-9c09-f7783e9ba546,531dd921-3435-419e-b587-7dfeebb99084</data>
      <data key="d6">SLEEP DEPRIVATION</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MITOCHONDRIAL DYSFUNCTION">
      <data key="d0">MITOCHONDRIAL DYSFUNCTION</data>
      <data key="d1">BIOLOGICAL_MECHANISM</data>
      <data key="d2">Impaired mitochondrial function reported as a signature of post-acute sequelae of COVID-19 and relevant to fatigue, exercise intolerance, and possible ME/CFS-like illness. | Candidate biological pathway inferred from the cited discussion of the role of mitochondria in myalgic encephalomyelitis/chronic fatigue syndrome and its possible relevance to SARS-CoV-2 long-haulers. | Impaired mitochondrial function referenced as a biological mechanism in psychiatric disorders. | A cellular hallmark of aging involving impaired mitochondrial function and energy metabolism. | Central biological mechanism implicated across Gulf War illness studies, including animal models and veteran case-control evidence. | Cellular mitochondrial activity and integrity, described as protected by curcumin and affected in several cited studies. | Mitochondrial impairment attenuated by miR-137-overexpressing EPC-derived exosomes in oxyhemoglobin-treated SH-SY5Y cells. | Cellular energy and mitochondrial homeostasis reported to be enhanced after administration of NSC-derived exosomes. | Identified as an increasingly recognized driver of neurodegeneration in the context of MCI and Alzheimer’s disease-related dementias. | A biological mechanism inferred from serum metabolomics, with lactate and pyruvate emphasized as markers relevant to mitochondrial metabolism. | Biological mechanism mentioned in the title of a 2021 Biomolecules article on hyperbaric oxygen treatment and mitochondrial function. The excerpt identifies it as a mechanistic topic but does not explicitly connect it to long COVID, respiratory infection severity, or cognitive outcomes.</data>
      <data key="d3">0.98</data>
      <data key="d4">5265c67c-1282-4966-b4a4-87b9ba0eed26_chunk_33,5265c67c-1282-4966-b4a4-87b9ba0eed26_chunk_146</data>
      <data key="d5">5265c67c-1282-4966-b4a4-87b9ba0eed26,c69cfb78-198c-48f8-b6ac-7bf289971093,9fd2d2b7-7479-41aa-88bf-84e0286f9506,5e8deba7-6350-4d1f-9bea-c12bfb32945c,2c31a5f9-1331-4ce8-b018-d23282fde00f,c7521404-6f18-4f62-856e-417421405a30,edc67c59-b539-46b8-9064-e280c6f86904,c04453e8-1d88-445b-9399-ac03633dec1f</data>
      <data key="d6">MITOCHONDRIAL DYSFUNCTION,MITOCHONDRIAL FUNCTION</data>
    </node>
