    <node id="OBJECTIVE DECLINE IN COGNITIVE FUNCTION">
      <data key="d0">OBJECTIVE DECLINE IN COGNITIVE FUNCTION</data>
      <data key="d1">COGNITIVE_DOMAIN</data>
      <data key="d2">Primary cognitive outcome discussed in the excerpt. Participants were categorized by whether they developed objective decline in cognitive function, particularly at the three-month follow-up.</data>
      <data key="d3">0.9</data>
      <data key="d4">df64f13c-364f-45a5-8ebe-31b8c3ebccb7_chunk_13,df64f13c-364f-45a5-8ebe-31b8c3ebccb7_chunk_14</data>
      <data key="d5">df64f13c-364f-45a5-8ebe-31b8c3ebccb7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="INFLAMMATION">
      <data key="d0">INFLAMMATION</data>
      <data key="d1">BIOLOGICAL_MECHANISM</data>
      <data key="d2">Proposed biological mechanism linked to cognitive dysfunction and suggested as a possible explanation for some neuropsychological morbidity. | Potential pathogenic process assessed through fluid markers and imaging-related measures. The study examines whether inflammatory markers are associated with clinical and cognitive features of PACS, while noting that mechanisms remain uncertain. | A broad immune activation process referenced across the cited literature, including its role in depression and effects on the brain. It serves as the overarching mechanism linking cytokine activity, neuroinflammatory processes, and brain-related outcomes. | Immune-inflammatory mechanism cited as linking mild COVID-19 with long-term cognitive impairment, encompassing pathways such as cytokine elevation and microglial activation. | Proposed alternative biological explanation for menstrual alterations after SARS-CoV-2 infection in an observational study that did not find ovarian injury. | Inflammatory process listed alongside capillary transit-time changes and tissue hypoxia in relation to symptoms during and after COVID-19. | Biological pathway assessed through blood biomarkers and relevant to persistent symptoms and possible neurocognitive effects in long COVID. | Biological pathway referenced in the cited article on stress, lung health, and inflammatory mechanisms. | General inflammatory process assessed through non-invasive cardiovascular magnetic resonance tissue-characterisation markers, including T1, T2, and extracellular volume. | Mentioned in relation to neurodevelopment and maternal inflammatory cytokine levels. It is relevant as a biological pathway potentially linking immune activation with neurobehavioral outcomes, although the excerpt does not directly connect inflammation to respiratory infection-related cognitive deficits. | Biological process investigated through inflammation profiling in relation to long-COVID clinical characteristics and one-year recovery after hospitalisation. | Listed as one of Austin Baird's research areas in the context of physiology modeling and medicine. | A catabolic biological process that can negatively influence muscle protein balance and is more common in older people. | Biological mechanism implicated in the relationship between exercise and brain health. | A biological mechanism referenced by Dantzer et al. as a pathway linking immune activation to sickness behavior and depression through effects on the brain. In this excerpt, inflammation is cited mechanistically and is not directly tied to a specific respiratory infection, healthcare-worker cohort, neuropsychological test, or follow-up timepoint. | One of the two leading hypothesized biological mechanisms underlying Gulf War illness, supported by reports of abnormal inflammatory blood markers such as interleukin-6 and C-reactive protein. | Biological process mentioned in relation to arginine bioavailability, PTSD symptom severity, and inflammatory markers. It provides contextual support for a possible immune-metabolic pathway relevant to neuropsychiatric outcomes. | Biological mechanism examined as part of the relationship between anger expression and ill health. | Inflammatory biological process identified as part of the molecular pathology of intervertebral disc degeneration. | A downstream biological process described in the cited article title as being induced by chronic intermittent hypoxia in brain regions. It is relevant as a possible neuroinflammatory mechanism, though the excerpt does not provide biomarker data or direct cognitive associations. | Mentioned as a biological process that may be indicated by differences detected on T2 FLAIR MRI scans. | One of the biological processes represented among the 1,075 CSF proteins examined in the study. | An immune-related biological process referenced in relation to estrogen activity. | Inflammation is described as a biological mechanism associated with major depressive disorder. | Inflammation is cited as being disrupted by acute sleep deprivation. It represents a plausible biological pathway relevant to cognition, although the text does not directly connect it to respiratory infection.</data>
      <data key="d3">0.95</data>
      <data key="d4">4b18c63f-2f87-46c1-a3ae-8c4337490271_chunk_34,4b18c63f-2f87-46c1-a3ae-8c4337490271_chunk_58,4b18c63f-2f87-46c1-a3ae-8c4337490271_chunk_59</data>
      <data key="d5">4b18c63f-2f87-46c1-a3ae-8c4337490271,5f73e261-6d67-4a51-a17e-d25381a17114,b0310b04-4fe9-4dc6-8bb6-5040028e4f04,5e787f6f-1547-4d32-aa5f-4a0f4d841129,5265c67c-1282-4966-b4a4-87b9ba0eed26,c69cfb78-198c-48f8-b6ac-7bf289971093,338d5c8e-550b-436e-8de2-d7967ecb69eb,78467a8e-f680-4a51-ab1e-3319fa63fbc4,4f3e9c61-8ff4-4cd2-9644-17d1747f3161,3f321026-7152-49c7-8437-9d8bc137d2de,ed7d7537-d658-4242-92f7-fb95689c31b6,955321f0-cf24-4364-98c1-bbcf8dcd88cd,86d550d9-5e6f-448f-b14a-da2055e63c80,432eead5-bd19-425c-9c09-f7783e9ba546,5e8deba7-6350-4d1f-9bea-c12bfb32945c,2c31a5f9-1331-4ce8-b018-d23282fde00f,104e587b-e1a7-495c-92cb-cf76b7504b4c,8fd1f72e-929f-457c-b76b-3655e2e4b342,c7521404-6f18-4f62-856e-417421405a30,f1a9ac9c-ad81-407f-9d35-d5d0b1f52702,f40f9b83-7586-4b71-869a-2ceb88840fbe,3e3acef8-1a7d-425a-b577-e9cb913726f4,4396816b-63b7-47a7-92e2-b5c781b3160a,be8e1faa-13e6-4111-8446-ccf9848530c7,531dd921-3435-419e-b587-7dfeebb99084</data>
      <data key="d6">INFLAMMATION</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DEVICE TYPE">
      <data key="d0">DEVICE TYPE</data>
      <data key="d1">CONFOUNDING_FACTOR</data>
      <data key="d2">Measurement-related factor that can influence task performance independently of cognitive ability. Low device sensitivity was prioritized during task selection, although SRT and Motor Control were retained despite higher device sensitivity. | The kind of device used to complete a cognitive task. The passage states that cognitive task performance varies systematically by device type, making it a potential source of measurement bias or confounding in remote assessment. | Testing device context, including home computer, tablet, and smartphone use. Device-related sensitivity is explicitly examined when comparing IDoCT ability estimates with raw scores.</data>
      <data key="d3">0.85</data>
      <data key="d4">e83b96c5-4b27-4c65-81de-ecaece6f2395_chunk_14,e83b96c5-4b27-4c65-81de-ecaece6f2395_chunk_40,e83b96c5-4b27-4c65-81de-ecaece6f2395_chunk_47,e83b96c5-4b27-4c65-81de-ecaece6f2395_chunk_49</data>
      <data key="d5">e83b96c5-4b27-4c65-81de-ecaece6f2395,8c67a44e-7846-44b0-8872-5359629554cd,96db7d9b-cde8-428e-87bc-8f2b0c328957</data>
      <data key="d6">DEVICE_TYPE,DEVICE TYPE</data>
    </node>
    <node id="EDUCATION">
      <data key="d0">EDUCATION</data>
      <data key="d1">CONFOUNDING_FACTOR</data>
      <data key="d2">A demographic factor used to adjust neuropsychological norm values, reflecting the influence of educational background on cognitive test performance. | Participant characteristic displayed across clusters and potentially relevant to cognitive performance. | Potential explanatory or confounding variable evaluated with working position; not significantly associated with CFQ score in the structural equation model. | A demographic and cognitive-reserve-related covariate controlled for when comparing olfactory identification and discrimination performance across cognitive groups. | Educational background was identified as a factor that may influence participation and attendance in intervention programs. | Demographic variable collected at T0. Educational attainment is relevant to cognitive reserve and interpretation of cognitive questionnaire responses. | Educational covariate included as a fixed effect in the linear mixed model. | A demographic variable collected in the study. Educational attainment can influence neuropsychological test performance and cognitive reserve. | A demographic characteristic that partially explains differences in cognitive task performance across device types and operating systems, likely because people with different education levels may access different devices. | Demographic regressor included in the multiple linear regression models, with preGCSE as the reference category. Education was expected to show substantial and interpretable association with AS. | A population factor among patients accounted for because educational attainment can affect cognitive test performance and therefore influence apparent patient-control differences. | A sample characteristic that may affect cognitive test performance and contribute to variability across studies. | A component of parental socioeconomic status that may contribute to associations between socioeconomic conditions and neurodevelopmental outcomes. | Participants were required to have more than 6 years of education, and education level was used in block grouping because it may influence cognitive test performance. | Demographic factor compared between cohorts; no significant difference in education was reported. | Participant education was included as a control variable in analyses when it was not a primary factor of interest. | Individual socioeconomic factor identified as significantly protective against depression. | A demographic and cognitive reserve-related variable that could influence cognitive performance. The study reported no education differences between the impaired and non-impaired groups. | Socioeconomic subgroup variable tabulated separately for descriptive statistics and device performance reporting; not explicitly established as a confounder in the text. | Lifetime experience and proxy of Cognitive Reserve that may contribute to interindividual variability in cognitive outcomes. | A covariate adjusted for in cognitive models assessing memory and executive function, including baseline education adjustment in BrANCH longitudinal analyses. | Sociodemographic factor examined for its association with amyloid-beta burden in preclinical familial and sporadic Alzheimer disease. | Educational level or education-related factors discussed as relevant to workplace health and safety culture.</data>
      <data key="d3">0.85</data>
      <data key="d4">86f9ab26-f1be-4c98-8916-d978196e27f5_chunk_8,86f9ab26-f1be-4c98-8916-d978196e27f5_chunk_9,86f9ab26-f1be-4c98-8916-d978196e27f5_chunk_10</data>
      <data key="d5">86f9ab26-f1be-4c98-8916-d978196e27f5,e83b96c5-4b27-4c65-81de-ecaece6f2395,b24179f1-ebda-47e4-9ae7-42c367d15e44,378fc975-98b1-4272-98a2-05ff991584ad,c88820fc-d2e7-4693-97b2-fd50c3d1e971,af5ff8eb-8135-49d4-81fd-6de4008d60d8,38ae95af-7b69-4f7b-a1f0-e672a8f6c0c1,59bdc7c1-081d-427b-bece-fe0f50531795,8c67a44e-7846-44b0-8872-5359629554cd,96db7d9b-cde8-428e-87bc-8f2b0c328957,52c7de5e-fc25-4a5d-9923-173be2895317,a459cfaf-0077-4b3b-bd9e-1701c220e2a5,3f321026-7152-49c7-8437-9d8bc137d2de,45af2bd4-b43c-43db-a81d-ea630c893220,a87cbe0e-cdb3-421a-a394-315bee01fcdf,21b1a9a8-da62-4649-a460-6e9c3ce3d5c5,f4c249e6-968e-45aa-a5c0-98565cad65a3,ed93f40c-bf1d-4c99-b4e6-3ee0682b88c6,571cc5d8-ef93-4de7-b99b-e079f0d332b1,fe2c70b3-e143-43f7-bbf4-4cdc081212c3,4396816b-63b7-47a7-92e2-b5c781b3160a,c4e2b48b-b968-4f16-8949-178e8840bad4,ef5c7cda-3f3f-4419-8d49-bc9f0435945c</data>
