    <node id="COGNITIVE DECLINE">
      <data key="d0">COGNITIVE DECLINE</data>
      <data key="d1">COGNITIVE_DOMAIN</data>
      <data key="d2">Cognitive outcome reported in relation to a history of mild symptomatic SARS-CoV-2 infection, reflecting reduced cognitive functioning over time. | Broad cognitive outcome examined among individuals with a history of mild symptomatic SARS-CoV-2 infection. The excerpt does not specify individual cognitive subdomains or neuropsychological tests. | Cognitive outcome mentioned in cited long COVID literature, particularly in association with brainstem hypometabolism. | A broader cognitive outcome reported after COVID-19. The cited systematic review is described as revealing substantial cognitive decline, with working memory specifically affected. | Cognitive deterioration discussed as a potential consequence or pathway-related outcome associated with the COVID-19 pandemic and dementia risk. | Worsening cognitive functioning over time in older adults, including cognitively unimpaired individuals and those with mild cognitive impairment. | General cognitive adverse effect noted in relation to H1 antihistamine blockers. This is distinct from, but potentially overlapping with, long COVID cognitive symptoms such as concentration difficulty. | Cognitive health state in older adults targeted by non-pharmacological intervention guidelines, particularly physical activity recommendations. | A decline in cognitive functioning mentioned in related content on post-COVID-19 syndrome, specifically in relation to whether it corresponds to persistent neuronal or astrocytic damage. | Progressive worsening of cognition discussed in relation to chronic pulmonary disease, hypoxemia, systemic inflammation, oxidative stress, and neurodegenerative processes. | Longitudinal worsening of cognitive performance reported to be steeper after the onset of the COVID-19 pandemic in studies that had begun before the pandemic. | A broad reduction in cognitive performance mentioned in the title of a cited study on mental fatigue and frontoparietal subnetwork flexibility. | Cognitive deterioration discussed as a clinical concern in T2DM patients, potentially related to altered hippocampal intrinsic brain activity. | Early deterioration in cognitive functioning that the article aims to detect using scalable, repeatable, and relatively non-invasive strategies. | Progressive worsening of cognition in Parkinson’s disease, motivating the development of technologies for monitoring patients. | The clinical cognitive outcome of interest, described as progressively worsening across control, SCD, MCI, and probable AD groups. | A decline in cognitive functioning referenced as being associated with reduced system segregation in aging. | A cognition-related outcome included in the literature search terms and relevant to dementia assessment and progression. | Age-associated worsening in cognitive function, discussed as a key late-life change that may occur alongside muscle loss and may be slowed by exercise. | Longitudinal worsening in cognition evaluated as an outcome in suspected prodromal Alzheimer’s disease clinical-trial research. | Cognition-related outcome explicitly mentioned as something that urban green spaces may help prevent during COVID-19-related lockdown conditions. | Cognitive health factor; more severe cognitive decline was associated with greater financial exploitation risk. | Cognitive outcome explicitly mentioned in the cited article title on depressive symptoms and cognitive decline among community-dwelling older adults. | Decline in cognitive functioning described as one important impact of neurodegeneration in older individuals. | Cognitive decline is mentioned as a possible consequence of inadequate or poor sleep quality. | Longitudinal worsening of cognition referenced in reserve-related literature, relevant as a broader cognitive outcome that may be influenced by cognitive or brain reserve. | Broad cognitive outcome mentioned in the cited article title, “Are infections associated with cognitive decline and neuroimaging outcomes?” The excerpt does not identify specific cognitive domains, tests, or quantitative effect estimates. | A cognitive outcome construct referenced in relation to self and informant perception and neuropsychological testing. | Longitudinal cognitive worsening between the two assessment timepoints. Infected participants showed larger average decline than controls, although the abstract does not specify the exact test or cognitive subdomain. | Broad decline in cognitive function referenced in a study on grey matter loss across stages of cognitive decline and Alzheimer’s disease development. | Cognitive outcome referenced in studies examining associations with Lewy body disease and Alzheimer’s disease. | Longitudinal worsening in cognitive functioning, evaluated in relation to modifiable risk factors and behavioral questionnaire measures.</data>
      <data key="d3">0.98</data>
