    <node id="HYPOXIA">
      <data key="d0">HYPOXIA</data>
      <data key="d1">BIOLOGICAL_MECHANISM</data>
      <data key="d2">Reduced oxygen availability listed as a potential contributor to cognitive effects after SARS-CoV-2 infection, especially biologically relevant in severe COVID-19 and ICU-level illness. | A potential biological mechanism arising from shortness of breath that could contribute to CNS dysfunction and cognitive consequences. | Reduced blood oxygenation during SARS-CoV-2 infection, identified as a contributor to neurovascular disease and a plausible pathway to neurological or cognitive injury. | Reduced tissue oxygenation observed as a pathophysiological process in infected nonhuman primates and proposed as a contributor to neurological or neuropsychiatric manifestations after SARS-CoV-2 infection. | Reduced oxygen availability listed as an indirect mechanism contributing to cardiomyocyte and vascular endothelial cell damage during acute SARS-CoV-2 infection. | Low blood oxygenation listed as a mechanism contributing to COVID-19-related myocardial injury. | Possible biological mechanism referenced in cited literature as potentially contributing to long-term cognitive impairments following COVID-19. | Reduced blood oxygenation proposed as a mechanism contributing to cognitive decline in patients with pulmonary disease and respiratory compromise. | A physiological change reported in Antarctic winter-over personnel. Hypoxia is a plausible biological pathway through which extreme-environment exposure could influence brain function and cognitive performance. | Proposed mechanism by which reduced oxygenation during or after acute COVID-19 illness may contribute to later cognitive dysfunction.</data>
      <data key="d3">0.88</data>
