    <node id="RECALL BIAS">
      <data key="d0">RECALL BIAS</data>
      <data key="d1">CONFOUNDING_FACTOR</data>
      <data key="d2">Bias affecting the quality of retrospectively reported baseline data, potentially distorting interpretation of cognitive status before and after COVID-19. | Potential limitation affecting retrospective reporting of symptoms during the acute infection phase when assessed at follow-up. | Misremembering menstrual cycle regularity or cessation, which may lead to misclassification of menopausal status. | A potential bias introduced when retrospective self-reports are affected by participants' current emotional states, influencing how past experiences are remembered or interpreted. | Bias that may arise when participants inaccurately remember prior mental health history. | Questionnaire-based subjective answers may be susceptible to inaccuracies in participants’ recollections, creating potential discrepancies between past experiences and survey responses. | A measurement-related bias in retrospective self-reporting, where longer recall windows can distort reported symptom or fatigue experiences. | Participants' retrospective responses about experiences before lockdown may contain recall bias. | Potential bias caused by repeated administration of the same cognitive assessment, especially with frequent testing, which may distort apparent cognitive change over time. | A limitation of self-report questionnaires that may reduce the accuracy of sunlight exposure estimates.</data>
      <data key="d3">0.87</data>
