      <data key="d1">DISEASE_SEVERITY</data>
      <data key="d2">Milder acute COVID-19 severity category mentioned in relation to Shanley et al. reporting follow-up MoCA data at 6 months after mild illness. | Non-severe COVID-19 severity category mentioned in a cited study reporting neurocognitive deficits after recovery from mild infection. | Mild severity category of COVID-19 specifically referenced in studies of neurocognitive deficits after recovery and cognitive complaints after infection. | Mild COVID-19 severity category in which a specific visuoconstructive deficit was detected in approximately one-quarter of individuals. | Non-severe COVID-19 illness category that is the main focus of the study; the abstract emphasizes that mild, often non-hospitalized cases represent a large but understudied portion of COVID-19 infections. | Mild COVID-19 severity subgroup referenced in a favipiravir clinical study, where favipiravir did not show a better duration of viral clearance. | Mild acute COVID-19 severity category referenced in cited literature linking mild infection with inflammation and long-term cognitive impairment. | Non-severe acute COVID-19 category mentioned in relation to persistence of SARS-CoV-2 RNA in lung tissue after mild infection, indicating that biological persistence can occur even after less severe disease. | Clinical severity category referenced in a cited study of symptoms and functional impairment assessed 8 months after mild COVID-19 among health care workers. | Disease severity category in the cited healthcare-worker cohort, where symptoms and functional impairment were assessed up to 8 months after infection. No cognition-specific outcome is reported for this severity subgroup. | Non-severe symptomatic COVID-19 category included in the infected worker cohort; participants had mild rather than hospitalized or severe disease. | Mild acute COVID-19 illness referenced in a cited healthcare-worker study assessing symptoms and functional impairment 8 months later. | Clinical severity category explicitly referenced in the citation suggesting that even mild COVID-19 may be associated with brain changes.</data>
