      <data key="d1">CONFOUNDING_FACTOR</data>
      <data key="d2">Specific chronic disease mentioned as connected to cognitive deterioration and therefore relevant when evaluating post-COVID cognitive failure. | Comorbidity considered as a potential confounder in infection-outcome association analyses; adjustment reportedly had minimal effect. | Component of the aggregated vascular risk score and a possible contributor to altered cerebral perfusion or cognition. | Common non-communicable disease and vascular risk factor noted among prevalent health factors in the MENA region. | A clinical condition examined in relation to risk factors for depression among elderly adults living at home in China. | A cardiac risk factor collected during the semistructured interview. It may influence cardiovascular prognosis and rehabilitation outcomes. | Medical condition in elderly subjects living at home in China, referenced in relation to risk factors for depression. | A metabolic syndrome factor described as tied to impaired energy metabolism and alleviated by mitochondrial supports. As a vascular/metabolic condition, it could confound analyses of health or cognition if present in a study population, though the excerpt itself does not specify a direct cognitive outcome. | Clinical comorbidity listed as part of the CHANeL predictor set for oxygen requirement in COVID-19 patients. It is extracted as a potential health-related covariate or confounding factor; no cognition-specific association is provided. | Vascular comorbidity adjusted for as a potential contributor to cognitive outcomes. | Health condition included as an adjusted covariate because vascular risk may relate to both infection hospitalization and cognitive outcomes. | A prevalent baseline comorbidity reported in 67% of participants. It is relevant as a vascular risk factor that may influence cognitive vulnerability. | A cardiovascular factor explicitly mentioned as absent in the cerebral blood flow study. Because hypotension can independently reduce cerebral perfusion, its absence helps clarify that reduced cerebral blood flow was not dependent on low blood pressure in the cited finding.</data>
