      <data key="d1">RESPIRATORY_INFECTION</data>
      <data key="d2">Clinical illness caused by SARS-CoV-2; the severity of the acute phase was evaluated as a potential determinant of later post-COVID outcomes. | The acute respiratory infection whose recovered patients were evaluated for persistent post-acute neuropsychological and cognitive consequences. | Acute SARS-CoV-2 respiratory infection discussed as the antecedent illness for acute neurological complications and persistent post-acute cognitive symptoms. | The antecedent SARS-CoV-2-related illness referenced in studies of post-recovery neurocognitive deficits, cognitive complaints, cognitive impairment, and persistent neurocognitive symptoms. | Respiratory infectious disease under study; the article focuses on patients recovering from COVID-19 and examines behavioral, functional, and structural brain alterations. | Respiratory infection referenced in the cited study title 'Cognition and memory after Covid-19 in a large community sample.' Its relevance in this excerpt is as an infectious exposure associated with subsequent cognitive and memory assessment. | Respiratory viral infection discussed as potentially causing persistent respiratory, neurological, cognitive, and neuropsychiatric sequelae after recovery. | Respiratory infection discussed as the antecedent condition for post-infection cognitive failure. The study concludes that cognitive failure after COVID-19 is multifactorial and not solely explained by COVID-19 severity. | Respiratory infectious disease discussed as producing post-acute neurological consequences and as disproportionately burdening marginalized communities. | Acute respiratory infection diagnosed in the prospective cohort. The study assessed persistent post-infection outcomes up to 24 months, including long COVID symptoms with cognitive complaints such as amnesia and difficulty concentrating. | Respiratory infection defining the exposed ICU survivor group in this retrospective cohort study of post-intensive-care outcomes during the COVID-19 pandemic. | Coronavirus disease 2019 is the central respiratory infection discussed in the excerpt, with direct health impacts and broader pandemic-related effects. | Coronavirus disease 2019, explicitly described as being caused by SARS-CoV-2. It is the central infection discussed in the introduction and in the listed article titles on post-COVID-19 condition and long COVID. The excerpt does not provide specific cognitive-domain findings. | Respiratory infectious disease referenced as the context for the COVID pandemic and for COVID-19 patients with transient smell loss. The pandemic motivated development of an abbreviated COVID Smell Test using one-time-use odor labels. | COVID-19 is the respiratory infectious disease underlying the pandemic discussed in the article. The excerpt describes its broad impact on psychiatry, mental health burden, psychiatric services, and psychiatric research, but does not provide healthcare-worker-specific cognitive outcomes, neuropsychological tests, disease-severity strata, follow-up timepoints, biological mechanisms, or quantified effect sizes. | Coronavirus disease 2019, an illness caused by SARS-CoV-2. The abstract describes COVID-19 as initially considered an acute respiratory illness but now associated with persistent multi-organ manifestations after the acute phase, commonly referred to as long COVID. | Respiratory infectious disease examined in the systematic review and meta-analysis as the antecedent infection associated with long-term sequelae among survivors, including neurological and cognitive outcomes. | The clinical respiratory disease caused by SARS-CoV-2, discussed in relation to acute illness and subsequent long-term complications. | Respiratory infectious disease mentioned in the cited Science article title on inferring the effectiveness of government interventions against COVID-19. The reference does not provide cognitive outcomes, healthcare-worker cohort information, severity strata, follow-up timing, mechanisms, or effect sizes. | Respiratory infection assessed after clinical resolution for associations with serum neural injury biomarkers and cognitive performance in adult workers. | Coronavirus disease confirmed in eligible participants before enrollment; participants reported prolonged symptoms after COVID-19. | The disease caused by SARS-CoV-2 and the focus of the pandemic context discussed in the passage. The text emphasizes post-acute consequences rather than acute disease severity or cognitive outcomes. | Respiratory infectious disease and pandemic context for the study. The text examines the impact of the first COVID-19 wave on healthcare workers in a Spanish tertiary hospital, focusing on mental health outcomes rather than cognitive-function outcomes. | Respiratory infectious disease described as having physical and psychological effects in survivors, including vulnerability to PTSD-related symptoms and potential post-traumatic psychological outcomes. | Respiratory infectious disease underlying the pandemic context examined in the study. The abstract concerns the longer-term impact of the COVID-19 pandemic, rather than confirmed SARS-CoV-2 infection in individual participants, on older adults' self-reported cognitive functioning. | COVID-19 is mentioned as the infectious disease responsible for the 2020–2021 pandemic during which part of the randomized controlled trial was conducted. In this passage, it is a contextual factor that may have affected study recruitment, hospital stay willingness, dropout, and therapist-child communication. | COVID-19 is the pandemic/outbreak context in which studies examined perceived positive appraisal style and stress resilience. The passage does not describe COVID-19 infection severity, cognitive outcomes, or neuropsychological testing. | COVID-19 is the respiratory infection underlying the pandemic context in the abstract. The text discusses COVID-19-related pandemic stressors in relation to alcohol-use outcomes, but does not report cognitive outcomes, healthcare-worker cohorts, neuropsychological tests, disease severity, or biological mechanisms. | Respiratory infection mentioned in the cited resting-state functional MRI study of COVID-19 survivors. The passage indicates that survivors exhibited altered amplitudes of low-frequency fluctuation in the brain, but does not provide cognitive test results, healthcare-worker-specific findings, disease-severity strata, or quantitative effect sizes. | COVID-19 is mentioned as the pandemic context that increased the need for remote neuropsychological assessment. The passage does not describe COVID-19-related cognitive impairment directly. | COVID-19 appears only in the title of a cited reference about behavior, personality traits, and mental health during the COVID-19 pandemic; the excerpt does not provide direct evidence about COVID-19 infection effects on cognition. | Novel Coronavirus Disease 2019, referenced as the context for the pandemic and outbreak. The passage discusses mental-health trajectories during the COVID-19 pandemic rather than direct infection-confirmed cognitive outcomes. | Respiratory infection named as the pandemic context that worsened already difficult working conditions for frontline healthcare workers, especially ICU staff. The passage supports an indirect occupational-stress pathway rather than a direct post-infection cognitive effect. | Respiratory infectious disease referenced through long COVID and post-acute sequelae of COVID-19. It is the clinical illness associated with persistent post-infectious outcomes. | Respiratory infectious disease referenced in cited studies examining cognitive deficits after recovery and in hospitalized cohorts. | Respiratory infection described as causing wide-ranging long-term sequelae, including cognitive symptoms such as memory loss and concentration difficulties, with greater likelihood after symptomatic and severe infection. | A respiratory infection mentioned as an example of a disorder in which brain fog has been studied in relation to objective cognitive impairment. | COVID-19 is the respiratory infection defining the pandemic context of the study. The pregnant women in the pandemic cohort were described as having no known COVID-19 exposures, so the text does not analyze confirmed maternal infection or infection severity. | COVID-19 is mentioned only in the title of a cited article, specifically in the phrase 'COVID-19 related stress exposure.' The excerpt does not provide data on infection severity, cognitive outcomes, neuropsychological testing, healthcare-worker cohorts, or follow-up timepoints. | Respiratory infection referenced in the related article title describing post-COVID-19 subjects with cognitive impairment. The provided text does not specify disease severity, follow-up interval, effect size, or cohort details. | Clinical disease caused by SARS-CoV-2, referenced through the phrase 'COVID-19 survivors.' It is relevant as the acute respiratory infection after which post-acute sequelae may be observed. | COVID-19 is the pandemic context that led to lockdown measures in Germany and was treated as a broad global stressor. The excerpt does not report SARS-CoV-2 infection severity, neuropsychological testing, cognitive impairment outcomes, or quantified cognitive effect sizes. | Mentioned only as the pandemic context that limited face-to-face therapeutic interaction and increased the relevance of remote online interventions. The text does not describe COVID-19 infection status, disease severity, cognitive sequelae, mechanisms, effect sizes, follow-up timepoints, or healthcare-worker outcomes. | Referenced through a cited study conducted during the COVID-19 pandemic. The provided text does not report cognitive outcomes, neuropsychological tests, or effect sizes related to COVID-19. | COVID-19 is described as a pandemic respiratory infection that exposed health-system vulnerabilities and disrupted access to dementia care and neurological services in underserved regions, including MENA. | Respiratory infectious disease referenced through topic titles on sleep quality and mental health during the COVID-19 pandemic and among healthcare workers during COVID-19. The text does not specify confirmed infection status, disease severity, cognitive outcomes, or neuropsychological testing. | COVID-19 is mentioned as an infectious disease being studied during the pandemic. Ryan Au Yeung uses Mendelian randomization to explore determinants, such as glycaemic traits, that may contribute to COVID-19 susceptibility. No cognitive outcomes, disease severity categories, healthcare-worker cohorts, neuropsychological tests, follow-up timepoints, or effect sizes are described in the provided text. | Mentioned as the pandemic context in which facemask wearing became widespread. The text does not report COVID-19 infection status, severity, neuropsychological outcomes, or follow-up data. | COVID-19 is mentioned as the respiratory infection defining the pandemic context for a meta-analysis of anxiety symptoms among Chinese university students. The passage does not describe COVID-19-related cognitive outcomes, neuropsychological testing, disease severity, healthcare-worker cohorts, or biological mechanisms. | COVID-19 is referenced as the pandemic context that contributed to choosing a remote study design, through travel restrictions, lockdowns, and concerns about older adults attending hospital settings. The excerpt does not report COVID-19 severity, cognitive domains, neuropsychological testing, effect sizes, follow-up timepoints, biological mechanisms, or healthcare-worker cohorts. | Mentioned only as contextual background in the cited phrase “post-Covid-19 pandemic period.” The passage does not report COVID-19 infection status, severity, cognitive outcomes, neuropsychological testing, healthcare-worker exposure, follow-up after infection, or effect sizes. | Coronavirus respiratory infection referenced through the COVID-19 pandemic as a context in which healthcare workers may experience post-traumatic stress-related symptoms. | COVID-19 is mentioned as the global pandemic that affected trial logistics by allowing some study visits to be conducted remotely because of local travel and clinic restrictions. | Respiratory infectious disease referenced in the citation 'The role of IgA in COVID-19.' The passage provides an immunological context but does not report disease severity, healthcare-worker cohort data, follow-up timing, or cognitive outcomes after infection. | COVID-19 is the pandemic respiratory-infection context for the study. The article discusses COVID-19-related public stress and behavioral responses such as home gardening, but it does not report cognitive outcomes, neuropsychological tests, disease severity strata, healthcare-worker cohorts, biological mechanisms, or follow-up cognitive assessments. | COVID-19 is cited as an acute infectious respiratory disease in which retinal vascular findings have been reported. | COVID-19 is mentioned only indirectly through study enrollment pauses during lockdowns between 2019 and 2025; the text does not describe COVID-19 infection status, severity, cognitive effects, or follow-up outcomes. | A respiratory infection mentioned only in a cited article title, where the social implications of COVID-19 were associated with exacerbation of visual hallucinations in Charles Bonnet syndrome. | Respiratory infection mentioned only in a cited reference about physical and mental health consequences of social isolation and loneliness in the context of COVID-19. The excerpt does not present COVID-19-specific cognitive findings, severity strata, testing, or effect sizes. | COVID-19 is the respiratory infection underlying the pandemic context in which mandatory quarantine measures were implemented. The study focuses on the psychological effects of COVID-19-related quarantine rather than direct infection effects. | COVID-19 is mentioned only as part of article titles or citation fragments referring to lockdown, pandemic, and outbreak contexts. The text does not provide extractable evidence about healthcare-worker cohorts, cognitive domains, neuropsychological testing, disease severity, biological mechanisms, effect sizes, or follow-up timepoints. | COVID-19 is mentioned only as part of the title of a cited study examining physical activity, stress, and quality of life among community-dwelling older adults in Shenzhen during the post-COVID-19 pandemic period. | COVID-19 is mentioned only in a cited reference title about barriers and facilitators to resuming in-person psychotherapy during the COVID-19 pandemic. The excerpt does not report COVID-19 infection outcomes, cognitive domains, neuropsychological testing, severity categories, follow-up timepoints, or effect sizes. | COVID-19 is mentioned only as the event associated with China's lowest GDP growth rate in 2020. The passage does not provide information about cognitive outcomes, disease severity, healthcare-worker cohorts, neuropsychological tests, biological mechanisms, or follow-up timepoints. | COVID19 is mentioned in the title of a cited article discussing past epidemics, natural disasters, COVID19, and mental health. The excerpt identifies COVID19 as an infectious disease context but does not provide details about healthcare worker cohorts, cognitive outcomes, severity, follow-up timing, mechanisms, or measured effect sizes. | Respiratory infection referenced through citations on post-COVID-19 sequelae and long-term neuropsychiatric consequences. | COVID-19 is mentioned only as the pandemic context in which a remote pragmatic randomized clinical trial evaluated mental health apps for distress. The excerpt does not report COVID-19 infection status, disease severity, cognitive domains, neuropsychological tests, biological mechanisms, follow-up timepoints, or quantified cognitive effect sizes. | Respiratory infection/pandemic context after which increases in adolescent eating disorders were reported. | COVID-19 is mentioned only within a cited study title about oral microbiome dysbiosis, symptom duration, and long-COVID. The excerpt does not report cognitive outcomes, neuropsychological testing, healthcare-worker data, disease severity strata, or effect sizes related to COVID-19. | COVID-19 is mentioned only as the context of cited pandemic-era mental health studies. The passage does not report cognitive outcomes, disease severity, follow-up timing, healthcare-worker data, or effect sizes related to COVID-19. | COVID-19 is mentioned through the COVID-19 pandemic, which complicated endovascular treatment for acute ischaemic stroke and created a need to reduce infectious exposure during care delivery. | Respiratory infectious disease referenced through an article title on circadian disturbances, sleep difficulties, and the COVID-19 pandemic. The excerpt is bibliographic and does not report infection severity, cohort characteristics, cognitive outcomes, neuropsychological tests, or effect sizes. | COVID-19 is mentioned only in the title of a cited study concerning Thailand’s COVID-19-related policies and their impact on the stock market. The provided text does not describe COVID-19 symptoms, severity, cognitive outcomes, healthcare-worker cohorts, neuropsychological tests, biological mechanisms, follow-up periods, or confounding factors. | COVID-19 is mentioned as the respiratory infection that prompted a national state of emergency in March 2020 and disrupted study operations. | Respiratory infectious disease providing the epidemic context for the study of Chinese frontline medical staff. The excerpt concerns emotional status during the early COVID-19 epidemic and does not report direct cognitive outcomes, neuropsychological tests, or quantified cognitive effects. | A highly infectious respiratory disease caused by SARS-CoV-2 and the central topic of the Nature collection. The text discusses research areas such as detection, treatment, epidemiology, viral origin, evolution, biology, and animal vectors, but does not report cognitive outcomes or neuropsychological findings. | COVID-19 is mentioned in article titles as the respiratory infection whose impact or related variables are studied in relation to mental health, coping, and broader health outcomes. | COVID-19 is referenced in the article title as the respiratory infection context for biomarker-network analysis involving inflammatory drivers, disease severity, and thyroid adaptive responses. | COVID-19 is identified as a major medical condition targeted by exosome-related clinical trials, with 16 trials representing 27% of the trials discussed. The text emphasizes COVID-19-associated viral infection and lung damage. | Respiratory infectious disease responsible for the pandemic context and associated lockdowns during which sleep, physical activity, sedentary behavior, depression, and anxiety symptoms were studied in South African adults. | COVID-19 appears only in the title of a cited reference about musculoskeletal physical therapy during the COVID-19 pandemic and telerehabilitation. The provided passage does not present COVID-19 as a study exposure or outcome, nor does it discuss cognition, healthcare-worker cohorts, disease severity, neuropsychological testing, follow-up timepoints, confounders, or biological mechanisms. | Respiratory infection after which longitudinal cognitive outcomes were assessed. The text describes post-COVID cognitive impairment that improved over time but left persistent deficits in processing speed and executive functioning. | Respiratory infection referenced through the study focus on post-COVID-19 condition, in which cognitive profiling and assessment are being investigated. | A viral respiratory infection highlighted among post-HCAP incident hospital-treated infections. Executive function showed a strong prospective association with lower risk of viral infections, especially COVID-19. | Respiratory infectious disease mentioned only in the title of a report on remote cognitive and behavioral assessment best practices for dementia care during COVID-19. The text does not report cognitive outcomes, effect sizes, severity strata, follow-up timepoints, mechanisms, or healthcare-worker cohort findings related to COVID-19. | Respiratory infectious disease context for the preprint, described as having strong prior evidence for brain-related abnormalities. | Respiratory infectious disease mentioned only as the patient population in prior clinical trials where photobiomodulation was reported to reduce inflammatory cytokines. The excerpt does not report COVID-19-related cognitive outcomes, disease severity, follow-up timing, or healthcare-worker exposure. | Respiratory viral infection referenced in the cited article title 'Clinical and CSF single-cell profiling of post-COVID-19 cognitive impairment'; it is temporally associated with the subsequent cognitive impairment described. | Respiratory infectious disease context referenced through post-COVID syndrome and the COVID-19 pandemic, after which ME/CFS prevalence is expected to have risen. | Respiratory viral infection mentioned in a cited article title in the context of possible neurologic complications. The excerpt does not report a cohort, cognitive outcome, test, follow-up timepoint, or effect size. | Respiratory infection mentioned only in the linked article title 'Post Intensive Care Syndrome (PICS) in COVID-19 ARDS Survivors: A 6-Month Longitudinal Study'. The excerpt does not provide cognition-specific findings, neuropsychological tests, effect sizes, or mechanisms. | COVID-19 is listed as one of the disease traits included in a GWAS/eGene colocalisation analysis. The passage does not describe cognitive outcomes, healthcare-worker cohorts, disease severity, follow-up timepoints, or effect sizes for COVID-19. | Respiratory infection referenced through the COVID-19 pandemic context. The excerpt states that, in the first year after the pandemic, mental illnesses, especially depressive and anxiety disorders, increased worldwide. | A respiratory infection referenced only to explain that Massachusetts General Hospital Clinical Cases related to the COVID-19 pandemic were excluded from the dataset by limiting publication dates to November 2000 through November 2018. No cognitive outcomes, severity strata, cohorts, mechanisms, or follow-up assessments are described. | COVID-19 is the respiratory infectious disease underlying the pandemic period included in the dataset. The text discusses it as a contextual factor that may influence mood challenges and childhood experiences among young individuals. | Respiratory infection referenced in the cited article title on anxiety among prisoners during the COVID-19 pandemic. | COVID-19 is the pandemic context for the Tkachev et al. (2022) study, which examined well-being, daylight exposure, sleep-wake patterns, and lifestyle changes under mandated social restrictions. The text does not report COVID-19 infection severity, post-infection cognitive impairment, neuropsychological testing, or healthcare-worker-specific outcomes. | Respiratory viral infection associated with long-term cognitive effects in post-COVID-19 condition, including prolonged cognitive dysfunction and specific impairments in memory, attention, executive function, and processing speed. | Coronavirus disease-19 was the exposure of interest. The study compared adults after COVID-19 with non-infected controls to assess whether prior infection was associated with higher odds of cognitive impairment. | Coronavirus disease 2019, described as leading to post-acute chronic symptoms and cognitive complaints after recovery from acute SARS-CoV-2 infection. | Mentioned only in the cited article title as the context of a lockdown; no clinical details, severity, cognitive outcomes, or measured effects are provided in the text.</data>
