    <node id="NEUROPSYCHOLOGICAL TESTS">
      <data key="d0">NEUROPSYCHOLOGICAL TESTS</data>
      <data key="d1">NEUROPSYCH_TEST</data>
      <data key="d2">Assessment instruments used to evaluate cognitive domains and determine whether participants reached normal threshold values. | Clinical testing domain whose correlations with clinical parameters in LCov were deferred to a later publication. The absence of these correlations is described as a limitation that could have strengthened clinical relevance. | Assessment modality used in adults recovered from mild COVID-19 to evaluate cognitive domains and detect visuoconstructional impairment. | Diagnostic cognitive assessment method explicitly noted as not used in the study. Its absence limits the strength of conclusions about cognitive functioning because outcomes relied on patient-reported measures rather than objective neuropsychological testing. | Clinical cognitive testing that is often normal at the onset of self-reported memory symptoms in subjective cognitive decline. | Testing used to determine whether participants were cognitively unimpaired or met criteria for cognitive impairment subtypes. | Conventional cognitive assessment method integrated into the DigiCog study to evaluate cognitive functioning in PCC. | Cognitive assessment instruments referenced as being used to differentiate vascular dementia from Alzheimer disease. | Testing used in the aging cohorts to assess cognitive outcomes including memory performance and executive function. | A MeSH-indexed assessment category in the text, relevant as a method for evaluating cognitive domains such as short-term memory.</data>
      <data key="d3">0.86</data>
      <data key="d4">5f73e261-6d67-4a51-a17e-d25381a17114_chunk_27</data>
