    <node id="LONG-COVID SYNDROME">
      <data key="d0">LONG-COVID SYNDROME</data>
      <data key="d1">RESPIRATORY_INFECTION</data>
      <data key="d2">Post-acute condition following COVID-19 infection, referenced in a cross-sectional study of brainstem Raphe hypoechogenicity and depressive symptoms. Although not an acute respiratory infection itself, it is retained under the closest available infection-related type. | Post-acute COVID-19 condition associated in the passage with persistent biomarker abnormalities, post-COVID complications, neurological manifestations, and potential neuropsychiatric manifestations. | Post-acute syndrome following COVID-19, discussed in relation to neurological signs, symptoms, differential diagnosis, and pathogenesis. | Long-COVID syndrome is referenced in the title of a narrative review on neurological manifestations. Although long-COVID is more precisely a post-acute sequela of SARS-CoV-2 infection rather than an acute respiratory infection, RESPIRATORY_INFECTION is the closest available schema type. The excerpt does not specify particular cognitive domains, tests, or quantified outcomes. | Post-acute condition following COVID-19, discussed in relation to neurological manifestations and potential mechanisms including gut microbiota imbalance, immune dysregulation, inflammation, and psychological stress. | Post-acute condition following COVID-19 infection. The excerpt states that its causes are not fully understood, are likely multifactorial, and that female predominance cannot be adequately explained by the factors discussed. | Post-infectious COVID-19 syndrome cited in a systematic review specifically focused on executive-function deficits in affected patients. | Post-acute condition following COVID-19/SARS-CoV-2 infection, mentioned in the cited clinical pilot study on hyperbaric oxygenation. The excerpt does not provide cognitive-domain findings, healthcare-worker cohort details, or quantified cognitive effect sizes. | Post-acute condition following COVID-19 infection, referenced in relation to gut microbiota dynamics and psychiatric complications. Although Long-COVID is not an acute respiratory infection, it is retained under the closest available infection-related entity type in the schema. No specific cognitive domain, neuropsychological test, or quantified outcome is provided in the excerpt.</data>
      <data key="d3">0.95</data>
      <data key="d4">516f7ce3-73d5-40bf-a988-9d3371580712_chunk_75</data>
      <data key="d5">516f7ce3-73d5-40bf-a988-9d3371580712,5265c67c-1282-4966-b4a4-87b9ba0eed26,af5ff8eb-8135-49d4-81fd-6de4008d60d8,338d5c8e-550b-436e-8de2-d7967ecb69eb,4f3e9c61-8ff4-4cd2-9644-17d1747f3161,fe2c70b3-e143-43f7-bbf4-4cdc081212c3,c04453e8-1d88-445b-9399-ac03633dec1f,5ad22f78-62b3-4abf-9cb7-e4887ad39cc9</data>
      <data key="d6">LONG COVID-19 SYNDROME,LONG-COVID SYNDROME,LONG COVID SYNDROME,LONG-COVID-19 SYNDROME</data>
    </node>
    <node id="EXECUTIVE FUNCTIONS">
      <data key="d0">EXECUTIVE FUNCTIONS</data>
      <data key="d1">COGNITIVE_DOMAIN</data>
      <data key="d2">Higher-order cognitive domain reported as one of the most affected in the post-COVID subgroup with symptoms lasting more than 12 weeks. | Specific cognitive domain identified as one of the most commonly affected areas among patients with neuropsychological deficits after COVID-19. | Cognitive domain in which abnormal neuropsychological scores were among the most frequently observed. Executive-function results motivated further analysis focused on memory and executive tests. | Higher-order control functions attributed to DLPFC nodes, including response selection, task-response modification, and cognitive control processes that may be compromised by Long Covid-related connectivity deficits. | Higher-order cognitive domain evaluated using the Chilean version of the INECO Frontal Screening. In this excerpt, executive functioning includes response inhibition, set-shifting, working memory, and abstraction capacity. | A cognitive domain that can be involved in multiple sclerosis-related cognitive deficits, though the text indicates it is affected less frequently than processing speed, working memory, sustained attention, and memory encoding. | A cognitive domain covering higher-order control processes such as switching, fluency, cognitive flexibility, and executive control. It is assessed here using Verbal Fluency Switching and the Five Point Test. | A cognitive domain referenced in the title of Niendam et al. (2012), which describes a cognitive control network supporting diverse executive functions. | A higher-order cognitive domain involving control processes such as planning, inhibition, and cognitive flexibility. The text reports that structured exercise and healthier lifestyles are associated with improvements in executive functions. | Cognitive domain reported to show deficits in young and middle-aged adults who were never hospitalized during acute COVID-19. | Specific cognitive domain identified as potentially impaired after COVID-19 as part of broader cognitive deficits. | Cognitive domain identified as vulnerable to impaired brain function associated with chronic hypoxemia in pulmonary disease contexts. | Secondary higher-order cognitive domain assessed in the rehabilitation trial. | A higher-order cognitive domain involving planning, control, regulation, and goal-directed behavior. It is explicitly referenced in relation to children with acquired brain injury. | Higher-order cognitive functions described as potentially negatively affected by thalamic dysfunction. | A cognitive domain mentioned in the cited work on trauma, PTSD, alcohol dependence, and family history of alcoholism in adolescents and young adults. | A higher-order cognitive domain referenced by the citation to Diamond's review article on executive functions. | A cognitive domain referenced through cited work on temporal variability and improved assessment. It is relevant to the passage because repeated assessment may better capture fluctuations than single clinic-based snapshots. | A higher-order cognitive domain particularly emphasized as affected in Parkinson’s disease, encompassing mental flexibility, set-shifting, switching, planning, and problem-solving. | Frontal/executive cognitive functioning implied by the cited Frontal Assessment Battery article. This domain is relevant because the FAB is designed to assess frontal-lobe executive functions at bedside. | A cognitive domain assessed with at least two neuropsychological tests, reflecting higher-order abilities such as planning, inhibition, flexibility, and problem solving. | Broad higher-order cognitive domain examined in the study and operationalized using neurocognitive measures of planning, spatial working memory, sustained attention, and response inhibition. | A cognitive domain associated with suicidality in StD. The conclusions specify spatial planning and problem-solving as executive functions relevant to suicidality. | A cognitive domain involving goal-directed control, planning, decision-making, and inhibition. The text states that low subjective well-being may impair executive functioning, reducing the capacity for consistent healthy dietary choices. | Broad impairment of executive cognitive functioning, mentioned in the title of a study reporting that transcranial direct current stimulation improves executive dysfunctions in ADHD. The citation does not connect this construct to respiratory infection, disease severity, healthcare-worker cohorts, or follow-up outcomes. | A cognitive domain explicitly listed among the functions assessed by the computerized cognition testing framework. It refers to higher-order control processes such as planning, inhibition, and flexible decision-making. | Impairments in executive functioning, referenced in ADHD and linked to inhibitory control, interference control, working memory, and cognitive flexibility. | Cognitive domain referenced in the title about modeling executive functions in the metacognitive Wisconsin Card Sorting Test. Executive functions include cognitive flexibility, inhibition, planning, and problem-solving. | A higher-order cognitive domain assessed after SARS-CoV-2 infection. Executive function scores were included in adjusted longitudinal analyses and improved progressively through 42 months post-COVID. | Cognitive domain involving higher-order control processes. It improved longitudinally after COVID-19 but remained at least 1 standard deviation below the normative mean. | A cognitive domain emphasized because executive dysfunction is frequently reported in PCC; assessed using measures such as the Clock Drawing Test, Trail Making Test, and Stroop Color and Word Test. | A cognitive domain described as especially important for rapidly adaptive infection-prevention behaviors, such as responding to changing public-health guidance to avoid COVID-19 and other viral exposures. | A cognitive domain in which one subject showed decline at the 1-week post-surgery assessment among participants who self-reported cognitive impairment at 3 months but did not meet objective criteria for POCD at 3 months. | A cognitive domain referenced in the cited article title on ageing-related cognitive change. The title indicates that ageing can yield both improvements and declines across executive functions, but no specific test, infection-related exposure, or quantitative effect is reported in the excerpt. | Specific cognitive domain reported to improve in a 2022 randomized controlled trial of HBOT in post-COVID syndrome. | A higher-order cognitive domain assessed by the Montreal Cognitive Assessment, involving abilities such as planning, flexible thinking, and goal-directed control. | A cognitive domain explicitly listed under the Everyday Cognition assessment, reflecting higher-order cognitive control and planning abilities. | Mentioned in the title of a cited article on low-income preschoolers' behavior and emotion regulation. The excerpt provides no direct link between executive functioning and COVID-19 or any other respiratory infection. | Executive functions are mentioned in the title of a study protocol evaluating running therapy and sleep among prisoners. The record is marked as having no original data, and the text does not provide any measured executive-function outcomes or link them to respiratory infection. | Executive functioning was the only cognitive domain with a statistically significant adjusted association with prior COVID-19. Post-COVID-19 participants had greater odds of impairment in this domain than controls.</data>
      <data key="d3">1.0</data>
