      <data key="d0">1-YEAR FOLLOW-UP</data>
      <data key="d1">FOLLOW_UP_TIMEPOINT</data>
      <data key="d2">Longitudinal follow-up duration specified in Méndez et al. 2022, indicating assessment of neuropsychiatric outcomes approximately one year after COVID-19. | Long-term follow-up interval used to assess symptoms and return to work in complex post-COVID-19 patients two years after the acute illness or post-COVID condition. | Follow-up interval explicitly referenced in the title 'Two-years follow-up of symptoms and return to work in complex post-COVID-19 patients,' indicating assessment approximately two years after the relevant COVID-19 illness period. | Follow-up duration used to assess prevalence and recovery rate of altered smell or taste in patients with mildly symptomatic COVID-19. | Follow-up interval reported for the assessment of neuropsychological features in childhood and juvenile multiple sclerosis. | Long-term reassessment timepoint mentioned in the study of cognitive dysfunction in early-onset multiple sclerosis. | Follow-up interval referenced in prior longitudinal research on cognitive changes in older COVID-19 survivors. | Follow-up duration used in the retrospective cohort study assessing neurological and psychiatric risk trajectories after SARS-CoV-2 infection. | Longer-term follow-up interval mentioned in the partial citation on 1-year outcomes in hospital survivors with COVID-19. | Longitudinal time interval over which odor identification deficit and APOE4 interaction was reported to predict cognitive decline. | A longitudinal follow-up interval over which cognitive decline was assessed in elderly individuals. | Follow-up interval reported in the cited severe COVID-19 treatment trial. The excerpt does not link this timepoint to neuropsychological testing or cognitive outcomes. | Long-term observational follow-up of SARS survivors that provided evidence about recovery from pulmonary interstitial damage and functional decline. | Follow-up timepoint indicated by the cited study title '1-year outcomes in hospital survivors with COVID-19,' referring to outcome assessment approximately one year after acute illness. | Long-term follow-up timepoint referenced by the article title 'Postacute sequelae of COVID-19 at 2 years,' indicating assessment of persistent or delayed sequelae two years after infection. | Follow-up timepoint in the German study at which acute infection severity was significantly associated with lung function impairment, chest CT findings, and respiratory symptoms. | Long-term follow-up interval from the cited SARS cohort study, during which persistent bone and lung consequences were evaluated. The passage does not link this timepoint to cognitive outcomes. | Follow-up duration reported for the UK Biobank analysis referenced in the text. | Longitudinal duration referenced in a related prospective cohort study of resilience changes among healthcare workers during and after the COVID-19 pandemic. The excerpt does not link this follow-up period to cognitive outcomes. | A follow-up duration referenced in prior literature evaluating cognitive rehabilitation in pediatric acquired brain injury. | Long-term follow-up interval used to assess outcomes after severe pediatric brain injury. | A follow-up period reported in a cited study of original subjects with baseline alcohol use disorders, with a range of 16 to 23 years. | The assessment timepoint specified in the cited COVID-19 survivor resting-state functional MRI study, indicating that altered low-frequency brain fluctuation amplitudes were evaluated one year after COVID-19. | Longitudinal interval over which Aβ-positive and Aβ-negative participants were followed for neuropsychological and EEG changes. | The longitudinal assessment point at which episodic memory and attention/executive functions were evaluated. | A long-term follow-up point used to evaluate health symptoms, quality of life, participation, and health care satisfaction after a Q fever outbreak. | Longer follow-up timepoint in the Katz et al. adolescent study, where a minority still reported debilitating symptoms and unrecovered status. | Longitudinal follow-up period during which mood episode relapse likelihood was assessed. | Longitudinal observation interval specified in the study of executive function impairments and suicide risk in patients with major depressive disorder. | Long-term follow-up timepoint used to assess trajectories of positive and negative symptoms in the OPUS cohort. | The longitudinal follow-up interval specified in the Halvorsen et al. study title. | The follow-up duration reported in the cited study of social activity changes and incident long-term care needs among depressed older adults. | Previously reported long-term follow-up results that were validated by the later 12-year follow-up study. | Long-term follow-up timepoint at which reduced cardiovascular mortality was still reported after prior selenium and coenzyme Q10 supplementation. | A six-year follow-up period reported in the cited study on dialectical behavior therapy for comorbid borderline personality disorder and eating disorder. | Follow-up duration over which cognitive change was evaluated in the Canadian Longitudinal Study on Aging. | Longitudinal follow-up duration for most participants, reported as median 8.0 years, mean 7.1 years, standard deviation 3.1 years, and range 0 to 11.3 years. | Long-term follow-up interval reported in the cited BMJ Open study, described as a 50-year follow-up of the Population Study of Women in Gothenburg, Sweden.</data>
