    <node id="FEMALE SEX">
      <data key="d0">FEMALE SEX</data>
      <data key="d1">CONFOUNDING_FACTOR</data>
      <data key="d2">Female sex is repeatedly described in the cited literature as associated with a higher risk of developing post-COVID syndrome or long COVID. | Demographic characteristic of the cohort, with 39 participants, or 80%, being women. Sex distribution may be relevant when interpreting symptom burden and cognitive outcomes. | Demographic factor differing between 24-month symptom groups; the persistent long-COVID group had a higher proportion of women than the no-symptom group, with P = 0.02. | Participant sex factor; women were more likely to satisfy long Covid criteria. | Patient demographic characteristic listed among factors associated with long-term abnormal CT manifestations during follow-up after COVID-19. | Patient characteristic identified in the passage as a high-risk factor for persistent pulmonary diffusion dysfunction after COVID-19. | Demographic factor associated with financial hardship among adults with long COVID in the cited cross-sectional analysis. | Demographic factor reported as positively associated with mental health issues among healthcare workers during the first COVID-19 wave. | Female sex was identified as a factor associated with higher risk of worse psychological-distress trajectories during the pandemic. | A demographic factor associated with impaired subjective physical and mental status in the cohort; patients with higher symptom burden were significantly more likely to be women. | Demographic factor associated with no recovery after SARS-CoV-2 infection and potentially relevant to differences in persistent symptom reporting. | Sex-specific factor associated with stronger links between midlife cognitive symptoms and later dementia risk. | Demographic factor noted in the passage: postacute COVID-19 syndrome has predominantly been identified in females. This may influence observed risk or presentation across cohorts. | A demographic factor frequently reported to be associated with higher risk of mental dysfunction during lockdown. | Demographic factor associated with depression in Egypt; sex-related differences in depression prevalence may indirectly affect cognitive-outcome interpretation. | The text reports a higher detection rate of depressive symptoms in females than in males, making sex a potentially important confounding or stratification factor in analyses involving depressive symptoms. | Females, particularly older women, are reported to have a higher detection or prevalence rate of depressive symptoms than males. | Individual demographic factor associated with a higher likelihood of depression compared with male sex. | Sex-related factor noted because psilocybin effects on reward motivation and anxiety-like behavior were largely driven by female mice. | A sex-based subgroup used as the comparison group for males in differential item functioning analysis. | Women are described as being more severely affected by clinical depression, making sex or gender a relevant demographic factor to consider in analyses. | Sex category associated in prior research with greater vulnerability to emotional dysregulation after adversity and with more complex, varied, co-occurring adverse childhood experiences. | Sex demographic indexed in the source; may act as a stratification variable or potential confounder in analyses of HIV-related cognitive dysfunction.</data>
