    <node id="LOCKDOWN MEASURES">
      <data key="d0">LOCKDOWN MEASURES</data>
      <data key="d1">CONFOUNDING_FACTOR</data>
      <data key="d2">Lockdown measures are described as a severe and sustained societal stressor during the COVID-19 pandemic. They are associated in the text with worsened mental health and increased psychological distress during the initial pandemic phase, and may confound interpretation of infection-related mental or cognitive outcomes. | Lockdown measures are described as dynamically issued and relaxed over time, potentially influencing mental health trajectories differently at different time points during the COVID-19 pandemic. | COVID-19-related public health restrictions in Thailand described as amplifying symptoms of insomnia and depression and increasing risk of suicidal ideation among individuals with depressive symptoms. | Lockdown measures are referenced as pandemic-related restrictions affecting mental health in the general population. They are relevant as potential confounders for infection-related cognitive or mental health outcomes because they introduce stress, social disruption, and lifestyle changes independent of infection. | Lockdown measures are mentioned as COVID-19-related public health restrictions that may influence behavior and observed outcomes in the referenced study of asthmatic children. | Public-health restrictions referenced alongside the COVID-19 pandemic in relation to mental health impact. In infection-related cognitive or mental-health studies, lockdown exposure could confound observed outcomes, although the excerpt does not provide direct cognitive data.</data>
