    <node id="SOCIAL ISOLATION">
      <data key="d0">SOCIAL ISOLATION</data>
      <data key="d1">CONFOUNDING_FACTOR</data>
      <data key="d2">Pandemic-related psychosocial factor listed as a possible contributor to cognitive outcomes independently of direct viral pathology. | Pandemic-associated social exposure linked to loss of educational activities, loss of structured work, and mental health problems, potentially complicating attribution of psychiatric sequelae to infection alone. | Pandemic-related contextual factor that could contribute to symptoms or functional changes independent of direct SARS-CoV-2 infection. | Social factor reported as influencing post-COVID psychological health. Social isolation can independently worsen mood, stress, sleep, and subjective cognitive functioning. | Pandemic- or disaster-related psychosocial factor described as negatively affecting older adults' wellbeing and cognitive functioning, potentially complicating interpretation of pandemic-associated cognitive outcomes. | A stress-related factor associated with recurrence of alcohol use disorder, with stronger association reported among men than women. | Social isolation is described as occurring during the initial phase of the pandemic and being associated with worsened mental health and increased psychological distress. It is relevant as a contextual factor that may independently influence psychological and cognitive functioning. | A pandemic-related social stressor identified as contributing to elevated mental health burden among pregnant women. | Psychosocial factor reported as a significant contributor to depression among older adults in Egypt; isolation may also worsen cognitive and functional outcomes. | A social factor that can contribute to reduced dietary protein intake in older age. | A psychosocial exposure referenced in the cited article title as delaying the positive effects of running on adult neurogenesis. | Social isolation is partially mentioned in the context of the COVID-19 pandemic. It may contribute to psychological burden and could confound associations between pandemic exposure and mental-health or cognitive outcomes, although the excerpt is truncated before providing details. | A psychosocial condition mentioned as a consequence of stressors in older adulthood and as relevant to psychological distress and mood disorders. | Social-contextual factor in aging populations that may accompany or worsen mood-related disturbances and reduced emotional well-being. | Adverse social determinant of health contrasted with positive social engagement in later-life health research. | Adverse social-environmental condition identified as a contributor to psychological burden and anxiety. | A social exposure assessed for its association with risk of incident hospital-treated infections. | Social isolation is identified as an associated factor in mental illness that may worsen overall health, functioning, or treatment outcomes.</data>
      <data key="d3">0.92</data>
