    <node id="SEX">
      <data key="d0">SEX</data>
      <data key="d1">CONFOUNDING_FACTOR</data>
      <data key="d2">Demographic factor evaluated as a possible influence on risk of developing post-COVID-19 syndrome; no significant impact was reported in the cited analysis. | Demographic covariate added to permutation tests and partial correlation analyses to control for sex-related differences. | Participant sex was evaluated as a demographic factor; Stage 1 participants had a higher prevalence of females than the overall UKMSR population, while no sex differences were observed between Stage 2 returners and non-returners. | Demographic variable collected in the survey and a potential covariate for post-COVID symptom reporting and Long COVID risk. | Sex is explicitly referenced in the phrase 'sex-specific vulnerability,' suggesting that associations between neurobiology and depressive symptoms may differ by sex. | Sex was used as a matching variable when selecting contemporary controls for comparison with invited SARS-CoV-2 cases. | A demographic covariate controlled for when comparing olfactory identification and discrimination performance across cognitive groups. | Pre-illness demographic factor listed as a potential risk factor for long-term sequelae after COVID-19. | Demographic factor associated with variation in MCI prevalence; rates are reported as higher in women than in men. | Participant demographic variable reported in the study; the sample included 38 women and 24 men. | Baseline demographic characteristic measured at T1; around 46% of participants in the final sample were male. | Sex is described as modifying alcohol-related changes during the pandemic: increases in drunkenness among individuals with pre-pandemic AUD symptoms were more pronounced among women than men, while relationship quality was especially relevant for men. | A demographic variable included with plasma GFAP, age, and APOE-ε4 carriage to improve classification of Aβ-positive versus Aβ-negative status. | A demographic variable collected for validation and preprocessing of participant data. | Participant demographic factor included in the predictive analysis. | Participant sex, included as an adjustment factor in the modelling of standardized MoCA score. | Participant sex differed between responders and non-consenters; responders were more likely to be female. Sex may confound symptom reporting and health outcomes. | Population characteristic expected to influence cognitive task performance and therefore relevant when interpreting cognitive differences. | Biological sex of the offspring, examined as a potential modifier of fetal brain development and cortical findings. | Demographic variable whose distribution differed significantly across groups and may confound group comparisons. | Demographic covariate included in the latent trajectory models to help predict trajectory membership and improve interpretive validity. | Sex was used as a demographic matching variable in block grouping before random assignment to reduce imbalance between training conditions. | Demographic factor evaluated across the HC, MDD, and StD groups. The sample included 42 men and 37 women, with no statistically significant between-group sex differences. | Demographic variable considered in cohort comparisons; the cohort was majority female, and age differences were adjusted for sex. | Participant sex is presented as a potentially confounding factor because males and females may differ in brain structure, perceived stress, and depressive symptoms. Analyses restricted to one sex may reduce confounding from these sex-related differences. | Participant sex, assessed as a potential effect modifier in associations between indoor microbiome characteristics and retinal microcirculation metrics. | Potential confounding factor considered in evaluating the distribution of MRI volumes. | A demographic variable collected during the assessment interview. Sex may be considered as a covariate in analyses of cardiac rehabilitation or psychological variables. | Sex was included as a conventional covariate in the machine-learning framework and may need to be controlled for during model training and evaluation. | A potential confounding factor in stress and depression research; the study focused on women only to reduce sex-related confounding. | Sex is mentioned as a moderator in the cited study on second-hand smoke exposure and depression. Within the available schema, it is represented as a potential confounding or modifying factor, although the text specifically describes moderation rather than confounding. | Sensitivity-adjustment covariate added to the models; its inclusion did not change the direction or interpretation of effects. | Matching factor used to pair controls 1:1 with qualified cases. | A biological demographic factor associated with differences in brain volumes and cognitive scores; males had larger brain volumes but lower cognitive scores than females. | Clinical covariate included in the models to adjust associations between microbial abundance and APNS outcomes. | Participant sex, represented by male and female groups in the demographic table, used as the grouping variable for comparisons of age, puberty, IQ, and anxiety severity. | Participant sex showed differential item functioning, indicating systematic response bias favoring either males or females on specific PVI items. | A factor associated with differing BMI patterns across age groups, with girls showing higher BMI at ages 10–15 years and boys generally showing higher BMI at ages 17–18 years. | A demographic factor evaluated for its association with Device performance; the text reports consistent performance across sex, including correct identification of 92.3% of girls with ASD when the Device provided a result. | Demographic factor explicitly mentioned as influencing or stratifying COVID-19 death patterns in the descriptive international comparison. | Sex is listed as a grouping variable across which associations between COVID-19-related variables and health are analyzed among Ghanaian youths. | Participant sex was statistically controlled for when modelling associations between sleep, sedentary behavior, physical activity, and mental health symptoms. | Sociodemographic variable collected during study assessments. Sex may be relevant for stratification or adjustment when interpreting cognitive and health-related measurements. | A covariate categorized as male versus female. It was included among established risk factors and may account for demographic differences relevant to severe infection, dementia risk, and cognitive outcomes. | Patient-reported demographic characteristic collected via REDCap that may be relevant to cognitive assessment outcomes. | Participant characteristic used for matching infected participants and controls, limiting sex-related confounding in longitudinal comparisons. | A participant characteristic examined in relation to age, CSF sex hormones, and CSF protein levels. | Covariate included in comparisons between patients and healthy controls. | Men were included as a comparison group, and analyses examined protein-level differences across menopause stages and sex; no significant age differences by sex were reported. | A demographic variable used to adjust the MoCA corrected total score. | Participant sex was relevant to the observed association between APOE epsilon 2 and cognitive decline, with protection reported in men but not women. | A demographic factor mentioned as a possible stratification or adjustment variable that can influence interpretation of cell-state analyses. | The study plans equitable inclusion across different sexes during recruitment, aiming for a sex distribution similar to recent prevalence data from the health sector or general population in Zaragoza or Aragon. | Stratification factor in the analysis of associations between childhood experiences and mood challenges. | Biological variable identified in the title as having a modulatory effect on resting-state neural dynamics changes in older adults with post-COVID syndrome. Sex may modify or confound observed post-COVID neural and cognitive findings.</data>
