      <data key="d1">CONFOUNDING_FACTOR</data>
      <data key="d2">Post-traumatic stress symptoms reported in recovered COVID-19 patients. Within this schema, PTSD is treated as a potential confounding factor because it can influence cognition, although cognition is not directly measured in the passage. | Post-traumatic stress disorder, reported among surveyed healthcare workers. PTSD can independently affect attention, memory, executive function, sleep, and subjective cognition, making it a relevant potential confounder even though cognition was not measured in the excerpt. | Post-traumatic stress disorder is reported alongside cognitive impairment following COVID-19 infection and may influence cognition-related symptoms or performance. | Post-traumatic stress disorder is referenced in the title of an article about hippocampal volume and overgeneralization of negative context. In infection-cognition research, PTSD or trauma symptoms could confound cognitive findings, although this excerpt does not directly connect PTSD with COVID-19 cognition. | Post-traumatic stress disorder. The passage cites evidence that PTSD is associated with frontal cortex hyperexcitability, approach-aversion conflict during decision-making, and altered reward processing. | PTSD symptoms are reported in relation to the COVID-19 epidemic and home quarantine. PTSD may confound cognitive assessments because it can affect attention, working memory, sleep, arousal, and executive functioning, although no direct neuropsychological testing is described in the text. | Post-traumatic stress disorder was described as prevalent among incarcerated individuals and associated with insomnia and sleep problems.</data>
