    <node id="ATTENTIONAL CONTROL">
      <data key="d0">ATTENTIONAL CONTROL</data>
      <data key="d1">COGNITIVE_DOMAIN</data>
      <data key="d2">The capacity to regulate attention according to task goals, especially during interference or conflict; referenced in relation to complementary roles of anterior cingulate and prefrontal cortices. | A cognitive domain involved in maintaining focus on task-relevant information while ignoring irrelevant stimulus dimensions. It is listed as a cognitive function that can be negatively affected by fatigue. | Cognitive domain involving control over attentional allocation and task-relevant selection, identified as a possible target for selected Stroop paradigms. | A cognitive control domain probed by the flanker task, particularly through conflict monitoring and response inhibition demands. | Cognitive domain cited as fluctuating throughout the menstrual cycle, indicating a non-infectious source of cognitive variability. | Cognitive control domain mentioned in a citation about working memory, attention control, and the construct validity of the N-back task. | Cognitive control domain described as closely linked to oculomotor behavior captured by eye-tracking-based paradigms.</data>
