      <data key="d2">A severe form of COVID-19 referenced through cited literature on patterns of cognitive deficits in severe infection. | More severe COVID-19 disease category referenced in a related article title about subacute cognitive and psychiatric impairment. | Severe COVID-19 illness category in which neurological and psychiatric consequences had already been expected and described, serving as a contrast to the study's focus on mild disease. | Severe form of COVID-19 requiring intensive care-level treatment; used in the text as the clinical context for ICU survival and post-acute symptoms. | Severity subgroup referenced in relation to persistent respiratory dysfunction and altered gut microbiota composition at follow-up. | A severe acute COVID-19 illness category mentioned as a factor associated with long-term abnormal CT manifestations after infection. | Severity subgroup of COVID-19 patients described as recovered and discharged, yet with persistent abnormal chest CT findings three months after discharge. | More severe clinical form of COVID-19. The text states that excessive and chronic alcohol consumption has been proposed as a risk factor for severe COVID-19 and notes biological overlap with chronic alcoholism. | More severe acute COVID-19 category used as the contrast group when the text emphasizes that PASC after mild or moderate illness has notable similarities to other post-infectious chronic illnesses. | Severe manifestation of COVID-19 mentioned in the article title about Galectin-3 as a potential prognostic biomarker in SARS-CoV-2-infected patients. | Severity category referenced in a cited study reporting dysregulation of brain and choroid plexus cell types in severe COVID-19.</data>
