    <node id="SARS-COV-2 INFECTION">
      <data key="d0">SARS-COV-2 INFECTION</data>
      <data key="d1">RESPIRATORY_INFECTION</data>
      <data key="d2">The antecedent respiratory viral infection in the cohort. The study followed patients with Post-COVID syndrome after mild to moderate SARS-CoV-2 infection and assessed persistent neurological and cognitive symptoms. | The viral infection underlying COVID-19, referenced as being associated with neurological and neuropsychological changes. | Coronavirus respiratory infection referenced in a study of neurocognitive outcome following recovery from severe acute respiratory syndrome-coronavirus-1. | Confirmed viral infection required for participant inclusion and the antecedent infection associated with later post-acute COVID-19 syndrome symptoms. | Viral infection associated with changes in brain structure and with fusogen-like effects that can disrupt neuronal and glial function throughout the brain. | The infection causing COVID-19; the study included 73 adults with mild to moderate SARS-CoV-2 infection without signs of respiratory failure. | Respiratory viral infection or controlled exposure referenced in 'Changes in memory and cognition during the SARS-CoV-2 human challenge study.' It is associated with cognitive and memory changes during the challenge-study period. | The viral infection underlying COVID-19. It is described as producing predominantly mild to moderate, self-limited illness in non-hospitalized people worldwide. | Underlying viral infection discussed as having plausible mechanisms that may contribute to neurodegenerative effects and cognitive failure after COVID-19. | Repeat SARS-CoV-2 infection referenced as an outcome of interest in the meta-analysis title on risk of reinfection after primary infection. The excerpt does not provide quantitative reinfection risk estimates or connect reinfection to cognitive or neuropsychological outcomes. | Underlying viral infection associated with COVID-19; the text states that Black, Indigenous and Latino communities in the United States are more likely to be infected with SARS-CoV-2. | The viral infection described as increasing globally and as the antecedent infection after which long COVID can occur. | Respiratory viral infection identified in the text as the cause of COVID-19 and as the reason many patients required ICU treatment during the pandemic. | Confirmed prior SARS-CoV-2 infection in Icelandic adults, assessed in a population-based case-control study 5–18 months after infection for persistent symptoms and objective physical and cognitive outcomes. | Respiratory infection mentioned in a cited study title about longitudinal subjective and objective changes in olfactory function after infection. No cognitive-domain impairment, neuropsychological test result, effect size, or severity-stratified cognitive outcome is described in the provided text. | Respiratory viral infection referenced in the title of an article on long-term psychiatric disorder and psychotropic prescription risk after infection. The excerpt does not provide details on cognitive outcomes, neuropsychological assessment, healthcare-worker exposure, or infection severity. | Underlying viral infection referenced in the phrase 'post-acute sequelae of SARS-CoV-2 infection'; it is the infection after which long COVID may occur. | Repeated SARS-CoV-2 infection described as extensively associated with additional morbidity from long COVID sequelae across multiple organ systems. | Respiratory infection caused by SARS-CoV-2, the virus underlying COVID-19. The text notes its relationship to SARS-CoV-1 and MERS-CoV within the beta-coronavirus clade as a rationale for comparing long-term sequelae. | The viral infection identified as the initiating event after which post-acute sequelae may emerge. | Related coronavirus infection referenced for structural similarity within betacoronaviruses and for its specific binding receptor, angiotensin-converting enzyme 2. | Viral infection exposure underlying both asymptomatic SARS-CoV-2 cases and mild COVID-19 cases; participants were evaluated after SARS-CoV-2 negativization. | Confirmed antecedent viral infection after which post-COVID-19 condition or long COVID symptoms may develop. | Respiratory viral infection mentioned in the title of a cited article about management challenges and the possible role of oral bacteriotherapy as a complementary therapeutic strategy. No cognitive, neuropsychological, healthcare-worker, exposure, severity, or longitudinal outcome data are provided in the excerpt. | The antecedent respiratory viral infection after which persistent symptoms may occur; the text defines long COVID relative to symptoms continuing after SARS-CoV-2 infection. | Respiratory viral infection underlying COVID-19, identified as preceding direct neuropsychiatric sequelae. | Underlying viral infection referenced in the phrase 'post-acute sequelae of SARS-CoV-2 infection.' It is the infection after which post-acute pulmonary symptoms and psychological distress are described. | Mentioned only in a cited reference concerning neuroimaging markers of cognitive fatigue in individuals with post-acute sequelae of SARS-CoV-2 infection. It is not part of the primary study described in the passage. | Participant-level respiratory viral infection exposure reported in the trial sample; 59% of healthcare-worker participants had been infected with SARS-CoV-2. | The viral infection underlying COVID-19, referenced in relation to multi-organ assessment after infection and subsequent long COVID/post-acute sequelae. | Laboratory-confirmed SARS-CoV-2 infection in the Scottish population cohort; the primary exposure used to study long-COVID outcomes against a never-infected comparison group. | Respiratory viral infection monitored in the REACT study in England, with infection-rate data collected from 1 May 2020 to 31 March 2022. | Respiratory viral infection used in the review as the key contemporary example of an acute infection that can be followed by post-acute sequelae. The text emphasizes that SARS-CoV-2 infection is not unique in this respect. | Respiratory infection context implied by the citation 'SARS-CoV-2 specific sIgA in saliva increases after disease-related video stimulation.' The entity is typed as an infection rather than the virus itself to conform to the valid respiratory-infection category. | SARS-CoV-2 infection is referenced indirectly through discussion of national control efforts during the early pandemic. The excerpt does not provide extractable information about cognitive outcomes, biological mechanisms, occupational exposure, or healthcare-worker-specific effects. | Respiratory viral infection mentioned in the title of a cross-sectional study examining its association with soluble angiotensin-converting enzyme 2 in saliva. | The viral respiratory infection explicitly described as preceding post-COVID-19 condition in the longitudinal case-control study of long-lasting cognitive outcomes. | The preceding viral respiratory infection after which persistent cognitive symptoms are described as frequently reported and disabling. | Respiratory infection exposure referenced in the study title as being associated with changes in brain structure in UK Biobank participants. | Respiratory viral infection identified as one of the most frequent infectious triggers of ME/CFS in the post-COVID context. | Infection caused by SARS-CoV-2, listed in the keywords and referenced in similar-article titles as preceding post-COVID cognitive and mental-health effects. | Respiratory viral infection referenced in studies of long-lasting cognition-related outcomes and executive deficits after infection. | The acute viral infection underlying COVID-19; proposed to damage cerebral blood vessels and the intestinal wall through ACE2 receptor binding and systemic cytokine elevation.</data>
      <data key="d3">1.0</data>
