    <node id="OLDER AGE">
      <data key="d0">OLDER AGE</data>
      <data key="d1">CONFOUNDING_FACTOR</data>
      <data key="d2">A participant characteristic more common among Stage 2 returnees and associated in the literature with greater risk of cognitive impairment in multiple sclerosis. | Age-related demographic factor linked with more severe COVID-19 illness. | Demographic risk factor listed for development of impairments after ICU admission; relevant when interpreting post-ICU cognitive impairment because age can independently influence cognition. | Old age is the population context in the cited study linking cumulative life stress with aberrant resting-state EEG dynamics. Age may influence EEG dynamics and therefore should be considered when interpreting neurophysiological outcomes. | A participant characteristic associated with higher risk of more severe acute SARS-CoV-2 infection. | The population context for the cited study on olfactory identification and incidence of mild cognitive impairment. Older age is a major potential confounder for both olfactory decline and cognitive impairment. | A demographic characteristic associated with higher risk of long-term sequelae in subgroup analysis. Older age may influence the likelihood or reporting of post-COVID cognitive outcomes. | Older adult status, including adults aged 60 years or older, characterizes the study populations and may influence baseline cognitive function and intervention response. | Older adulthood is the population context for the cited study on subjective cognitive complaints and is also relevant to the referenced literature on aging, stress, coping, and emotional well-being. | Population characteristic relevant to the studies, as the findings concern elderly or older individuals. | Adults aged 30–65 years and older adults over 65 years had lower odds of being in the fast recovery and deteriorating groups. | Demographic factor associated with lack of complete recovery after symptomatic SARS-CoV-2 infection and relevant as a potential confounder when interpreting post-infection cognitive and functional outcomes. | Age is reflected in the cited populations, including memory-clinic patients over 75 years and adults aged 60 years or older, and is an important factor influencing dementia and cognitive impairment risk. | Sociodemographic factor reported as a dementia risk factor in Jordan; age independently affects cognitive decline risk and may confound analyses of cognitive impairment. | Age-related context for the population discussed; cognitive decline and muscle loss are described as associated with aging. | The text specifically discusses older adults aged 60 years or above as the population in which depressive symptoms are highly prevalent. Older age may act as a confounding factor in studies relating health exposures to depressive or cognitive outcomes. | Age-related population characteristic implied by the citation on elderly mental health during the COVID-19 pandemic. The excerpt does not quantify its role or establish it as an adjusted covariate. | The text focuses on older people as a population with high prevalence of depressive symptoms and substantial adverse health implications. | A demographic characteristic of the sample, with the majority of participants being over 60 years old. Age is relevant because it can influence memory performance and depressive symptoms. | Old age is explicitly referenced in the same article title. Age is an important contextual and potential confounding factor in cognitive studies because cognition may vary substantially across later life independently of other exposures or conditions. | Aging-related context associated with shifts in gut microbial composition and increasing difficulty maintaining gastrointestinal health. | Population context for the cited depression and quality-of-life study; may confound associations between health conditions and outcomes. | The cited COVID-19/dementia article concerns older adults in Sweden. Older age is a relevant baseline factor for COVID-19 infection risk and outcomes, although no specific age range or effect estimate is provided in the text. | Age-related patient factor emphasized in studies of elderly or older adult surgical populations and postoperative cognitive outcomes. | Demographic characteristic that differed between hospitalized and non-hospitalized SARS-CoV-2-positive cases. Hospitalized patients were on average older, making age a potential confounder in comparisons by hospitalization status. | Age-related context indicated by the study focus on older adults; age may influence both neural slowing and cognitive performance. | Age range 60-89 is specifically referenced in relation to CVLT normative standards, indicating age is relevant when interpreting memory and verbal learning performance. | Age was discussed as associated with CSF estrogen levels in women and considered in analyses of CSF estrogen and CSF proteins. | Age status of the rhesus macaques in the estradiol supplementation study; aging may influence brain transcriptomic profiles and hormone-response effects. | An age-related population characteristic explicitly referenced in the title 'Depression and quality of life in old age.' Older age is relevant as a confounding factor because baseline cognition, comorbidity burden, depression prevalence, frailty, and recovery trajectories can differ substantially in older populations. | Older incarcerated individuals showed higher insomnia prevalence in the Chinese carceral setting.</data>
