    <node id="COGNITIVE IMPAIRMENTS">
      <data key="d0">COGNITIVE IMPAIRMENTS</data>
      <data key="d1">COGNITIVE_DOMAIN</data>
      <data key="d2">Broad cognitive outcome assessed after COVID-19 hospital discharge, with the cited study addressing its pattern, severity, and relationship to illness variables. | Broad cognitive impairment outcome addressed in a systematic review of long COVID. | Long-term cognitive outcome after SARS-CoV-2 infection; described as correlating with the severity of the acute episode in a longitudinal cohort study. | Cognitive impairment state mentioned in a cited article title alongside anosmia and biomarkers of brain aging. Although not a specific cognitive domain, it is retained as the closest valid cognitive-related entity type. | Classification of participants as cognitively impaired or non-impaired using liberal, fair, or conservative criteria. Cognitively impaired participants differed from non-impaired participants in age, disease duration, sex distribution, and disease subtype composition. | Level of cognitive impairment among people with multiple sclerosis compared with controls, expressed using standardized scores. | Clinical cognitive impairment category for which the cited threshold is described as commonly recommended. | Cognitive impairments are persistent post-SARS-CoV-2 neuropsychological outcomes whose risk remains high two years after infection. | Broad cognitive outcome reported in cited literature four months after COVID-19 hospital discharge, with pattern and severity associated with illness variables. | Broad cognitive dysfunction reported as persistent in COVID-19 survivors and linked in cited work to inflammatory biomarker findings at follow-up. | Cognitive outcome mentioned as a possible consequence of persistent neuritis and downstream neuroimmune mechanisms in post-COVID conditions. The text does not specify a neuropsychological test or quantified effect size. | Observed improvements in cognitive outcomes after intervention, with greater improvement reported for cognitive training plus immersive VR Tai Chi than for cognitive training plus in-person Tai Chi. | A comparison cognitive-status category for older adults without cognitive impairment in randomized controlled trial reviews of combined physical and cognitive training. | Cognitive impairment outcome whose progression is described as potentially promoted by endothelial dysfunction, impaired neurovascular coupling, and white matter damage after mild COVID-19. | Broad cognitive outcome reported among prolonged post-COVID symptoms lasting beyond the initial infection phase. | Persistent post-COVID cognitive impairment referenced in the cited literature; represents cognitive deficits occurring after COVID-19 rather than a specific neuropsychological subdomain. | A cognition-related outcome mentioned in the childhood brain injury reference, where developmental divergence of structural brain networks is described as an indicator of future cognitive impairments, specifically executive functions. | A broad cognitive deficit state identifying patients who are described as more susceptible to cognitive fatigue. The passage does not specify a particular neuropsychological test or diagnostic category. | At-risk status for cognitive impairment, used to identify potential participants for inclusion in clinical trials. | Cognitive impairment state that the MoCA was originally developed to detect quickly and effectively. | Objective cognitive deficits reported to show more robust associations with cognitive symptoms in post-COVID-19 populations, particularly after more severe or persistent illness. | Participant cognitive status was categorized into ordered clinical groups: Control, SCD, MCI, and AD, with additional supplementary analyses separating aMCI and mMCI. | Cognitive dysfunction listed as one of the core symptoms of post-acute infection syndromes. It is part of the shared clinical presentation of infection-associated chronic disability described in the passage. | Cognitive deficits in people diagnosed with schizophrenia that alternative methods aim to alleviate. | Broad cognitive impairment outcome referenced in relation to major depressive disorder and recovery; included as a general neurocognitive outcome rather than a specific test domain. | Mild cognitive impairment (MCI), reported among older adults residing in nursing homes in Jordan. | A cognitive domain mentioned in relation to schizophrenia, forming part of the psychiatrist evaluator's area of clinical focus. | Broad cognitive outcome mentioned in the cited study title as occurring in COVID-19 survivors. It refers to impaired neurocognitive functioning rather than a specific cognitive subdomain. | Broad cognitive impairment outcome referenced in relation to long COVID, encompassing deficits across one or more cognitive domains. | Cognitive impairment state referenced in studies comparing cognitive profiles by underlying etiology. | General cognitive deficit outcome mentioned in the cited study on interleukin-6 signaling and Alzheimer’s disease-related metabolic alterations. | Cognitive impairment was the cognitive domain assessed using the MoCA. The excerpt reports that this domain remained stable over the study course. | Non-motor cognitive symptoms reported to be more prevalent in advanced Parkinson's disease. | Impairments in cognitive functioning; referenced as an outcome predicted by plasma p-tau217 before clinical onset. | Less severe cognitive impairment outcome examined for prevalence among World Trade Center exposed FDNY and general emergency responders.</data>
