    <node id="COGNITIVE FLEXIBILITY">
      <data key="d0">COGNITIVE FLEXIBILITY</data>
      <data key="d1">COGNITIVE_DOMAIN</data>
      <data key="d2">Executive-function-related cognitive domain in which deficits were observed in the patient group that did not improve over time. | Executive function domain cited as influencing Stroop Color-Word test performance, involving adaptation to competing task demands. | Cognitive domain assessed behaviorally in adults recovering from COVID-19. The findings indicate that both acute olfactory dysfunction and hospitalization had differential impacts on behavior in a cognitive flexibility task. | An executive-function domain involving the ability to shift between concepts, strategies, categories, or response sets. The cited title directly links this domain to assessment with a modified switching verbal fluency test. | An executive cognitive function explicitly listed among the processes supported by the dorsolateral prefrontal cortex. | Executive-function-related domain reflecting mental set-shifting and adaptability, assessed using CTT2 before and after the trial. | Executive-function domain involving the ability to infer, shift, and adapt sorting rules in response to feedback. It is assessed with computerized card sorting tests across different child age ranges. | Baseline cognitive factor examined as a predictor of later intrusive memories. Higher cognitive flexibility appeared protective against intrusions at three months. | Cognitive domain involving the ability to switch between rules, response sets, or stimulus dimensions. It is especially relevant to Switching Stroop and the number-letter alternating condition of Trail Making. | A cognitive capacity hypothesized to protect educators from the negative psychological effects of repeated school-related stress exposure. | Reduced ability to flexibly shift thinking or adapt interpretations. The excerpt states that cognitive inflexibility creates additional difficulty in interpreting and managing life events and may be a suicide risk factor in depression. | A cognitive domain involving the ability to shift thinking or adapt mental strategies, identified in the cited study as being affected by late-life anxiety and depression. | A cognitive domain listed in the cited study as potentially improved by transcranial direct current stimulation. | A cognitive domain reported to improve in experimental studies of nature exposure. | An executive-function domain commonly assessed by the Wisconsin Card Sorting Test. The excerpt does not provide an effect estimate or clinical association. | Executive-control domain involving flexible shifting between tasks, rules, or mental sets. | The adaptive cognitive ability to modify behavior in response to a changing environment. The text states that cognitive flexibility is impaired in people with PTSD and is the primary cognitive outcome assessed in the rodent experiments. | A cognitive domain involving the ability to shift mental sets, adapt behavior, and modify thinking strategies. The cited article directly examines how late-life anxiety and depression affect this domain. | Executive-function subdomain assessed by the Trail Making Test, reflecting the ability to shift between mental sets or task demands. | Cognitive flexibility was assessed together with executive functioning as part of the neuropsychological evaluation. The significant finding was reported for executive functioning/cognitive flexibility as an executive-control domain.</data>
      <data key="d3">0.97</data>
