      <data key="d1">CONFOUNDING_FACTOR</data>
      <data key="d2">A repeated-testing artifact in which performance changes because of familiarity with cognitive tasks rather than true cognitive change, potentially affecting assessment validity. | A repeated-testing effect that can influence cognitive test performance independently of the cognitive domain being measured. The text states that correction factors for practice effects were applied before analysis. | Potential confounding factor in repeated cognitive assessment, referenced through a longitudinal study of frequent repetitive cognitive testing. Practice effects can improve scores through familiarity with the tests rather than true cognitive change. | Potential longitudinal testing artifact minimized by using CVLT-II Standard and Alternate Forms; otherwise could influence apparent cognitive change across repeated assessments.</data>
