      <data key="d2">A subjective cognitive symptom complex in post-COVID syndrome, involving perceived mental cloudiness or reduced cognitive clarity. The cited article focuses on unraveling brain fog through its relationships with cognitive complaints, objective cognitive function, fatigue, and neuropsychiatric symptoms. | Patient-reported cognitive symptom or phenotype described as an important disabling element of long COVID. | Post-infectious cognitive symptom complex linked to SARS-CoV-2 infection, including concentration difficulty and cognitive impairment. | Subjective cognitive complaint described as one of the protracted post-acute symptoms of long Covid after SARS-CoV-2 infection. The excerpt does not specify an objective neuropsychological test for this symptom. | Post-COVID cognitive symptom syndrome described as affecting approximately one quarter of patients and encompassing attention deficits, slowed processing speed, language fluency difficulties, memory problems, and executive function disorders. Its risk was reported to continue increasing through the end of a 2-year follow-up period. | Persistent post-COVID cognitive symptom mentioned among neurological symptoms that continue despite normalization of central nervous system injury biomarkers by 6 months of follow-up. | A symptom descriptor used in the text to clarify cognitive dysfunction in long COVID, referring to subjective cognitive difficulties rather than a formal neuropsychological test. | A self-reported cognitive symptom described as part of subjective cognitive symptoms in menopause. | Subjective neurocognitive symptom listed among the main neurological/neurocognitive manifestations of post-acute infection syndromes. The text does not specify a neuropsychological test, cohort, or effect size for this symptom. | A subjective cognitive complaint identified as one of the most persistent and common manifestations of post-acute COVID-19 syndrome.</data>
