    <node id="MILD-TO-MODERATE COVID-19">
      <data key="d0">MILD-TO-MODERATE COVID-19</data>
      <data key="d1">DISEASE_SEVERITY</data>
      <data key="d2">Dominant COVID-19 severity category in the cited early pandemic data and global estimates. The WHO-China Joint Mission reported that 80% of 55,924 laboratory-confirmed cases in China through February 20, 2020 were mild to moderate, and the text extrapolates that more than 320 million global cases may have fallen into this category by February 2022. | Disease severity category mentioned for non-hospitalized individuals with mild to moderate symptoms of COVID-19. The passage notes that PCR or antigen testing may not have been applied in this group, affecting diagnostic ascertainment. | Intermediate acute COVID-19 severity subgroup used for comparison with asymptomatic community cases and hospitalized severe or critical cases in analyses of long-term sequelae prevalence. | Clinical severity category referenced in connection with elevated neurofilament light chain, indicating that neuronal injury markers may occur outside severe COVID-19. | Non-severe COVID-19 severity category specifically highlighted because PASC occurring after mild or moderate illness is noted to resemble chronic illnesses triggered by other pathogenic organisms. | Non-severe COVID-19 illness category emphasized as a major focus for investigating longer-term brain-related, neurological, and cognitive effects. | Disease-severity subgroup specified in the COVID-19 photobiomodulation study. It indicates that the cited COVID-19 cohort involved mild to moderate illness rather than severe or critical disease.</data>
      <data key="d3">0.9</data>
