      <data key="d1">CONFOUNDING_FACTOR</data>
      <data key="d2">Behavioral factor cited as shortening cognitive-impairment-free life expectancy and requiring adjustment as a competing variable in post-COVID cognitive analyses. | Behavioral factor mentioned as something patients should stop to help relieve exacerbated dyspnea in long COVID management. | Covariate controlled for in the adjusted prospective association between PASS-content and stress resilience. | A health behavior variable cited in the reference on socioeconomic disadvantage, race/ethnicity, gender, and smoking. Smoking is relevant as a confounder because it can influence respiratory health, vascular function, inflammation, and cognitive performance. | Component of the aggregated vascular risk score and a possible contributor to vascular risk. | Modifiable behavioral risk factor for dementia and contributor to non-communicable disease burden. | Behavioral cardiac-risk factor reported among mechanisms linking psychological distress or affective symptoms to cardiovascular events. | Health behavior identified as detrimental to mental health and associated with greater depression risk. | Health behavior assessed at baseline. Smoking patterns were reported to be similar between infection-history groups, making it a less prominent differentiating factor in this excerpt. | Baseline lifestyle risk factor compared between SARS-CoV-2 positive and control groups because smoking may influence respiratory disease risk, severity, vascular health, and cognition. | A behavioral exposure repeatedly referenced across cited article titles, including studies of HIV, brain neurotoxicity, delay discounting, and nicotine dependence.</data>
