    <node id="SEX DISTRIBUTION">
      <data key="d0">SEX DISTRIBUTION</data>
      <data key="d1">CONFOUNDING_FACTOR</data>
      <data key="d2">Demographic factor reported for both groups, with women and men represented in similar proportions in the healthy control and persistent-symptom patient groups. | Group-level sex composition. Cognitively impaired groups had a lower proportion of females than non-impaired groups, indicating a potential demographic confounder. | Demographic factor noted because the RBD group was approximately 90% male, consistent with the known population distribution of RBD. | Demographic factor that differed slightly among the schizophrenia game-training group, schizophrenia treatment-as-usual group, and healthy controls. | A sample-level characteristic investigated as a potential moderator of emotion regulation strategy use. | The cohort included 42 male participants (43%) and 56 female participants (57%). Sex distribution is a baseline demographic factor that may be considered when interpreting cognitive outcomes. | Demographic variable evaluated as a potential confounder between SARS-CoV-2 positive participants and controls. Age was not normally distributed, so two-sample Kolmogorov-Smirnov tests were used; no significant group differences were reported at Scan 1 or Scan 2. | Demographic factor compared between groups; the proportion of female participants did not differ significantly between active and sham groups. | Potential confounding factor assessed between participants with and without MRI; distribution was similar, with p=0.261.</data>
      <data key="d3">0.85</data>
      <data key="d4">4b18c63f-2f87-46c1-a3ae-8c4337490271_chunk_20</data>
