    <node id="COVID-19 LOCKDOWN">
      <data key="d0">COVID-19 LOCKDOWN</data>
      <data key="d1">CONFOUNDING_FACTOR</data>
      <data key="d2">Lockdown conditions during the COVID-19 pandemic are referenced as the context for psychological resilience. Lockdown-related social isolation, stress, disrupted routines, and changes in work or lifestyle may confound relationships between COVID-19 and cognitive or psychological outcomes. | Pandemic-related stay-at-home or lockdown context mentioned in the title concerning mental health and creativity. Lockdown conditions may influence psychological or cognitive-related outcomes independently of SARS-CoV-2 infection itself. | Pandemic-related social restriction context referenced in the title about doctoral researchers’ mental health and PhD training satisfaction during the German COVID-19 lockdown. It is included only as a potential contextual confounder; the text does not connect it to cognitive performance or neuropsychological assessment. | Lockdown is a contextual condition during the COVID-19 pandemic that amplified associations among physical activity, sedentary screen-use, insomnia, depression, and anxiety symptoms.</data>
