    <node id="BMI">
      <data key="d0">BMI</data>
      <data key="d1">CONFOUNDING_FACTOR</data>
      <data key="d2">Body mass index, reported as 24.03 ± 2.75 kg/m² on average. BMI may reflect metabolic or general health factors relevant to cognitive outcomes. | Potential confounder for biomarker and cognitive comparisons; healthy controls were matched to infected participants by body mass index. | Retinal microcirculation-related anthropometric covariate adjusted for in the multivariate regression models. | Body mass index, included as a covariate and replaced by zBMI in robustness analyses. | Anthropometric health measure obtained from the patient's most recent medical records; reflects weight status and metabolic health burden. | Anthropometric factor for which a nonsignificant trend toward higher values was observed in veterans with Gulf War illness. | Clinical covariate included in the models to account for potential confounding by BMI. | Body mass index, tested as a potential covariate in ANOVA analyses and reported not to covary with motor-evoked potentials obtained from TMS protocols. | A health-related covariate assessed as a possible factor influencing cognitive changes over time after SARS-CoV-2 infection. | Body-mass-index covariate adjusted for in the cognitive trajectory analysis. | Body mass index compared between cases and controls using a Mann-Whitney U-test. BMI may confound cognitive or imaging associations through metabolic and inflammatory pathways. | BMI was used as a participant characteristic for examining plasma E1 and E2 levels in the figure. | Body mass index was described as associated with CSF estrogen levels in women, consistent with prior reports. | A covariate adjusted for in Alzheimer disease plasma biomarker models involving p-tau/ABETA42 ratios. | Covariate adjusted for in analyses of Alzheimer disease pathology outcomes. | Clinical variable derived from height and weight and collected during the initial assessment. | Anthropometric factor reported in the clinical assessment section as an obesity-related measure that may influence or covary with health outcomes. | A health-related variable examined in relation to multi-comorbidities in elderly people; included as a potential confounding or explanatory factor within the constraints of the valid entity types.</data>
      <data key="d3">0.9</data>
