    <node id="REASONING">
      <data key="d0">REASONING</data>
      <data key="d1">COGNITIVE_DOMAIN</data>
      <data key="d2">Cognitive domain reported as one of the most affected in participants with symptoms persisting beyond 12 weeks after SARS-CoV-2 infection. | Higher-order cognitive function associated with angular gyrus regions involved in the observed task-related connectivity pattern. | Executive-function ability stimulated in week 10 of treatment together with categorization and abstraction. | Cognitive domain assessed by the Cognitron tasks, reflecting problem-solving and inferential abilities targeted by the computerised assessment battery. | A cognitive domain included among the distinct domains to consider when tailoring a cognitive testing battery. | Cognitive process referenced in the cited article title describing a negative relationship between reasoning and religiosity. The fragment does not connect reasoning to respiratory infection, healthcare-worker exposure, disease severity, or quantified impairment. | Higher-order cognitive domain named in the cited traumatic brain injury study as showing deficits after structural brain damage. | Cognitive domain assessed through the UK Biobank fluid intelligence score.</data>
      <data key="d3">0.85</data>
      <data key="d4">86f9ab26-f1be-4c98-8916-d978196e27f5_chunk_20</data>
