    <node id="COGNITIVE PERFORMANCE">
      <data key="d0">COGNITIVE PERFORMANCE</data>
      <data key="d1">COGNITIVE_DOMAIN</data>
      <data key="d2">Overall neuropsychological performance compared between recovered post-COVID-19 patients and healthy volunteers in the meta-analysis. | Overall cognitive outcome evaluated by converting raw cognitive scores into age- and education-adjusted scalar scores. Abnormal cognitive performance was defined as scalar score below 7. | Overall neuropsychological cognitive outcome evaluated using scalar scores adjusted according to age and formal education. Cognitive performance was classified as abnormal when one or more subtests had a scalar score below 7. | General cognitive functioning assessed through cognitive screening; the abstract states that groups did not differ in cognitive performance. | General cognitive outcome assessed in recovered COVID-19 individuals; prior web-based work reported alterations, especially among hospitalized patients but also among non-hospitalized patients. | Performance data generated from the Cognitron-MS battery and used in data-driven clustering to identify symptom-based phenotypes in people with multiple sclerosis. | Performance on cognitive tasks in people with multiple sclerosis, including accuracy, cost measures, and response times. | Cognitive outcome reported in cited evidence as negatively associated with long working hours, especially among middle-aged individuals. | A cognitive functioning domain explicitly mentioned as impaired in long COVID. No specific neuropsychological test or cognitive subdomain is provided in the excerpt. | General cognitive outcome described as improved in individuals with higher extraversion and stronger EEG connectivity, and reduced among COVID-19 frontline healthcare professionals. | Broad cognitive outcome referenced in the title of a review or conceptual article on motor-cognitive training for cognitive performance enhancement. | Broad cognitive outcome evaluated in relation to previous SARS-CoV-2 infection, mild COVID-19, and serum biomarkers of neuro-axonal and astrocytic injury. | General cognitive status noted as differing between groups at baseline, which may affect interpretation of executive-function outcomes. | Overall cognitive task performance, described as being influenced through the effect of worry and anxiety on working memory. | A broad cognitive outcome described as being influenced by cognitive fatigue. | General cognitive function assessed in the T2DM group using the MoCA score. | A broad cognitive outcome compared with plasma NfL concentrations in cross-sectional studies, where reported findings were mixed across studies. | A memory outcome reflecting identification of previously encountered information; sensitivity to differences in recognition performance improved with retention intervals of at least 3 days. | Performance on cognitive or neuropsychological tasks; the text notes gamelike features may improve enjoyment without affecting performance. | Performance on cognitive tasks or remote neurocognitive assessments; the text notes that such performance varies systematically by device type and operating system and may also reflect demographic differences. | Broad cognitive outcome assessed by computerized or online cognitive assessments and referenced in relation to COVID-19-associated cognitive effects. | Overall assessment-score performance across the neuropsychological tasks described in the passage. The reported predictors are age, education level, and native-language status; no respiratory infection or healthcare-worker cohort is discussed in this excerpt. | The cognitive abilities assessed across the disease continuum from RBD prodrome to later-stage Parkinson’s disease. | Overall cognitive functioning discussed as varying by menopausal status in some cross-sectional studies but not others. The text notes that results may differ depending on whether cognition is assessed globally or with task-specific measures. | Overall cognitive ability reported as cognitive scores in the participant groups. Scores decreased from controls through to AD, indicating worse cognition with more severe clinical status. | Overall cognitive performance discussed as being negatively associated with heightened attentional maintenance to threats and partially associated with training effectiveness. | A broad cognitive outcome mentioned as being influenced by cultural and social factors including lifestyle, dietary habits, literacy levels, healthcare access, and multilingualism. | Overall cognitive functioning discussed in relation to effects of the human gut microbiota. | General cognitive functioning assessed in the clinical study and in cited preclinical work. It is represented by outcomes such as the NTB, Attention Composites, and Morris water maze performance. | Overall cognitive functioning, including attentiveness, focus, and efficient task-related performance. | Cognitive performance is described as potentially impacted by environmental microbiome exposures and as worse in association with retinal vascular changes in children. | Cognitive functioning is referenced in the study title as a domain of interest during Antarctic isolation, although specific cognitive findings are not detailed in the provided abstract. | General cognitive functioning referenced in the review stating that EEG alpha and theta oscillations reflect cognitive performance. | Cognitive functioning assessed in schizophrenia research; the text notes that cognitive differences are subtle and generally apparent only at the group level. | Cognitive functioning reported to be modulated by altered gut microbiota in veterans with cirrhosis. | Cognitive performance assessed at 9 to 10 years of age in relation to birthweight and head circumference at birth. | A broader cognitive outcome encompassing domains such as working memory and attention. The abstract reports that cognitive performance is significantly enhanced at the circadian-preferred time. | The measurable cognitive outcome compared between participants with and without PCC or persistent COVID-19 symptoms and explored in relation to cognitive reserve proxies. | General cognitive or test-performance domain targeted by the DigiCog study. Eligibility criteria require participants to complete tests independently, and neurological or visual conditions are screened because they may affect performance. | Broad cognitive outcome assessed in HCAP and analyzed in relation to pre-HCAP hospital-treated infections. It includes general cognition, domain-specific cognition, and secondary analyses of individual cognitive test performance. | Objective or assessment-based cognitive performance whose interpretation could be influenced by testing conditions such as distractions, disengagement, or sensory impairment. Research staff rated the validity of assessment results at the end of each testing session. | General cognitive outcome domain assessed using 10 pre-selected cognitive variables. The passage reports no significant baseline cognitive differences between SARS-CoV-2 and control groups, but notes a longitudinal cognitive effect detected with the UK Biobank Trail Making Test. | A cognitive outcome domain referenced in the eligibility criteria, where medications that could enhance performance were excluded to reduce bias. | General cognitive outcomes assessed through structured neuropsychological testing. | Cognitive outcomes assessed repeatedly during the study and potentially susceptible to practice or attrition effects. | Longitudinal cognitive data collected from cognitively normal older adults in the PREVENT-AD study, including six additional years of follow-up in the new release. | A broad cognitive outcome explicitly mentioned in the title of Alhola &amp; Polo-Kantola (2007), where sleep deprivation is described as affecting cognitive performance. | General cognitive functioning reported to be directly influenced by light exposure. | Cognitive functioning assessed in the context of everyday task performance among institutionalised older adults.</data>
      <data key="d3">0.95</data>
