      <data key="d0">6 MONTHS FOLLOW-UP</data>
      <data key="d1">FOLLOW_UP_TIMEPOINT</data>
      <data key="d2">Follow-up interval in the cited Guillén et al. study at which SF-36 scores were compared with baseline values. | Specific follow-up interval from the cited Lancet cohort study assessing consequences of COVID-19 6 months after hospital discharge. | Later follow-up visit at which MRI was repeated and longitudinal stability of cognitive, psychological, neuroimaging, and biomarker measures was evaluated. | Follow-up interval from a cited MRI-based study reporting cerebral microstructural changes in COVID-19 patients. | Longitudinal assessment timepoint occurring one month after baseline to evaluate participants' clinical and cognitive status over time. | Longitudinal assessment timepoint occurring three months after baseline to monitor evolution of cognitive and clinical findings. | Longitudinal assessment timepoint occurring six months after baseline to evaluate longer-term evolution of cognitive and clinical findings. | Longitudinal reassessment timepoint. Biomarkers, including cytokines, NfL, and GFAP, were analyzed over time; IL-17a significantly decreased by this timepoint. | Post-infection assessment timepoint at which altered intrinsic brain activity and functional connectivity were reported in hospitalized COVID-19 patients. | Follow-up interval from the cited cohort study of 6-month consequences in patients discharged from hospital after COVID-19. | Time interval at which neurological and psychiatric outcomes were evaluated in COVID-19 survivors in the cited Lancet Psychiatry retrospective cohort study. | Follow-up interval referenced in a cited cohort study assessing consequences of COVID-19 in patients discharged from hospital. | Longer-term follow-up period in a study of 1733 patients who recovered from SARS-CoV-2 infection. The excerpt introduces this follow-up period but is truncated before detailing outcomes. | Post-hospitalisation follow-up interval used in a prospective study of respiratory outcomes after COVID-19-related hospitalisation. The excerpt does not associate this timepoint with cognitive outcomes. | Post-acute assessment timepoint used in a cited study evaluating anxiety, depression, and cognitive impairment in COVID-19 survivors. | The longitudinal follow-up interval over which language coherence was observed to decline significantly faster in the MCI/AD group than in cognitively unimpaired individuals. | Post-hospitalisation follow-up timepoint for respiratory outcomes in patients after COVID-19-related hospitalisation. | Six-month post-infection follow-up assessment in a subgroup with paired 6- and 12-month questionnaire data. | Twelve-month post-infection follow-up assessment used to evaluate persistence or change in recovery status after symptomatic infection. | Eighteen-month post-infection follow-up assessment in a smaller subgroup with paired 12- and 18-month questionnaire data. | Median follow-up duration from routine data available until January 2022, with an interquartile range of 6 to 8 months. | Follow-up interval in the Huang et al. cohort study assessing consequences of COVID-19 six months after hospital discharge. | Follow-up interval after treatment cessation during which the previously observed CBTp effects were not maintained. | The follow-up timepoint at which the pooled treatment effect size was reported to remain stable. | A follow-up timepoint at which questionnaires were re-administered and mobile sensor data collection occurred over the same general period. | Follow-up timepoint indicated in the extracted table text as approximately 6 months, with n=17 appearing in the surrounding corrupted text. | Follow-up interval at which previously hospitalised COVID-19 patients were reassessed. Brain hypometabolism was less pronounced at this timepoint but remained present. | Follow-up interval stated in the linked title 'A 6-Month Longitudinal Study'. It indicates the timing of longitudinal assessment for COVID-19 ARDS survivors, although no specific cognitive measure or result is described in the provided text.</data>
