    <node id="NEUROINFLAMMATION">
      <data key="d0">NEUROINFLAMMATION</data>
      <data key="d1">BIOLOGICAL_MECHANISM</data>
      <data key="d2">Proposed biological pathway that may be influenced by hormonal and immune differences and may contribute to post-COVID susceptibility, symptoms, and outcomes. | Candidate biological mechanism investigated in relation to COVID-19 brain effects. The passage emphasizes that research has failed to find a specific marker of neuroinflammation. | Underlying biological process contributing to individual variability and heterogeneity in multiple sclerosis. | Central inflammatory process discussed as a mechanism that can severely affect cognition and behavior in animal models. | Biological mechanism named in the review title 'Neuroinflammation and depression.' It is relevant as a possible inflammatory pathway contributing to neuropsychiatric symptoms, though the excerpt does not directly connect it to post-COVID cognitive impairment. | Inflammatory biological process in the brain reported as related to olfactory impairment in Alzheimer’s disease. | Pathophysiological inflammatory process observed in SARS-CoV-2-infected nonhuman primates and presented as a mechanism that may help explain neuropsychiatric manifestations of long COVID. | Mechanism mentioned in the cited reference title on COVID-19-associated cough and post-COVID syndrome. It is biologically relevant to possible neurocognitive sequelae, but no cognitive assessment is reported in the excerpt. | Inflammation within the nervous system described as a consequence of SARS-CoV-2 neurotropism and nervous-system invasion. | A proposed central nervous system inflammatory mechanism for long COVID and neurological manifestations after COVID-19. | Central nervous system inflammatory process referenced in the title 'Signal transduction and epigenetic mechanisms in the control of microglia activation during neuroinflammation.' | Inflammatory activity within the nervous system, described as being linked to neurodegeneration and central to the researcher’s work. | An alternative or additional biological mechanism that may contribute to neflamapimod clinical activity beyond effects on cholinergic degeneration or endolysosomal dysfunction. | An inflammation-related nervous-system mechanism referenced in connection with neurodegeneration development. | Central nervous system inflammatory process. The excerpt notes that a human in-vivo imaging study supports increased neuroinflammation in veterans with Gulf War illness. | Inflammation within the nervous system, identified in the article title as the process reduced by antibiotic-induced gut microbiome remodeling after traumatic brain injury. | Inflammatory nervous-system response identified as a key component of the ischemic cascade. | Biological mechanism implicated in perioperative neurocognitive disorders. | An exploratory blood-based biomarker domain assessed as a possible mechanistic pathway contributing to cognitive decline. | Inflammatory biological processes in the nervous system reported as associated with Alzheimer's disease biomarkers and differing by race. | Inflammatory biological activity in the brain; preclinical Alzheimer disease models indicate that FSH can trigger neuroinflammation. | Inflammatory processes in the nervous system referenced in relation to longer sleep duration among at-risk elderly individuals with a parental history of Alzheimer's disease. | Appears in the bibliographic context as the journal/title context for a cited article concerning immune mechanisms in major depressive disorder. Neuroinflammation is biologically relevant to immune-mediated neuropsychiatric or cognitive effects, but this excerpt does not provide a direct empirical finding linking neuroinflammation to respiratory infection-related cognitive outcomes. | Proposed biological mechanism contributing to COVID-19-related cognitive dysfunction through inflammatory effects involving the nervous system. | Inflammatory process involving the nervous system, listed as a keyword. It is relevant as a plausible downstream mechanism in post-acute COVID-19 pathogenesis and may be linked to gut-brain-axis signaling and endotoxicity.</data>
      <data key="d3">0.97</data>
      <data key="d4">5f73e261-6d67-4a51-a17e-d25381a17114_chunk_40</data>
