    <node id="RESPONSE INHIBITION">
      <data key="d0">RESPONSE INHIBITION</data>
      <data key="d1">COGNITIVE_DOMAIN</data>
      <data key="d2">Executive-control subdomain assessed by the IFS-Ch, reflecting the ability to suppress automatic or inappropriate responses. | A cognitive control domain explicitly mentioned in the cited article title, referring to the ability to suppress inappropriate or prepotent responses. In this bibliographic excerpt, it is studied in adolescents and young adults in relation to early sexual trauma exposure. | Executive-control domain reflecting the ability to suppress or stop a prepotent response, assessed using stop signal reaction time in the stop signal test. | A cognitive-control function assessed in the wildfire-disaster study as part of a broader battery of core cognitive abilities. | The ability to suppress inappropriate responses. In the AX-CPT, participants had to ignore non-target sequences, including high-conflict sequences such as BX and AY, despite the strong response bias created by frequent AX trials. | A cognitive control domain involving suppression of an automatic or prepotent response. In the Go/No-Go task, this is required during incongruent trials when participants must look opposite to the directional arrow.</data>
      <data key="d3">0.88</data>
      <data key="d4">037d79c5-a241-4f38-8c08-cac6a4c5c0ff_chunk_31,037d79c5-a241-4f38-8c08-cac6a4c5c0ff_chunk_32</data>
