      <data key="d2">Key outcome concept targeted by the systematic review, referring to cognitive or related psychological symptoms reported in patients with persistent COVID symptoms. | Neuropsychological manifestations of long COVID that may persist longer than other symptoms. | Neurologic manifestations described as being triggered in patients and linked to persistent SARS-CoV-2 activity in the context of long COVID. | Broad neurological manifestation category reported as more frequent in severe SARS-CoV-2 infection than in non-severe infection. Specific examples in the text include acute cerebrovascular disease, impaired consciousness, and skeletal muscle injury. | Outcome domain referenced in the title 'Neuropsychological symptom identification and classification in the hospitalized COVID-19 patients during the first wave of the pandemic.' Specific cognitive domains or individual symptoms are not listed in the excerpt. | Cognitive and neuropsychological outcomes referenced in relation to long-term outcomes in children and adolescents after cardiac arrest. | Neuropsychological component of the persistent symptom pattern described after healed COVID-19 illness. | Cognitive and behavioral outcome category explicitly referenced in the study title 'Long-term clinical and neuropsychological outcomes of West Nile virus infection.' | Broad neurological manifestation reported in COVID-19, particularly frequent among severe cases. Although not limited to cognition, it is the available outcome construct in the text for nervous-system involvement.</data>
