    <node id="SARS-COV-2">
      <data key="d0">SARS-COV-2</data>
      <data key="d1">RESPIRATORY_INFECTION</data>
      <data key="d2">Etiologic coronavirus term included in the search strategy as an alternative to COVID-19 for retrieving studies on post-infection neuropsychological or cognitive outcomes. | The respiratory infectious agent discussed as present in nasal brushings and olfactory mucosa biopsies of COVID-19 patients and as potentially involved in olfactory epithelial and neuronal effects. | Respiratory viral exposure referenced in the cited 'SARS-CoV-2 human challenge study'; the citation links controlled SARS-CoV-2 exposure with changes in memory and cognition during the challenge period. | Coronavirus respiratory infection associated in reports with neuropsychiatric and neurological sequelae, including narcolepsy, convulsions, encephalitis, encephalopathy, Guillain-Barre syndrome, and neuromuscular or demyelinating processes. | The current coronavirus discussed as related to earlier respiratory viruses that had been associated with neuropsychiatric sequelae, motivating concern for long-lasting symptoms. | Respiratory viral infection/pathogen referenced in studies of vaccine booster effectiveness and neutralizing-antibody responses. | SARS-CoV-2 is mentioned in the cited article title 'Humoral Immune Response to SARS-CoV-2 in Iceland.' The text identifies it as the infectious agent studied, but does not provide cognitive outcomes, severity strata, healthcare-worker cohorts, follow-up timepoints, or neuropsychological measures. | Virus responsible for COVID-19; described as probably invading the human brain, being detected in brains of infected nonhuman primates, persisting in human tissues including brain, and containing proteomic subsequences with potential neuronal toxicity. | Viral agent causing COVID-19, cited as associated with changes in brain structure in a UK Biobank study. It is represented under the respiratory infection category because the valid type list does not include a separate virus type. | Single-stranded positive-sense RNA respiratory virus described as encoding structural proteins including spike, nucleocapsid, matrix, and envelope proteins. The passage focuses on its host-cell entry through ACE2-expressing target cells and subsequent inflammatory activation. | Causative viral infection exposure for COVID-19; the text suggests it may have clinical cognitive impact even in individuals with mild disease. | Respiratory viral infection context represented indirectly through measurement of SARS-CoV-2-RBD-IgG as a viral-response biomarker. The excerpt does not report confirmed acute infection, infection severity, or respiratory-infection-related cognitive outcomes. | Respiratory viral pathogen referenced in a modelling study on long-term vaccination strategies to mitigate transmission impact. The excerpt provides no cognitive outcomes, neuropsychological measures, severity strata, or follow-up timepoints. | Causative coronavirus referenced in the cited article title about infected patients in Wuhan, China; underlying pathogen for COVID-19. | SARS-CoV-2 is mentioned only as a Nature subject tag. It is the coronavirus associated with COVID-19 and respiratory infection, but the text provides no study-specific details about cohorts, severity, cognition, tests, effect sizes, follow-up, confounders, mechanisms, or occupational exposure. | Severe Acute Respiratory Syndrome Coronavirus-2, identified in Wuhan, China in 2019 and described as the pathogen responsible for the COVID-19 pandemic. | The coronavirus responsible for the current pandemic and COVID-19. It is discussed in relation to SARS because viral similarity may make post-SARS sequelae informative for anticipating post-acute COVID-19 effects. | Mentioned only in a cited German public health source describing behavioral rules and recommendations to protect against the coronavirus SARS-CoV-2. The provided text is a reference list excerpt and does not report cognitive outcomes, healthcare-worker cohorts, disease severity, neuropsychological tests, effect sizes, biological mechanisms, occupational exposure, or follow-up findings. | SARS-CoV-2 is mentioned as one of the infections studied in large multicentre studies on naturally acquired and experimental immunity. No cognitive domains, occupational cohorts, severity comparisons, biological mechanisms, or measured effects are described. | Virus responsible for COVID-19, mentioned as the source of emerging variants affecting Taiwan, Thailand, and Vietnam. Because the valid schema lacks a pathogen-specific type, it is retained under RESPIRATORY_INFECTION as the closest available category. No cognitive or occupational-exposure findings are provided. | Respiratory viral agent referenced across multiple article titles, including studies of RNA expression, deletion mutations, small viral RNAs, and neuropathology in an hACE2 mouse model. | Severe acute respiratory syndrome coronavirus 2, the causative virus of the COVID-19 pandemic. The text discusses possible nervous-system effects of infection, including neurotropism, neuroinflammation, and possible CNS presence. | Viral respiratory pathogen listed as a MeSH term in the PubMed record and relevant to COVID-19-related virological associations with cognitive dysfunction.</data>
      <data key="d3">0.98</data>
      <data key="d4">4b18c63f-2f87-46c1-a3ae-8c4337490271_chunk_12</data>
