      <data key="d2">The Mini-Mental State Examination, described as one of the most widely used cognitive assessment tools in Parkinson disease, but noted as limited in domain-level detail and poorly sensitive to subtle cognitive changes. | Mini-Mental State Examination, a cognitive screening tool originally designed for Western populations. The text identifies it as commonly relied upon in dementia research and diagnosis but potentially limited in culturally and linguistically diverse MENA settings. | Mini-Mental State Examination used as an inclusion criterion; participants were required to have an MMSE score greater than 24. | A standardized cognitive screening instrument included in the face-to-face neuropsychological assessment battery, providing a conventional measure of global cognitive status. | Mini-Mental State Examination, a neuropsychological test of global cognition. Poorer performance was reported among participants with prior hospital-treated infection. | Mini-Mental State Examination used to assess global cognition, with total score serving as a primary outcome measure.</data>
