    <node id="TRAUMATIC BRAIN INJURY">
      <data key="d0">TRAUMATIC BRAIN INJURY</data>
      <data key="d1">CONFOUNDING_FACTOR</data>
      <data key="d2">Neurological condition referenced in a cited study of cognitive assessment. Because traumatic brain injury can independently influence cognition, it is appropriately represented as a potential confounding factor in analyses of infection-related cognitive outcomes. | A neurological injury referenced as the clinical context for Rey Complex Figure Test performance. Because traumatic brain injury can independently impair cognition and neuropsychological test performance, it is a relevant confounding factor in cognitive outcome analyses. | Specific neurological exclusion criterion because traumatic brain injury can independently cause cognitive, neurological, or functional impairments after ICU admission. | Neurological injury cited as affecting olfaction. Traumatic brain injury can independently alter olfactory function and may confound interpretation of olfactory or cognitive deficits. | Neurological injury condition referenced in a separate cited article on post-acute blood biomarkers and disease progression. In studies of cognition after respiratory infection, traumatic brain injury could independently influence neurological biomarkers or cognitive outcomes and therefore may be relevant as a potential confounding factor, although no direct COVID-19 cognition association is reported in the provided text. | A non-infectious neurological injury discussed in the cited references, including effects on body processing and behavioral outcomes. It could independently influence cognition and behavior outside any respiratory-infection context. | A neurological injury condition repeatedly referenced as the clinical context in which cognitive fatigue, subjective fatigue, and altered neural connectivity or activation are studied. | Neurological injury condition referenced in the article title 'Distinct patterns of structural damage underlie working memory and reasoning deficits after traumatic brain injury.' In respiratory-infection cognition studies, traumatic brain injury could be an alternative cause of cognitive impairment and therefore a potential confounder. | Neurological condition cited as being associated with structural damage and deficits in working memory and reasoning; it could independently contribute to cognitive impairment in studies of infection-related cognition. | Given as an example of a condition leading to brain function impediments and used as an exclusion criterion. Prior traumatic brain injury could independently alter brain function, neurophysiology, or cognitive performance. | A clinical condition named in the passage as one group in which neurofeedback was associated with improved memory performance. | The neurological injury context of the article. Because the valid schema is oriented toward respiratory infection and cognition, traumatic brain injury is represented as a non-respiratory clinical factor that may independently influence neuroinflammation and neuropsychiatric outcomes. | Given as an example of a condition that can impede brain function and was therefore exclusionary. Prior traumatic brain injury could independently alter brain function or neurophysiological measures. | Traumatic brain injury is cited in multiple references covering management, neuropathology, and treatment strategies. In studies assessing cognitive outcomes, a history of traumatic brain injury can independently contribute to cognitive impairment and therefore may confound attribution of deficits to respiratory infection or other exposures. | Neurological exclusion criterion because prior traumatic brain injury can independently affect cognitive or test performance, especially if occurring within the prior seven years. | Excluded neurological condition that could independently affect cognition, brain structure, or CSF-related profiles. | Referenced in the title of an animal study on hyperbaric oxygen therapy and rat microglial polarization after traumatic brain injury. Within respiratory infection and cognition research, traumatic brain injury could be a potential non-infectious contributor to cognitive or neurological impairment, but the excerpt does not directly link it to long COVID or cognitive outcomes. | Brain injuries reported among incarcerated women in New Zealand and associated with poor sleep quality. | Traumatic brain injury is reported as highly prevalent among female offenders and may be linked to increased mental health problems. It is relevant as a potential confounder because prior brain injury can independently influence cognitive functioning.</data>
      <data key="d3">0.82</data>
      <data key="d4">e83b96c5-4b27-4c65-81de-ecaece6f2395_chunk_62</data>
      <data key="d5">e83b96c5-4b27-4c65-81de-ecaece6f2395,b0310b04-4fe9-4dc6-8bb6-5040028e4f04,63b31c15-b9bb-4a8e-a6dd-082b7c1908fa,378fc975-98b1-4272-98a2-05ff991584ad,af5ff8eb-8135-49d4-81fd-6de4008d60d8,e1f5a260-ede2-4c0c-adc9-c544d472940a,050a4d09-f879-4946-af0d-10d9e1e68967,a459cfaf-0077-4b3b-bd9e-1701c220e2a5,46ccdeeb-683a-4650-9e07-68526b50880c,3b3e91e3-1658-4d23-b1ac-d599d49e418a,c1bfbf3a-d728-4b3c-b369-eb64b5b9e128,104e587b-e1a7-495c-92cb-cf76b7504b4c,65d2e8e3-6795-45dd-a0ac-fc33bf2597df,c7521404-6f18-4f62-856e-417421405a30,fe2c70b3-e143-43f7-bbf4-4cdc081212c3,3e3acef8-1a7d-425a-b577-e9cb913726f4,c04453e8-1d88-445b-9399-ac03633dec1f,531dd921-3435-419e-b587-7dfeebb99084</data>
