      <data key="d2">A symptom-based phenotype described as a common subtype identified through clustering of Cognitron-MS cognitive performance data and patient-reported motor disability data. | The cognitive impairment phenotype observed in the Severe Motor + Severe Cognitive cluster, potentially showing sex-related susceptibility in males. | Patient-reported cognitive difficulties, described as debilitating in ME/CFS-like syndromes. These may be captured by objective measures, although discrepancies between symptoms and test results are common. | Patient-reported cognitive difficulty described as occurring following SARS-CoV-2 infection and studied in relation to Co-ultraPEALut. | Clinically significant cognitive dysfunction mentioned as an example manifestation of neurological long COVID. It is not tied to a specific neuropsychological test or quantified effect size in the passage. | Self-reported cognitive difficulties that increased as the pandemic continued, especially among older adults who became or remained socially isolated. | A self-reported cognitive impairment state referenced in early postmenopausal women in association with altered brain connectivity. | Baseline cognitive status assessed using the objective cognitive test battery. The text states it was used to assess baseline cognitive impairment but was not included in the determination of objective cognitive decline at follow-up. | More severe cognitive impairment outcome examined for prevalence among World Trade Center exposed FDNY and general emergency responders.</data>
