      <data key="d1">CONFOUNDING_FACTOR</data>
      <data key="d2">Potential methodological bias acknowledged by the authors because included individuals were invited for a neuropsychiatric study. The passage notes that this could lead to overestimation of the observed findings. | Potential bias caused by incomplete questionnaire completion. If patients who completed questionnaires differed from those who did not, estimates of post-ICU cognitive symptoms may be biased. | Potential confounding factor affecting interpretation of associations between COVID-19 course, symptom burden, and post-COVID outcomes. The text notes selection bias risk in Townsend et al.'s trial, where only 153 of 487 patients accepted examination, and also acknowledges possible selection bias in the authors' long COVID unit setting. | Bias introduced when hospital-based samples are used instead of population-based recruitment, potentially leading to overestimated dementia prevalence. | Potential bias arising from the study's convenience and snowball sampling strategy and university-based participant recruitment.</data>
