    <node id="DIABETES">
      <data key="d0">DIABETES</data>
      <data key="d1">CONFOUNDING_FACTOR</data>
      <data key="d2">Metabolic comorbidity showing an insignificant risk association with higher cognitive failure; relevant as a possible independent contributor to cognitive symptoms. | Comorbid condition defining the cohort in the cited EHR-based study of metformin use and long COVID incidence. It may influence risk estimates for long COVID, but the excerpt does not provide adjusted effect sizes or details of confounder handling. | Pre-existing metabolic comorbidity defined using ICD10 codes or self-report. It may affect risk of severe post-infection outcomes and delayed recovery. | Component of the aggregated vascular risk score and a possible independent contributor to vascular and cognitive impairment. | Metabolic disorder postulated to be triggered or unmasked after COVID-19, including new-onset diabetes associated with SARS-CoV-2 infection of the pancreas. | Non-communicable disease and modifiable risk factor associated with dementia risk. | A metabolic and cardiac risk factor collected during assessment. Diabetes may influence biomedical status and rehabilitation outcomes. | Metabolic comorbidity adjusted for in the post-COVID cognitive trajectory model. | Health condition included as an adjusted covariate. Diabetes history was identified from self-reports in HCAP or prior ELSA waves and linked hospital records. | Baseline metabolic and vascular risk factor reported in 17/81 (21.0%) of the no-decline group and 3/17 (17.6%) of the decline group; p=0.756. Diabetes can influence cognition through microvascular and glycemic mechanisms. | Comorbidity more common among hospitalized SARS-CoV-2-positive participants than among non-hospitalized cases. Diabetes may contribute to hospitalization risk and confound severity-based comparisons.</data>
