      <data key="d0">RUMINATION</data>
      <data key="d1">COGNITIVE_DOMAIN</data>
      <data key="d2">Repetitive negative self-focused thought process described as a core symptom-related cognitive domain in major depressive disorder. | A repetitive cognitive process examined in relation to dynamic functional brain-network architecture. | Baseline cognitive-emotional processing factor associated with intrusive memories at three months. | Tendency to ruminate; identified as one of the strongest risk factors associated with experiencing intrusions at T1. | A cognitive-affective process referenced through a dorsomedial prefrontal cortex-based dynamic functional connectivity model. | A repetitive negative thinking process referenced in the context of posttraumatic stress disorder. | Emotion regulation strategy analyzed in relation to anger; the meta-analysis found a consistent positive association with anger. | A repetitive thought process described as co-occurring with poor sleep, short sleep, and aggression. | A repetitive negative thinking process referenced as a psychological or cognitive variable in the Borderline Personality Disorder study.</data>
