Brain Digital Neuropsychology Toolkit. | Cognitive-control domain assessed by the Cognitron platform and included among the intended cognitive demands evaluated against IDoCT-derived difficulty scales. | A cognitive domain involving executive performance; reported as showing underperformance and contributing to global accuracy deficits detected by the online battery. | A higher-order cognitive domain involving planning, inhibition, problem solving, and cognitive flexibility, listed as impaired in schizophrenia. | A broad cognitive domain identified as a target for anti-suicidal therapies in subthreshold depression and major depressive disorder. | A cognitive domain commonly assessed by tools such as the Trail Making Test and Wisconsin Card Sorting Test. The text discusses these tests as widely used neuropsychological measures in the MENA region. | A cognitive domain assessed in the study using tests from the CANTAB battery. The text specifically states that executive function was assessed with the One Touch Stockings of Cambridge and spatial working memory tests. | Higher-order cognitive control processes referenced through the cited work on executive dysfunctions. | A cognitive domain impacted in dementia with Lewy bodies and assessed in the primary neuropsychological test battery using Category Fluency, Letter Fluency, and One Back accuracy. | A higher-order cognitive domain found to be positively associated with neighbourhood greenness in a year-long study of approximately 2600 children in Barcelona, independent of socioeconomic status. | A cognitive domain involving planning, set-shifting, inhibition, and flexible problem solving. It is relevant to the card-sorting task referenced in the citation on frontal-lobe lesions. | A cognitive domain explicitly mentioned in a cited reference on the role of the orbital prefrontal cortex. The excerpt identifies it as a brain-function outcome but does not connect it to respiratory infection, healthcare-worker exposure, disease severity, or follow-up time. | Cognitive impairment domain mentioned in the referenced article on depression-executive dysfunction syndrome of late life. | Cognitive domain reported as impaired in association with longitudinal hippocampal and prefrontal volume loss in familial high-risk individuals. | A broad cognitive domain involving inhibitory control, rule-guided behavior, and attentional control. It is relevant to the Stroop task and AX-CPT included in the computerized task battery. | Higher-order cognitive domain involving planning, cognitive control, task switching, inhibition, and goal-directed behavior; explicitly linked to deficits in long COVID syndrome. | Domain-specific cognitive score assessed at HCAP. Executive function showed the clearest association with prior severe infections and was prospectively associated with lower risk of viral infections, especially COVID-19. | Cognitive domain involving higher-order control processes, assessed through backward number span and oral trail-making tasks. | Cognitive domain reported to worsen in SARS-CoV-2 positive participants, reflected by longer completion times on Trail Making Test components, particularly Trail B. | A cognitive domain reported as improved in preliminary human tPBM studies involving individuals with MCI or AD. | Cognitive domain assessed in both WRAP and BrANCH using composite/domain-specific scores calculated by averaging sample-based z-scores from cognitive or neuropsychological tests. | Higher-order cognitive domain assessed by the Trail Making Test and Color-Word Interference Test, including flexibility and inhibitory-control components. | Cognitive domain explicitly mentioned in the cited article title 'Effects of Physical Exercise on Executive Function in Adults with Depression.' It refers to higher-order cognitive abilities such as planning, inhibition, working control, and cognitive flexibility. | Specific manifestation of post-COVID-19 cognitive dysfunction involving impairment in higher-order cognitive control functions such as planning, organization, inhibition, and complex task management. | Primary cognitive outcome highlighted in the interpretation; patients after COVID-19 are reported to have greater odds of impairment in executive function. | Higher-order cognitive domain assessed after COVID-19 and identified as an outcome predicted by cognitive reserve, depressive symptoms, obesity, and change in employment status. | A cognitive domain objectively reported as impaired in post-acute COVID-19 syndrome, involving higher-order control, planning, and behavioral regulation.</data>
