      <data key="d2">Body-wide inflammatory response explicitly listed as a potential mechanism contributing to the cognitive effects of SARS-CoV-2 infection. | Body-wide inflammatory state positively correlated with SARS-CoV-2-specific T-cell responses in pulmonary post-acute sequelae. | Inflammatory systemic response listed as a contributor to abnormal liver function in acute COVID-19. | Inflammatory process reduced in preclinical studies combining heat-killed probiotics and n-3 polyunsaturated fatty acids; also relevant to chronic inflammatory states such as long COVID. | Proposed biological mechanism through which pulmonary diseases and respiratory illness may contribute to cognitive decline. | Body-wide inflammatory activity described as being increased by sleep disruption. | Inflammatory biological mechanism elicited by COVID-19 and proposed in the passage as a pathway that can negatively affect the central nervous system and contribute to depressive symptoms. | Aging-related biological process linked in the text to gut microbiome changes and relevant to the study’s measured health-related outcomes. | Inflammatory activity considered as a possible independent or downstream contributor to Gulf War illness symptoms. | The proposed mechanism underlying links between infections and cognitive impairment, suggested to be more important than pathogen-specific effects because similar associations were observed across multiple infection types. | Inflammatory biological state reflected in part by plasma IL-6, which was lowered after active tPBM and is implicated in Alzheimer’s disease progression.</data>
