      <data key="d0">SOCIAL SUPPORT</data>
      <data key="d1">CONFOUNDING_FACTOR</data>
      <data key="d2">Psychosocial factor that strengthened the buffering association between functional network connectivity and lower negative emotion. | Baseline social support level, assessed using the Social Support Rating Scale. Social support may influence cognition, intervention engagement, and psychosocial functioning. | Protective psychosocial factor described as buffering increases in depressive and anxiety symptoms during the pandemic. Its relationship to cognitive performance is indirect, operating through mitigation of mental health symptoms. | Protective factor cited as having pro-resilience effects that are mediated by positive appraisal style. | Social support is cited in relation to psychological symptomatology following a natural disaster and may function as a contextual factor influencing psychological outcomes. | Identified in the article title as a mediator of the association between COVID-19 related stress exposure and psychological distress. | Social support was reported as a protective psychosocial factor positively related to mental health outcomes during the lockdown period. | A contextual psychosocial factor suggested as a potential moderator or covariate in future studies of psychological pathways and health behaviors. | A psychosocial factor reviewed in relation to depression among community-dwelling older adults in Asia. | Availability or quality of supportive social relationships; its association with financial exploitation risk has been inconsistent across studies. | Additional contextual factor recommended for future examination to improve understanding of student mental health and social behaviour outcomes. | Psychosocial factor discussed alongside activity restriction and perceived stress in relation to depressive symptoms. | Social support was collected as a potential protective or influencing factor for anxiety and depression. | Protective variable noted as extensively studied and showing a protective-reactive pattern in relation to mood disorders.</data>
      <data key="d3">0.88</data>
