      <data key="d0">VISUAL MEMORY</data>
      <data key="d1">COGNITIVE_DOMAIN</data>
      <data key="d2">Visual memory is a cognitive domain relevant to tests involving recognition or recurrence of visual figures, such as Kimura’s recurring-figures test. The cited reference is a normative study rather than an infection-related outcome study. | Memory domain involving retention and recall of visual information; less frequently abnormal but reported to correlate with gray matter volume measures. | A cognitive domain involving encoding, retaining, and recalling visual information. It is commonly assessed through delayed or immediate recall components of the Rey-Osterrieth Complex Figure Test. | Cognitive visual process defined as the ability to recognize and recall visually presented information. It is included among the visual analysis processes assessed by the TVPS-3. | A specific memory-related cognitive domain emphasized as a key focus of the Paired Associates Learning test. The passage notes that visual paired associate learning performance has been identified as an early marker of Alzheimer’s disease-related phenomena. | A memory-related cognitive domain specifically mentioned in the cited neuropsychological study of Gulf War military pesticide applicators. | A memory-related cognitive domain assessed with immediate and delayed components, both of which showed significant improvement after intervention.</data>
