    <node id="ATTENTION">
      <data key="d0">ATTENTION</data>
      <data key="d1">COGNITIVE_DOMAIN</data>
      <data key="d2">A cognitive domain assessed in the study. Attention performance was significantly enhanced at the circadian-preferred time compared with the non-preferred time of day. | A specific cognitive domain assessed with validated in-person measures. Attention scores were analyzed longitudinally and reported to improve progressively through 42 months post-COVID. | Specific cognitive function reported as impaired in individuals with Post-COVID-19 Condition. | A cognitive domain relevant to timed sequencing tasks such as Trail Making Test A. | Cognitive domain involving focused mental engagement and immediate attention, assessed through forward number span and oral trail-making tasks. | Cognitive domain for which Trail Making Test completion measures are described as sensitive indicators of impairment. | A cognitive function supported by working memory. The text identifies attention as one of the related cognitive functions that depends on working-memory processes. | A cognitive function reported as improved in preliminary human tPBM studies involving individuals with MCI or AD. | A cognitive domain explicitly assessed by the Montreal Cognitive Assessment. | RBANS cognitive domain assessing attentional capacity, working memory span, and coding efficiency. | A cognitive domain assessed in the randomized controlled trial of yoga in correctional settings. The text lists a computerized attention/impulsivity test and identifies attention as an outcome. | A cognitive domain mentioned as being associated with lighting and mood in studies by Knez. No neuropsychological test, effect size, respiratory infection context, or healthcare-worker cohort is specified. | Specific cognitive domain mentioned as dysfunctional in COVID-19-related cognitive impairment and as a possible target for carefully monitored cognitive training. | Attention was one of the assessed cognitive domains. The abstract reported no significant association between COVID-19 history or acute-care site and impairment in domains other than executive functioning. | A cognitive domain objectively reported as impaired in post-acute COVID-19 syndrome, involving selective and sustained cognitive focus. | A cognitive domain assessed as part of the neurocognitive task battery, particularly using the Continuous Performance Task.</data>
