sociodemographic variables are involved. | Questionnaire-derived maternal or household socio-demographic information collected during the four-to-six-year follow-up and potentially relevant for adjustment in analyses. | Participant characteristics such as demographic and social variables that were examined for association with components of insight. | External contextual factors, including socioeconomic status and age, examined as moderators of the association between psychosocial functioning and financial exploitation risk. | Demographic characteristics summarized for the 260 study participants, including field of study, residence, income, parents' education, and parents' occupation. | Population-level social and demographic variables considered as factors that may shape neurodevelopmental trajectories. | Population-level demographic and social variables referenced in relation to SARS-CoV-2 infection over time in Ontario, Canada. The excerpt does not specify particular factors or link them to cognitive outcomes. | Group of participant background variables collected in the study, including age, sex, education, occupation-related variables, country of birth, marital status, family structure, and type of residence. These variables provide contextual information that may need adjustment in analyses of cognitive outcomes. | Population characteristics considered in relation to infection-related mortality risk. | Population and demographic characteristics considered in relation to circulating sex hormone levels.</data>
