    <node id="MONTREAL COGNITIVE ASSESSMENT (MOCA)">
      <data key="d0">MONTREAL COGNITIVE ASSESSMENT (MOCA)</data>
      <data key="d1">NEUROPSYCH_TEST</data>
      <data key="d2">Psychometric screening instrument used in the study to measure global cognitive function. | Cognitive screening scale used in some studies to assess cognitive impairment; described as validated across populations and ages, but mainly used in people older than 50 years and in possible dementia contexts. | A cognitive screening instrument included in the NACC Uniform Data Set neuropsychological battery and used to assess global cognitive functioning in study participants. | A cognitive screening test used as an outcome measure; the intervention improved MoCA scores by 3.29 points compared with the health education group. | MoCA scores used as a neuropsychological measure of cognitive function. The intervention group demonstrated a statistically significant greater increase in MoCA scores compared with the health education group. | A cognitive screening test used in the study. MoCA scores were reported in type 2 diabetic patients and correlated negatively with HbA1c and fasting blood glucose, and positively with right supramarginal gyrus activity measures. | MoCA score used as the neuropsychological measure of cognitive function; it was negatively correlated with HbA1c and fasting blood glucose and positively correlated with right supramarginal gyrus neuroimaging measures. | A cognitive screening instrument referenced in relation to telehealth reliability and as a comparator in Parkinson's disease cognitive screening research. | Cognitive screening instrument used in dementia assessment. The text notes that, like the MMSE, MoCA may perform imperfectly in MENA populations if cultural, literacy, language, bilingualism, and test-familiarity factors are not accounted for. | The Montreal Cognitive Assessment is cited as a cognitive screening test. In this text, a MoCA score below 3 is used as an exclusion or eligibility criterion because such impairment could hamper participation in the Holoreach exercise. | Traditional cognitive screening instrument used for perioperative cognitive evaluation; the text describes it as limited in scope relative to comprehensive neurocognitive testing. | A cognitive screening instrument included among the variables analyzed in relation to CSF proteomic features. The excerpt does not report a specific MoCA-associated effect size, follow-up timepoint, respiratory infection status, or disease-severity relationship. | A validated cognitive screening test mentioned in the excerpt. It is used to assess global cognition, although no score, outcome, or study finding is provided in the available text. | A cognitive screening test administered at the eligibility visit to help exclude cognitive impairment. Participants with MoCA scores below 26 were enrolled only if an exhaustive clinician-led neuropsychological evaluation confirmed normal cognition.</data>
      <data key="d3">0.96</data>
      <data key="d4">86f9ab26-f1be-4c98-8916-d978196e27f5_chunk_6,86f9ab26-f1be-4c98-8916-d978196e27f5_chunk_7,86f9ab26-f1be-4c98-8916-d978196e27f5_chunk_31,86f9ab26-f1be-4c98-8916-d978196e27f5_chunk_32</data>
