      <data key="d2">Mild SARS-CoV-2 infection is referenced in relation to healthcare workers with structural and functional brain markers of cognitive impairment after infection. | Variant context used in the cited Japanese study of neutralizing antibody persistence after infection. It is relevant to post-infection immune waning rather than directly to a measured cognitive outcome in the excerpt. | Respiratory infection context in the cited retrospective observational cohort study of Omicron-infected patients during recent regional outbreaks in China. | The initial infection used as the reference point for defining later post-COVID symptoms occurring at least 3 months afterward. | History of SARS-CoV-2 infection used to define the case group in the study. Participants with prior infection reported more symptoms than controls, while only a few objective physiological, cognitive, and blood parameters differed. | Underlying viral infections associated with post-COVID manifestations and with immune alterations such as anti-prothrombin autoantibody enrichment and inflammatory or neurology-related plasma protein profiles. | Respiratory viral infections referenced in the article title 'Identifiability of parameters in mathematical models of SARS-CoV-2 infections in humans.' The provided text does not report cognitive domains, neuropsychological tests, healthcare-worker cohorts, or follow-up timepoints. | Respiratory viral infections named in the cited JAMA Network Open article title about risk factors for infection, hospitalization, and mortality among US nursing home residents.</data>
