    <node id="WORKING MEMORY">
      <data key="d0">WORKING MEMORY</data>
      <data key="d1">COGNITIVE_DOMAIN</data>
      <data key="d2">A specific cognitive domain mentioned in a related article title as substantially impaired in neurological post-COVID syndrome. | A cognitive domain involving short-term maintenance and manipulation of information, identified among the COVID-19-associated cognitive deficit profile and assessed within MoCA-related cognitive evaluation. | A cognitive domain involving temporary storage and manipulation of information. It is one of the domains assessed by the Wechsler Adult Intelligence Scale-Fourth Edition. | Executive cognitive domain cited as influencing Stroop Color-Word test performance, involving maintenance and manipulation of task rules or stimulus-response mappings. | Cognitive domain referenced through a cited study reporting altered brain activation patterns during working-memory tasks in people with post-COVID condition and persistent neuropsychiatric symptoms. | A cognitive domain listed among the functions most commonly affected by cognitive deficits in multiple sclerosis. | A cognitive domain involving temporary storage and manipulation of information. In the text, working memory is assessed using Digit Span. | A specific cognitive domain reported to decline after COVID-19 in the cited systematic review. It is the clearest domain-specific cognitive deficit named in the passage. | A cognitive domain explicitly linked to dorsolateral prefrontal cortex function and also referenced in relation to schizophrenia literature. | Specific cognitive domain highlighted as being affected in prior literature on mild or non-severe COVID-19. | Cognitive domain assessed in individuals with long COVID using neuropsychological tests such as Digit Span. | Specific cognitive domain reported to be affected by worry and anxiety, thereby influencing broader cognitive performance. | Memory and executive-control subdomain trained in week 9 of treatment. | Working memory is identified as a cognitive function that may decline in association with fatigue and reduced alertness. | A cognitive domain assessed through the Digit Span Test, particularly the backward task, which requires active manipulation of information. | A specific cognitive domain referenced in cited evidence about age differences and time-of-day effects, illustrating that cognitive performance can vary with assessment timing. | Cognitive domain involving temporary storage and manipulation of information; assessed by Digits Span and Spatial Span. | A cognitive domain mentioned in the cited meta-analysis on the association of KIBRA with episodic and working memory. | A task-specific cognitive domain reported in menopause research to be poorer among women with cognitive symptoms. | One of the standard seven MCCB cognitive domains. Although not individually named in the excerpted list, the passage refers to seven MCCB domains, and working memory completes the standard MCCB domain set. | A cognitive domain involving temporary storage and manipulation of information; listed among impaired executive-function domains. | A specific cognitive domain explicitly mentioned in the topic title 'Cognitive Deficits and Working Memory in Schizophrenia.' | A cognitive domain listed in the cited transcranial direct current stimulation study. | Cognitive domain described as involved in the formation and retrieval of traumatic memories. | A cognitive domain listed among constructs experimentally shown to improve with nature exposure. | The cognitive domain represented by the NBCK N-back Working Memory Test. | A core cognitive ability assessed one year after the wildfire event, involving short-term maintenance and manipulation of information. | A cognitive domain assessed using the BACS, involving short-term maintenance and manipulation of information. | Cognitive domain involving short-term maintenance and manipulation of information. | A cognitive system implicated in learning task performance. The study aims to distinguish working-memory contributions from reinforcement-learning contributions to valenced learning, building on prior findings that learning decrements previously attributed to reinforcement learning may instead reflect reduced working memory. | A cognitive domain assessed in the study. Working memory performance was significantly enhanced at the circadian-preferred time compared with the non-preferred time of day. | A specific cognitive domain assessed after SARS-CoV-2 infection. Working memory scores were included in the longitudinal cognitive trajectory and improved progressively through 42 months post-COVID. | Cognitive domain described as closely linked to oculomotor behavior in eye-tracking-based paradigms. | Cognitive domain named in the article title as the target of neurofeedback-enhanced training. The provided text does not mention respiratory infection, healthcare workers, infection severity, follow-up timing, or infection-related cognitive outcomes. | Working memory, described as the cognitive function responsible for maintaining and manipulating information. The text presents WM as central to attention and cognitive control, as an early domain affected in mild cognitive impairment, and as the primary target of EEG-based neurofeedback intervention. | Specific cognitive domain linked to the right frontal pole, where modest cortical thickness increases were observed. No infection-related cognitive assessment is described. | Cognitive domain mentioned in the cited depression inpatient-treatment study, where aerobic exercise was reported to have larger effects on working memory than stretching. The excerpt does not specify the measurement instrument, effect size, respiratory-infection exposure, or follow-up timing. | Specific cognitive domain identified as dysfunctional in COVID-19-related cognitive impairment and potentially responsive to carefully monitored cognitive training in selected patients. | Working memory was assessed using validated cognitive measures. No statistically significant association with prior COVID-19 was reported for this domain. | A cognitive domain objectively reported as impaired in post-acute COVID-19 syndrome, involving short-term maintenance and manipulation of information. | A cognitive domain assessed in the study. HIV-infected smokers performed significantly worse than HIV-uninfected smokers on working memory.</data>
