    <node id="EDUCATIONAL LEVEL">
      <data key="d0">EDUCATIONAL LEVEL</data>
      <data key="d1">CONFOUNDING_FACTOR</data>
      <data key="d2">Potential confounding variable checked across groups. The study reported no significant educational-level differences between groups, and unlike age and time interval, it was not identified in the excerpt as a control regressor. | Sociodemographic variable associated with cluster membership; university degree prevalence was highest in minimal motor impairment clusters and lowest in the Severe Motor + Severe Cognitive cluster. | A participant characteristic cited as influencing immediate recall of short stories. Educational level can therefore confound interpretation of memory-test performance. | Demographic factor linked to mental capacity and decision-making; higher education is described as being associated with better cognitive function. | Educational covariate adjusted for because education can influence cognitive test performance and memory-related z-scores. | A socioeconomic and demographic factor compared between intervention completers and the broader screened MCI population; no significant difference was reported. | Socioeconomic factor referenced in disaggregated analysis of post-COVID outcomes. | Participant education is reported; most participants were highly educated and had a university or high-school degree. | Participant education level included as a covariate in the causal effect estimation. | Participant educational attainment included among demographic variables that may independently explain variation in DT estimates. | Self-reported sociodemographic covariate collected from participants. | Sociodemographic factor considered in comparisons between patient groups and controls; the text reports no significant difference in average education levels. | Population characteristic expected to influence cognitive task performance, especially baseline cognitive ability and test sensitivity. | Demographic factor compared across groups; groups were well matched for education level. | Lower education levels were reported more frequently among women attending memory clinics in Qatar and may influence dementia risk estimates. | Participant education level included as a control variable in the whole-brain correlation analysis. | Minimum educational attainment characteristic; all participants had at least a high-school education. Education can influence baseline cognitive performance and familiarity with testing. | Potential selection-related confounding factor: included patients had higher education levels than patients who were not included in the study. | Participant education level reported as categorical values with counts, percentages, and analysis codes across coding schemes. Education is a potential confounding factor because it can influence baseline cognitive performance and interpretation of neuropsychological outcomes, although the excerpt does not describe any respiratory infection, cognitive domain, or effect estimate. | Socioeducational variable controlled as a covariate in analyses examining gray matter volume and perceived stress. | Individual-level categorical control variable including primary school, middle school, high school, and college or above. | Education level was examined as a covariate; no difference in Device performance was detected based on overlap of corresponding 95% confidence intervals. | Educational level is listed as a grouping variable across which associations between COVID-19-related variables and health are analyzed among Ghanaian youths. | Education level was collected as a sociodemographic factor potentially associated with anxiety and depression. | Sociodemographic factor significantly associated with depressive symptoms. | Sociodemographic variable documented both as years of formal schooling and highest degree obtained. Education level is also relevant to cognitive reserve assessment. | Educational attainment is controlled for in ANCOVA models and is relevant to cognitive reserve and baseline neuropsychological performance.</data>
      <data key="d3">0.82</data>
