    <node id="EXERCISE">
      <data key="d0">EXERCISE</data>
      <data key="d1">CONFOUNDING_FACTOR</data>
      <data key="d2">Behavioral factor assessed in the study; no significant difference was reported for higher cognitive failure. | Exercise is cited as a factor examined for its association with mental health during the COVID-19 pandemic; it may modify or confound observed associations between pandemic context and outcomes. | Yes/no covariate indicating whether participants engaged in exercise. | Exercise is cited as a protective behavioral factor during the COVID-19 quarantine period. It is not presented as a neuropsychological measure or cognitive outcome in the excerpt. | A monitored behavioral factor during the winter-over mission and a potential countermeasure or modifier of psycho-physiological deconditioning and brain health. | Behavioral factor mentioned in the article title concerning COVID-19-related stress, exercise, and oral health-related quality of life among older adults. Exercise may be a relevant confounder in cognitive-health research, but no cognitive outcome is reported in the provided text. | A lifestyle behaviour stated to influence mental health outcomes independently of sleep quality. Exercise or physical activity may therefore be an important behavioural factor when interpreting mental health or cognition-related observations.</data>
