      <data key="d0">CHRONIC FATIGUE</data>
      <data key="d1">CONFOUNDING_FACTOR</data>
      <data key="d2">Fatigue condition described in survivors of COVID-19. It may independently contribute to reduced attention, slowed processing, subjective cognitive complaints, or poor neuropsychological performance, making it a potential confounder in cognition-focused post-COVID studies.</data>
      <data key="d3">0.74</data>
      <data key="d4">48d3bb41-1aa4-436d-9ee2-ae98f909777a_chunk_8</data>
      <data key="d5">48d3bb41-1aa4-436d-9ee2-ae98f909777a</data>
      <data key="d6">CHRONIC FATIGUE</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NEUROPSYCHOLOGICAL ALTERATIONS">
      <data key="d0">NEUROPSYCHOLOGICAL ALTERATIONS</data>
      <data key="d1">COGNITIVE_DOMAIN</data>
      <data key="d2">Broad neuropsychological outcome category associated with COVID-19, encompassing cognitive and behavioral-function changes reported across multiple studies. | Broad post-recovery neuropsychological outcome domain examined in the review as linked to post-acute COVID-19. | Broad neuropsychological or cognitive changes reported across studies during acute COVID-19 and the post-COVID-19 phase, with heterogeneous findings depending on evaluation timing.</data>
      <data key="d3">0.88</data>
      <data key="d4">4b18c63f-2f87-46c1-a3ae-8c4337490271_chunk_5</data>
      <data key="d5">4b18c63f-2f87-46c1-a3ae-8c4337490271,af5ff8eb-8135-49d4-81fd-6de4008d60d8</data>
      <data key="d6">NEUROPSYCHOLOGICAL MANIFESTATIONS,NEUROPSYCHOLOGICAL ALTERATIONS</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AGE">
      <data key="d0">AGE</data>
      <data key="d1">CONFOUNDING_FACTOR</data>
      <data key="d2">A demographic factor used to adjust neuropsychological norm values, indicating that test interpretation accounted for age-related variation in performance. | Demographic factor reported for both groups, with mean ages of approximately 53 years in healthy controls and patients with persistent COVID symptoms. | Adjustment factor used when transforming raw neuropsychological test scores into scalar scores, reflecting the influence of age on cognitive test performance. | Participant characteristic noted as influencing inter-network connectivity across the lifespan. | Potential confounding factor for between-group comparisons; the abstract reports that groups did not differ in age. | Participant age was evaluated as a demographic factor; Stage 1 participants did not significantly differ from the overall UKMSR population, but Stage 2 returners were older than non-returners. | A demographic variable assessed as a potential confounding factor for ROCF test values. ROCF values did not correlate with age, although CCL11 plasma levels increased with age. | Demographic factor included in the model; associated with disease severity and also considered relevant to cognitive failure. | Demographic factor derivable from birth date, which was collected in the survey; age may influence baseline cognition, COVID-19 severity, and post-infection symptom risk. | Patient demographic characteristic that differed between groups; COVID-19 patients were older, and responding patients were also older than non-responding patients. Age may influence post-ICU cognitive outcomes independently of COVID-19 status. | Age was used as a matching variable when selecting contemporary controls for comparison with invited SARS-CoV-2 cases. | A demographic factor associated with olfactory performance. Odor memory, identification, and discrimination scores decreased with age, and analyses of MCI-related olfactory differences were adjusted for age. | Pre-illness demographic factor listed as a potential risk factor for long-term sequelae after COVID-19. | Demographic factor listed as affecting post-COVID psychological health. Age is also a major determinant of baseline cognitive performance and vulnerability to post-acute sequelae. | A non-modifiable factor mentioned in relation to dementia and cognitive decline risk. | Potential confounder for biomarker levels and cognitive outcomes; healthy controls were matched to infected participants by age. | Demographic factor used in post-stratification weighting to restore the distribution of eligible hospital personnel. | Participant demographic variable reported in the study; ages ranged from 25 to 80 years, with a mean of 59 years and standard deviation of 13 years. | Baseline demographic characteristic measured at T1; participants in the final sample were on average 72 years old, with SD 5.30 and range 65–95. | Age is identified as a reason for missing cognitive data when children were younger than the age range covered by specific cognitive measures. | Covariate controlled for in the adjusted prospective association between PASS-content and stress resilience. | The COVID-19 questionnaire sample had an average age of 50.7 years. Age is a standard demographic confounder for cognitive, behavioral, and alcohol-related outcomes. | A demographic variable collected in the study. Age is a potential confounding factor because cognitive performance and brain measures can vary with age. | Demographic factor incorporated with plasma Aβ42/40 ratio and APOE-ε4 genotype to improve identification of amyloid positivity. | A demographic variable examined in relation to AS and DT. Older age is described as being associated with higher DT and lower AS in most tasks, except in the language task where word knowledge increases with age. | Self-reported sociodemographic covariate collected from participants. | Participant age; older age emerged as a protective predictor associated with lower occurrence of intrusions. | Sociodemographic covariate included in the regression model to adjust cognitive task summary scores; modeled in decade categories to allow non-linear age effects. | Demographic factor associated with no recovery after SARS-CoV-2 infection and relevant as a potential confounder of long-COVID outcome associations. | A sample characteristic that may influence the observed relationship between cognitive symptoms and objective cognitive performance. | A demographic variable reported for the analyzed educator sample and checked when comparing excluded and included participants. | Demographic variable that differed significantly across groups and may confound interpretation of group differences in cognition or perfusion. | Demographic variable cited as playing an important role in psychological distress risk during the COVID-19 pandemic. Age is also a major confounder in cognitive research because baseline cognitive performance and vulnerability to post-infectious symptoms vary across age groups. | Participants were required to be 16 to 45 years old, and age was used in block grouping to minimize demographic imbalance between randomized conditions. | Demographic factor evaluated across the HC, MDD, and StD groups. The text reports no statistically significant between-group age differences. | Age is highlighted through the review's focus on age-specific incidence rates and is a key factor in interpreting dementia epidemiology. | A participant characteristic matched between the two groups to reduce confounding. | Participant age was included as a covariate to account for potential age-related differences in study variables. | Demographic variable considered in comparisons between study participants and the wider TwinsUK cohort; sex differences were adjusted for age. | Participant age was used to convert raw Vocabulary and Matrix Reasoning scores into age-adjusted T scores before calculating abbreviated full-scale IQ. | Age is referenced in relation to late-life depression and all-cause mortality risk, and may influence observed associations in elderly populations. | Demographic factor referenced in the Canadian Human Activity Pattern Survey 2 citation as influencing time-activity patterns. It could act as a confounder in studies of exposure, infection risk, or cognitive outcomes, though no such analysis is provided here. | Established determinant of retinal microcirculation that may have stronger associations with retinal vessel metrics than environmental microbiome exposure. | Demographic factor compared between cohorts; no significant difference in age was reported. | Potential confounding factor; age-related trends were reported as equivalent by site. | Self-reported demographic variable collected from participants; age can independently influence mental health and cognitive or behavioral performance. | A demographic variable collected during the semistructured interview. Age may influence clinical, biomedical, and psychological assessment results. | A sociodemographic factor; the passage notes that prior research has not consistently shown older adults to be more vulnerable to financial exploitation. | Healthy controls were matched to patients on age to reduce demographic imbalance between groups. | Participant characteristic statistically controlled in the whole-brain correlation, cross-validation regression, and mediation-related analyses. | Individual-level continuous control variable that may contribute to variation in depression symptoms. | Demographic factor associated with differences in depression prevalence, with major depressive disorder prevalence declining from adolescents to adults aged ≥60 years. | Matching factor used to pair controls 1:1 with qualified cases within five years of age. | Participant age, reported to show a linear negative association with PVT-prefrontal connectivity. | Clinical covariate included in the models as a potential confounder of APNS outcomes. | Participant age reported in years as a demographic characteristic and compared between male and female participants. | Demographic variable collected from participants that may be relevant when interpreting psychological assessment results. | Age was a key demographic stratum used in the stratified random sampling design and was central to grouping children from birth through 18 years for BMI and head circumference chart construction. | Demographic variable tested in ANOVA analyses and reported not to covary with motor-evoked potentials obtained from TMS protocols. | Subgroup variable used in age analyses, where Boschloo’s Exact Test examined statistical differences in device performance among age subgroups; not explicitly established as a confounder in the text. | Demographic factor explicitly mentioned as influencing or stratifying COVID-19 death patterns in the descriptive international comparison. | Age is listed as a grouping variable across which associations between COVID-19-related variables and health are analyzed among Ghanaian youths. | Demographic factor evaluated for association with anxiety and depression; no significant correlation was found. | Demographic covariate controlled for in the older-adult analysis; relevant because both sleep patterns and mental health outcomes may vary by age. | Sociodemographic variable collected during study assessments. Age is a key background factor that may influence cognitive performance and interpretation of study outcomes. | A covariate measured at HCAP participation. It was included because age is an established risk factor for severe infection and dementia and is strongly related to cognitive performance. | Participant demographic characteristic extracted from electronic health records; eligibility required age of at least 65 years and age may influence cognitive function. | Participant characteristic used to match SARS-CoV-2 infected participants with controls, reducing the risk that age-related differences explain observed longitudinal effects. | Participant-level factor suggested as a possible contributor to Participant C’s slightly longer reaction times. | Demographic factor compared between active and sham groups; mean age did not differ significantly. | A participant characteristic examined in relation to sex, CSF sex hormones, and CSF protein levels. | A demographic adjustment factor used when interpreting SDMT standard deviations. Age may influence normative performance on information-processing-speed measures. | Chronological age considered as a potential confounding factor; the reported menopause-associated proteomic changes tracked with hormones rather than age. | A demographic variable used to adjust the MoCA corrected total score, indicating its relevance to interpretation of cognitive test performance. | Lifespan or age-related factor relevant to studies of brain structure and function across adulthood and older age. | A demographic factor mentioned as a possible stratification or adjustment variable that can influence interpretation of cell-state analyses. | A factor associated with movement problems that physical therapists may address. | Stratification factor in the analysis of associations between childhood experiences and mood challenges. | Demographic factor associated with sleep quality in the prison sample. | Participant demographic characteristic reported for the sample; mean age was 25.6 years with SD = 9.19.</data>
      <data key="d3">0.95</data>
      <data key="d4">86f9ab26-f1be-4c98-8916-d978196e27f5_chunk_8,86f9ab26-f1be-4c98-8916-d978196e27f5_chunk_9,86f9ab26-f1be-4c98-8916-d978196e27f5_chunk_10,86f9ab26-f1be-4c98-8916-d978196e27f5_chunk_16</data>
