    <node id="POST-ACUTE COVID PERIOD">
      <data key="d0">POST-ACUTE COVID PERIOD</data>
      <data key="d1">FOLLOW_UP_TIMEPOINT</data>
      <data key="d2">Time period after the acute phase of COVID-19 infection during which persistent symptoms and potential cognitive-function effects are evaluated. | Time period after the acute phase of COVID-19 during which neuropsychological manifestations, persistent symptoms, and related outcomes were assessed. | Time period after the acute COVID-19 infection phase, corresponding to the PACS context in which persistent cognitive and non-neurological symptoms are discussed. | Time period after the acute phase of COVID-19 illness during which neurocognitive disorders and cognitive complaints are evaluated in the referenced study. | Period after the acute COVID-19 episode during which persistent symptoms such as cognitive complaints, fatigue, cephalea, and olfactory alterations were reported. | Post-infection context represented by post-COVID-19 syndrome, during which fatigue and cognitive impairment were assessed. The excerpt does not provide a precise follow-up duration. | Period after the initial infection during which patients may require rehabilitation, mental health, home health, long-term care and other services, and during which neurological consequences remain a research priority. | Post-acute phase after COVID-19 infection, reflected in the phrases 'post-acute COVID-19 syndrome,' 'post-acute sequelae of COVID-19,' and 'Long COVID.' No exact duration after infection is provided in the excerpt. | Post-acute phase after COVID-19 referenced through terms such as post-acute COVID-19 syndrome and post-acute sequelae of COVID-19. It frames the timing of persistent abnormalities after the acute infection. | Post-acute phase after COVID-19 infection, corresponding to the period in which persistent or delayed functional brain-network effects were studied. | Follow-up phase implied by long COVID and post-COVID-19 condition, during which persistent symptoms such as cognitive symptom clusters may be evaluated.</data>
      <data key="d3">0.82</data>
      <data key="d4">4b18c63f-2f87-46c1-a3ae-8c4337490271_chunk_11</data>
