      <data key="d1">BIOLOGICAL_MECHANISM</data>
      <data key="d2">A biological mechanism referenced as relevant to language impairments and the translation of laboratory findings into clinical rehabilitation. | The biological mechanism proposed as potentially being triggered by sufficient dose intensity of the intervention practice. | A biological mechanism that may be disrupted by cognitive fatigue, potentially reducing the effectiveness of cognitive rehabilitation in patients with cognitive impairments. | Referenced as a mechanism of brain flexibility, including proposed 'upward' and 'downward' forms of neuroplasticity. | A brain physiological process involving stimulation-induced or experience-related changes in neural function, discussed as one of the mechanisms underlying behavior and cognition. | Adaptive remodeling of neural circuits after stroke; reported to be enhanced by Zeb2/Axin2-enriched BMSC-derived exosomes. | Adaptive neural change referenced in the context of olfactory loss and regain. | Neuroplasticity is described as a mechanism potentially modulated by non-invasive brain stimulation, contributing to persistent therapeutic effects.</data>
