    <node id="PHYSICAL ACTIVITY">
      <data key="d0">PHYSICAL ACTIVITY</data>
      <data key="d1">CONFOUNDING_FACTOR</data>
      <data key="d2">Trigger noted by respondents for relapse or worsening of persistent neurological symptoms after COVID-19. | Behavioral health factor assessed by the International Physical Activity Questionnaire Taiwan Long Version. Physical activity can affect physical functioning, fatigue, and quality-of-life measures. | Lifestyle factor described as cognitively stimulating and helpful for coping with stress; reduced activity may contribute to cognitive changes independently of infection. | Physical activity is listed as a factor associated with mental health during COVID-19 lockdown. It could act as a behavioral confounder in studies of post-COVID or pandemic-related outcomes, though no cognitive outcome is reported in the excerpt. | Behavioral factor mentioned in the title of a related article stating that post-acute sequelae of SARS-CoV-2 associate with physical inactivity in COVID-19 survivors. The excerpt does not establish it as a causal factor or link it specifically to cognition. | Lifestyle factor listed as affecting mental health during COVID-19. Physical activity may independently influence psychological well-being and cognitive functioning, making it a relevant covariate in COVID-19 outcome studies. | Modifiable lifestyle risk factor associated with dementia risk and broader non-communicable disease burden. | A catabolic stressor and contributor to anabolic resistance; older people tend to experience higher rates of inactivity. | An anabolic stimulus that influences muscle protein synthesis and improves the effect of protein supplementation when performed as resistance exercise. | Referenced as a variable in a cited study on physical activity, stress, and quality of life among community-dwelling older adults during the post-COVID-19 pandemic period. It may be relevant as a behavioral confounder in broader cognition or quality-of-life analyses, but no direct cognitive relationship is stated here. | Physical activity is referenced as the subject of an article on the COVID-19 pandemic and may represent a behavioral factor relevant to health outcomes, although the excerpt provides no quantitative findings. | Wake activity and exercise-related factor, including gym use, associated with less pronounced gray matter volume reductions in exploratory analyses. | Physical activity is mentioned in a cited study title alongside stress and quality of life among community-dwelling older adults during the post-COVID-19 pandemic period. | A lifestyle factor that was not specifically controlled or monitored. The text notes that physical activity can influence gut microbiota composition and inflammatory markers. | Behavioral activity level measured by FitBit-derived average steps over 7 consecutive days. Greater physical activity was associated with fewer depressive symptoms in PSEN-1 E280A mutation carriers, but was not directly related to NfL levels or memory performance. | Lower physical activity predicted greater insomnia symptom severity, which in turn predicted increased depressive and anxiety-related symptoms; its direct effects on mental health outcomes were significant during lockdown. | Reduced or insufficient physical activity; reported in prior lockdown research as associated with worse mental-health outcomes. | Lifestyle factor in midlife and late life associated with brain integrity and cognitive trajectories. | A behavioral factor cited as influencing health-related quality of life and mental well-being; it may need consideration as a confounding or explanatory factor in analyses of well-being outcomes.</data>
      <data key="d3">0.95</data>
      <data key="d4">57aa938b-52d6-42df-a29c-33afca074c75_chunk_2</data>
      <data key="d5">57aa938b-52d6-42df-a29c-33afca074c75,338d5c8e-550b-436e-8de2-d7967ecb69eb,9ac1c295-0116-47ef-a0b9-9e8531f02406,bc6b9304-2c43-48f3-9a0e-787398c896ae,ed7d7537-d658-4242-92f7-fb95689c31b6,9187db96-dcf3-44df-84d0-501718447003,b9a4d38f-8897-48a5-8f6c-27e19ee6770e,86d550d9-5e6f-448f-b14a-da2055e63c80,c25b6f4c-84d2-420a-96d3-e1e3b098131c,48d3bb41-1aa4-436d-9ee2-ae98f909777a,432eead5-bd19-425c-9c09-f7783e9ba546,fb8152f0-db86-4a24-bd62-ab9650b4159b,5e8deba7-6350-4d1f-9bea-c12bfb32945c,ed93f40c-bf1d-4c99-b4e6-3ee0682b88c6,2032fba9-e1ef-40e4-8be1-15076f27c7ff,c4e2b48b-b968-4f16-8949-178e8840bad4,1f409999-3c26-48fb-a37b-15dc787bc0f0</data>
      <data key="d6">PHYSICAL ACTIVITY,PHYSICAL INACTIVITY</data>
    </node>
    <node id="COVID-19 SEVERITY">
      <data key="d0">COVID-19 SEVERITY</data>
      <data key="d1">DISEASE_SEVERITY</data>
      <data key="d2">Overall severity of acute COVID-19 illness, including hospitalization status, reported as relevant to later neurological and cognitive outcomes. | Clinical severity of COVID-19 considered as a possible determinant of later cognitive failure. The conclusion states that cognitive failure after COVID-19 does not solely depend on disease severity. | Latent disease-severity construct for COVID-19. The text states that loss of smell, shortness of breath, and headache were selected to build a latent variable representing COVID severity. | Severity of acute COVID-19, noted as similar across compared patient groups, suggesting that observed outcome differences were not primarily attributable to worse infection severity. | Severity dimension of COVID-19 referenced by cited evidence showing that gut microbiota composition reflects disease severity. | Clinical severity of acute COVID-19, referenced as potentially influenced by ACE2 variants. | Degree of acute COVID-19 illness. The excerpt states that pulmonary restriction during follow-up has a positive correlation with COVID-19 severity. | Clinical or epidemiologic severity of COVID-19 referenced in the citation reporting that spatial heterogeneity can lead to substantial local variations in COVID-19 timing and severity. The text does not specify severity categories, hospitalization status, or individual-level disease severity. | General severity dimension of COVID-19 referenced in the title associating peripheral-blood miR-144 levels with COVID-19 severity and mortality. | Severity of COVID-19 is explicitly mentioned as an outcome investigated by the COVID-19 Host Genetics Initiative in relation to host genetic factors.</data>
