      <data key="d0">SLEEP</data>
      <data key="d1">CONFOUNDING_FACTOR</data>
      <data key="d2">Clinical variable included in the psychometric assessment; sleep disturbance may independently affect attention, concentration, and memory. | Variable assessed in the broader survey but explicitly not reported or analyzed in the current paper. Sleep may be relevant to cognition, but the excerpt states it was outside the scope of the current analysis. | Health and behavioral factor referenced in relation to infectious disease risk; sleep can also influence cognitive performance. | A monitored behavioral factor during the winter-over mission; its relationship with exercise and brain health in ICE environments is described as complex and heterogeneous. | A physiological state referenced as mutually linked with circadian clocks and as influencing physiology and cognition. | Sleep is mentioned as a health and lifestyle factor relevant to depressive and anxiety-related outcomes, particularly in relation to physical activity and cumulative effects on mental health. | A behavioral factor evaluated alongside cognitive testing and pain assessment. Sleep status may influence cognitive performance and could confound interpretation of postoperative neurocognitive outcomes. | Sleep was assessed using questionnaires and overnight monitoring because sleep quality or sleepiness can influence cognitive performance and related study outcomes.</data>
