      <data key="d1">RESPIRATORY_INFECTION</data>
      <data key="d2">Acute respiratory infection episode preceding the neuropsychological assessment. The excerpt describes post-acute symptoms compatible with COVID-19, including dyspnea, anosmia, dysgeusia, hospitalization, oxygen support, and ICU admission. | Index acute respiratory viral infection used as the starting point for longitudinal follow-up of long COVID morbidity over 6 months and 2 years. | The acute infectious phase after which persistent symptoms and post-COVID sequelae are evaluated. | The antecedent respiratory infection caused by SARS-CoV-2 after which patients developed persistent long COVID symptoms. The study examined how severity of this initial infection related to later symptom burden. | The respiratory infection exposure under study, also described as acute COVID-19. Adults were followed after SARS-CoV-2 infection to characterize post-COVID cognitive trajectories. | Index viral infection used in the WHO definition of Post-COVID-19 Condition; PCC symptoms occur or persist after this acute infection.</data>
