      <data key="d1">CONFOUNDING_FACTOR</data>
      <data key="d2">Income is mentioned as a factor associated with mental health during COVID-19 lockdown. It may represent socioeconomic confounding in broader COVID-19 outcome studies, but no neuropsychological outcome is described here. | Socioeconomic factor listed as affecting mental health during COVID-19. Income may influence access to resources, pandemic stress exposure, health status, and therefore interpretation of COVID-19-associated mental or cognitive outcomes. | Participant income included as a control variable in the whole-brain correlation analysis. | Socioeconomic variable included as a control covariate in analyses of gray matter volume and perceived stress. | Socioeconomic variable studied for its longitudinal relationship with depressive symptoms. | A socioeconomic factor evaluated for its association with Device performance; performance was reported as consistent across income levels. | Income is listed in the Austria lockdown study title as a factor examined in relation to mental health during the COVID-19 lockdown.</data>
