    <node id="LONG-TERM FOLLOW-UP">
      <data key="d0">LONG-TERM FOLLOW-UP</data>
      <data key="d1">FOLLOW_UP_TIMEPOINT</data>
      <data key="d2">Later period after COVID-19 illness or hospitalization during which persistent neurological and cognitive manifestations were described. | Later observation period after acute COVID-19. The cited literature reports that neurochemical signs of astrocytic and neuronal injury normalized during this period. | Post-acute follow-up period during which cognitive impairments were reported to correlate with acute infection severity. | A long-term assessment period for evaluating cognitive outcomes in multiple sclerosis patients. | Non-specific extended follow-up period after COVID-19 during which effects of vaccination, booster doses, and heterologous vaccination regimens on cognitive failure remain unanswered. | Extended follow-up period needed to evaluate the lasting impact of COVID-19; no precise duration is specified in the text. | General post-acute follow-up period referenced in studies of long-term outcomes after severe COVID-19 infection. | Future follow-up period recommended by the authors because the current study assessed outcomes only 3–6 months after ICU discharge. | Later follow-up period referenced in the title 'Vascular dysfunction of COVID-19 is partially reverted in the long-term.' It indicates assessment of whether COVID-19-associated vascular abnormalities persist or improve over time. | The post-acute assessment period during which persistent or delayed health consequences of COVID-19 infection are evaluated. | Extended post-recovery period during which microstructural and cerebral blood-flow changes were reported in patients recovered from COVID-19, including those without overt neurological manifestations. | Extended follow-up periods proposed for future studies to determine whether intervention benefits are maintained over time. | Future or extended follow-up horizon proposed for characterizing the trend of sNfL and sGFAP elevation and the consequences of CNS damage. | Later, non-specified follow-up period after the first COVID-19 pandemic wave, during which persistent effects on subjective cognitive functioning were reported. | A later post-injury assessment period implied by references to persisting and long-term outcomes after pediatric brain injury. | A later assessment point not included in the study but recommended for future research to evaluate persistence or change in intrusive thoughts over time. | Extended follow-up period used in studies assessing long-term outcomes after Lyme disease. | Extended follow-up period used to evaluate psychotic symptom stability, symptom development, and antipsychotic medication use. | The later treatment-response assessment horizon targeted by the prediction framework. | A chronic or post-acute time frame implied by the phrase 'long-term neuropsychiatric COVID-19.' | Future extended follow-up periods recommended to further evaluate whether predictive hit rates remain valid over time. | Later post-infection assessment period implied by references to long-lasting cognitive effects, symptom trajectories over time, and long-term health-related quality of life. | A follow-up period emphasized as difficult to maintain in NCD research, particularly because comprehensive in-person neuropsychological testing can burden older adults after surgery. | Future follow-up period needed to determine whether the deleterious brain-related effects of COVID-19 persist or can be partially reversed. | Later assessment point implied by the cited study title referring to long-term effects of hyperbaric oxygenation in patients with Long COVID-19 syndrome. | A longitudinal time frame over which plasma proteomic profile changes were assessed. | A later assessment period referenced in relation to pulmonary rehabilitation outcomes in mild COPD.</data>
      <data key="d3">0.86</data>
      <data key="d4">4b18c63f-2f87-46c1-a3ae-8c4337490271_chunk_30,4b18c63f-2f87-46c1-a3ae-8c4337490271_chunk_32,4b18c63f-2f87-46c1-a3ae-8c4337490271_chunk_37,4b18c63f-2f87-46c1-a3ae-8c4337490271_chunk_49</data>
