    <node id="GUT MICROBIOTA">
      <data key="d0">GUT MICROBIOTA</data>
      <data key="d1">BIOLOGICAL_MECHANISM</data>
      <data key="d2">Complex intestinal microbial ecosystem described as substantially affecting human health and multiple diseases. In the passage, gut microbiota is implicated in gastrointestinal symptoms of long COVID and in reflecting COVID-19 severity and immune dysfunction. | Intestinal microbial community discussed in a cited article as having a role in immune homeostasis and autoimmunity. | A biological system described as being modulated in the context of probiotic-related antidepressant-like effects. It is relevant to brain-gut and inflammation-related mechanisms but is not directly tied to measured post-COVID cognition in this excerpt. | Gut microbiota is referenced as a biological mechanism targeted by nutritional modulation to alleviate severe gastrointestinal symptoms in Post-Acute COVID-19 syndrome. | Described as a biological factor investigated for its interactions with viral and dietary environmental factors and immune system responses in neurological autoimmune diseases. | The gut-resident community of bacteria, archaea, viruses, and eukaryotic microbes, along with their collective genomes and surrounding environment; described as potentially important for healthy physiology in ageing. | The gut microbial community, presented as having the potential to alter host physiology and contribute to metabolic phenotypes. | The intestinal microbial ecosystem described as an important modulator of aging and disease progression. | The intestinal microbial community described as interacting with cognitive functioning and broader brain-related outcomes. | Microbial community profiled from shotgun metagenomic data to estimate its contribution to variability in post-trauma APNS outcomes. | Gastrointestinal microbial community referenced in related article titles concerning SARS-CoV-2 pathogenesis and post-COVID conditions. It is represented as a mechanistic component because microbiota changes may influence endotoxicity, immune activation, and gut-brain-axis signaling.</data>
