      <data key="d1">COGNITIVE_DOMAIN</data>
      <data key="d2">A cognitive domain identified as one of the functions most commonly affected by cognitive deficits in multiple sclerosis. | A cognitive domain discussed as a proposed primary information-processing deficit in multiple sclerosis and as a major contributor to poor performance on the Paced Addition Test. The passage also notes that focusing only on this domain during brief assessment may miss substantial impairment in a selective cognitive subtype. | A cognitive domain reflecting the speed of interpreting and responding to information. The text identifies it as engaged by the Stroop task and potentially negatively affected by fatigue. | A cognitive domain referenced in the cited study on slowness of information processing in Parkinson’s disease. | A cognitive domain evaluated in the study using the Identification and Detection tests within the NTB. These two tests were used to form the exploratory Attention Composite endpoint. | A cognitive domain explicitly mentioned in the cited Gulf War military pesticide applicator study as being affected in neuropsychological functioning. | A cognitive domain described as being measured by the Stroop color-word task, reflecting the speed with which participants process task-relevant information and generate responses. | Cognitive domain reflecting the speed and efficiency of processing information; referenced as slowed after severe traumatic brain injury and commonly assessed by processing-speed tests. | Cognitive outcome reported as significantly improved after HBOT compared with baseline in ME/CFS patients.</data>
