      <data key="d0">MALE SEX</data>
      <data key="d1">CONFOUNDING_FACTOR</data>
      <data key="d2">Male sex is mentioned as not having been identified as a risk factor for developing long COVID in the cited review. | A sex-related factor associated with higher representation in the Severe Motor + Severe Cognitive cluster and proposed as a possible susceptibility factor for more severe cognitive impairment. | A demographic characteristic associated with higher risk of long-term sequelae in subgroup analysis. Male sex may act as a risk modifier or confounding factor for post-COVID sequelae. | A sex-related factor noted in the text because the incidence of stroke after SARS-CoV-2 infection is reported to be higher in men than in women. | Demographic factor modifying the association between physical inactivity and dementia prevalence; smoking was also reported as more prevalent among men than women. | Male sex is used as the comparison group in the cited observation that females have a higher detection rate of depressive symptoms than males. | Associations between indoor microbial loads and retinal microcirculation were reported to be stronger among boys. | Demographic characteristic common among participants and consistent with Gulf War deployment demographics. | Sex-related factor; volume loss was reported as more prominent in males among familial high-risk individuals. | A sex-based subgroup used in differential item functioning analysis to evaluate whether items functioned equivalently across groups. | Sex-related demographic factor more common among hospitalized SARS-CoV-2-positive participants. The text states that hospitalized patients were more likely to be men than non-hospitalized cases. | Men served as a comparison group for plasma estrone and estradiol levels. | Sex category associated in prior research with greater likelihood of externalized responses, such as anger and impulsivity, after childhood adversity. | Participant demographic characteristic; studies described samples consisting of adult males, which may influence generalizability and associations involving sleep, aggression, and hormone levels.</data>
