      <data key="d1">CONFOUNDING_FACTOR</data>
      <data key="d2">Social and economic background factor that may affect baseline cognition, recovery, and access to care, potentially confounding observed post-COVID cognitive outcomes. | Potential confounding factor weakly positively associated with neuropsychological test performance, with rho = 0.290 and p &lt; 0.01; adjustment for socioeconomic status did not remove the significant group difference. | Social determinant identified as important for achieving good cognitive function and noted as being disrupted during the pandemic, making it relevant to interpretation of cognitive outcomes. | A factor identified in the UK Biobank analysis as a major contributor to dementia risk and also listed among non-modifiable factors. | Demographic variable collected before randomization for enrolled patients; it may influence access, development, or rehabilitation-related outcomes. | Demographic variable collected before randomization that may independently influence adjustment, rehabilitation outcomes, or intellectual test performance. | Socioeconomic status variable compared between groups at baseline; no significant difference was detected. | Socioeconomic status is mentioned in a cited reference title concerning in utero fetal brain development. It is a potential confounding factor in neurodevelopmental studies, but no infection-related cognitive association is described here. | A socioeconomic factor referenced in the figure as SES and discussed as part of the broader network of interacting dementia risk factors. | A family circumstance described as part of broader dysfunction and linked to higher emotional distress in children. | A social contextual factor linked to stress, health, and disease outcomes and potentially relevant as a confounder in neuropsychological or biological-development studies. | A factor explicitly noted as being accounted for in the association between neighbourhood greenness and executive function. | Socio-economic status was considered a potential confounder in the study's directed acyclic graph and covariate selection. It may influence both environmental exposures and cognitive functioning, requiring adjustment in analyses. | Participant socioeconomic status assessed as individual or family affluence; socioeconomic status may influence health, mental health, educational opportunity, and cognitive performance. | Sociodemographic factor associated with financial exploitation risk; lower socioeconomic status was linked to the greatest risk. | A social and economic stratification factor described as influencing perceptions of relative deprivation and potentially modifying the mental-health impact of CET policy implementation. | Matching factor used when comparing controls with patients. | Socioeconomic status was explicitly included as a demographic stratum to ensure the sample represented children from diverse socioeconomic backgrounds across the UAE. | A social and potentially modifiable factor associated with disability risk in individuals with multiple sclerosis. | Baseline contextual factor compared between SARS-CoV-2 positive and control groups; it was assessed via the Townsend deprivation index and may relate to exposure, health status, and cognition.</data>
