ty-managed infection. | Severity context for COVID-19 cases requiring hospital admission. The excerpt focuses on sequelae following hospitalisation rather than mild outpatient infection or ICU-specific disease. | Severity stratum indicating COVID-19 cases requiring hospitalization. This group is specifically referenced in the study of depressive symptoms at three-month follow-up. | Hospitalized severe COVID-19 subgroup referenced in relation to objective olfactory findings and hypoxemia during hospitalization. | A more severe clinical context of COVID-19 described in patients admitted to the hospital, including those with acute kidney injury and renal insufficiency at discharge. | Severity context referring to COVID-19 patients requiring hospitalization; serum NfL and GFAP levels were reported as increased in hospitalized COVID-19 patients. | Severity-defined patient group requiring hospitalization for COVID-19. The cited JAMA Network Open study assessed symptom, disability, and financial trajectories in this group at 6 months; ICU status or finer severity strata are not specified in the provided text. | COVID-19 illness severe enough to require hospital admission; used to define the cohort in which multivariate cognitive deficit profiles and acute-phase correlates were studied. | A more severe clinical subgroup represented by hospitalised cohorts. Half of the studies in the first meta-analysis investigated hospitalised cohorts, which may limit generalisability of long-COVID symptom prevalence estimates. | Clinical severity subgroup consisting of patients hospitalized with COVID-19, referenced in studies of prothrombotic autoantibodies, annexin A2 autoimmunity, and new-onset IgG autoantibodies. | Clinically significant COVID-19 severity context referenced in the study reporting that autoimmunity to annexin A2 predicts mortality among hospitalized patients. | Disease severity/context category explicitly mentioned in the title 'Coagulation parameters abnormalities and their relation to clinical outcomes in hospitalized and severe COVID-19 patients.' Hospitalization indicates clinically significant disease, but no cognitive outcomes are reported. | Severity category indicating COVID-19 illness requiring hospitalization; the cited cohort evidence concerns hospitalized patients.</data>
