      <data key="d2">Biological communication pathway linking gut microbiota changes with brain-related behavioral, biological, and psychological outcomes. | Biological mechanism proposed to connect psychological well-being, emotional regulation, and eating patterns. | A proposed biological pathway linking gut microbiota activity to brain and cognitive function. | A biological mechanism involving bidirectional gut-brain pathways influenced by the microbiota. The passage presents the gut–brain axis as an established concept relevant to potential links between microbiota and cognition. | Biological communication pathway through which probiotics are suggested to influence cognitive development and neurodegeneration-related aging outcomes. | The gut-brain axis is presented as a mechanistic pathway through which probiotics may influence psychological and emotional outcomes in older adults. | Bidirectional communication pathway between the gut microbiome and the brain. The text invokes the gut-brain axis as a framework by which microbiome characteristics could influence neuroinflammation and APNS pathogenesis. | The proposed pathway linking COVID-19 gastrointestinal disruption, intestinal dysbiosis, barrier permeability, neurovascular compromise, brain exposure to harmful substances, and cognitive deficits.</data>
