      <data key="d1">NEUROPSYCH_TEST</data>
      <data key="d2">Neuropsychological test referenced as the Trail Making Test, commonly used to assess attention, processing speed, visual scanning, and executive set-shifting. | The Trail Making Test is cited as a neuropsychological test that could predict impairment across cognitive domains in patients with multiple sclerosis. | Neuropsychological test identified in the protocol as assessing attention. | Neuropsychological test cited through Reitan's reference on its validity as an indicator of organic brain damage. The excerpt identifies the test but does not specify which cognitive domains were assessed in the surrounding study or any infection-related findings. | A neuropsychological task in the computerized assessment battery. Secondary measures included Number Reaction Time and switching cost during task performance. | A neuropsychological test abbreviated as TMT in the figure legend and included as a cognitive feature in the data model. | Trail Making Test, cited as a time-based neuropsychological measure. Because lower completion times indicate better performance, percentile ranks are reversed so that higher percentiles indicate better cognitive performance. | Neuropsychological test, also referenced as TMT, used to assess processing speed and executive components such as task switching, working memory contributions, inhibition/interference control, and visuomotor demands. | Neuropsychological assessment including Trails A and B. The excerpt reports longitudinal increases in time taken to complete both tasks among SARS-CoV-2 infected participants. | A neuropsychological test cited in relation to how physical layout affects test performance. | Trail Making Test, listed as a neuropsychological test included in the cognitive assessment materials available in the repository.</data>
