    <node id="INSOMNIA">
      <data key="d0">INSOMNIA</data>
      <data key="d1">CONFOUNDING_FACTOR</data>
      <data key="d2">Sleep-related symptom reported at T1. Insomnia may affect attention, memory, and perceived cognitive performance. | Sleep-related symptom listed among the most common persistent long COVID symptoms. Insomnia can independently impair attention, memory, and concentration, making it a plausible contributor to cognitive complaints. | Sleep-related problem reported after COVID-19 recovery. Insomnia can independently impair attention, memory, and processing speed, making it an important potential confounder for cognitive symptoms. | Sleep-related symptom domain assessed with the Insomnia Severity Index. Insomnia is relevant as a potential confounder because sleep disturbance can influence attention, memory, processing speed, and subjective cognitive complaints, although no cognitive endpoint is presented in the excerpt. | Persistent sleep-related psychological issue documented among healthcare workers during the COVID-19 outbreak; relevant as a potential confounding factor because disrupted sleep can influence fatigue, distress, and performance. | Sleep-related mental health outcome reported among healthcare workers during the COVID-19 pandemic. Insomnia is relevant as a potential confounder because poor sleep can impair attention, processing speed, and executive functioning. | Insomnia was listed as one of the depressive symptoms assessed by the PHQ-9. Sleep disturbance can independently affect attention, processing speed, executive function, and fatigue-related cognitive performance. | Insomnia is cited in relation to anxiety and depression, including diagnostic and management implications and possible bidirectional associations. It may confound cognition-related analyses because insomnia can independently impair cognitive performance and increase symptom reporting. | Sleep disturbance reported as an adverse consequence of social isolation measures in Taiwan and as a symptom amplified by lockdown measures in Thailand. In cognition-focused studies, insomnia could independently influence cognitive performance or complaints. | A psychological effect reported in Antarctic winter-over personnel. Insomnia may independently impair cognitive functioning and overlap with disrupted sleep as a contributor to cognitive impairment. | Sleep-related disturbance identified among mental health problems during the COVID-19 pandemic; insomnia could independently influence cognitive functioning in related studies. | Sleep-related variable identified as a mediator in the relationship between social media addiction and mental health among university students. | Clinical levels of insomnia are reported in a United States nationwide survey, with rates of 17.6% before the COVID-19 pandemic and 19.0% during the pandemic. Sleep disturbance can be a potential confounder in cognitive-outcome studies. | A sleep-related condition described as intimately associated with symptoms of depression and anxiety. It is relevant as a potential non-infectious factor that may influence mental health and, indirectly, cognitive outcomes. | Insomnia is cited in relation to suicidal behaviour in prisoners. It is relevant because insomnia may independently impair attention, working memory, emotion regulation, and executive functioning, potentially confounding observed cognitive or behavioral outcomes.</data>
      <data key="d3">0.85</data>
      <data key="d4">86f9ab26-f1be-4c98-8916-d978196e27f5_chunk_10</data>
      <data key="d5">86f9ab26-f1be-4c98-8916-d978196e27f5,5e787f6f-1547-4d32-aa5f-4a0f4d841129,c69cfb78-198c-48f8-b6ac-7bf289971093,338d5c8e-550b-436e-8de2-d7967ecb69eb,d134d8d2-15f1-4ff0-8200-7d5648814a90,3f321026-7152-49c7-8437-9d8bc137d2de,46ccdeeb-683a-4650-9e07-68526b50880c,3b3e91e3-1658-4d23-b1ac-d599d49e418a,48d3bb41-1aa4-436d-9ee2-ae98f909777a,432eead5-bd19-425c-9c09-f7783e9ba546,58d74265-e619-4fb5-901a-b5a77919251b,b2180992-3aba-4ab5-b7bd-4b9cce0aef9f,49cf3a6d-f9ae-40b3-a69d-cb2b9c6b11ee,2032fba9-e1ef-40e4-8be1-15076f27c7ff,531dd921-3435-419e-b587-7dfeebb99084</data>
      <data key="d6">INSOMNIA</data>
