    <node id="ACUTE COVID-19">
      <data key="d0">ACUTE COVID-19</data>
      <data key="d1">RESPIRATORY_INFECTION</data>
      <data key="d2">Initial acute COVID-19 illness used as the reference infection after which Long COVID prevalence and quality-of-life impact were evaluated at a 2-year follow-up timepoint. | Acute respiratory infection discussed in relation to post-acute survivor outcomes, including incident cardiovascular outcomes, grip strength, and exercise capacity compared with controls. | Acute phase of COVID-19 infection referenced in relation to skeletal muscle alterations and systemic complications such as hypoxia. | Initial acute phase of COVID-19 infection referenced in the citation 'Persistent symptoms in patients after acute COVID-19.' It provides the infection stage preceding later persistent symptoms, but the excerpt does not specify cognitive symptoms or objective cognitive assessment. | Acute respiratory infection discussed in a longitudinal observational study linking acute illness characteristics to later pulmonary function and respiratory symptoms. | Acute phase of COVID-19 during which probiotics are reported to modulate gut microbiota and reduce inflammation, with associated clinical benefits such as milder symptoms, fewer complications, and lower mortality. | Acute phase of COVID-19 infection. Studies focused solely on acute COVID-19 without long-term sequelae were excluded from the primary analyses. | Respiratory infectious disease referenced in the cited observational longitudinal study. The title links acute COVID-19 with respiratory failure severity and chest CT findings, but it does not report cognitive outcomes or neuropsychological measures.</data>
