    <node id="COGNITIVE DYSFUNCTION">
      <data key="d0">COGNITIVE DYSFUNCTION</data>
      <data key="d1">COGNITIVE_DOMAIN</data>
      <data key="d2">A clinical cognitive symptom that, when persistent or newly developed more than three months after SARS-CoV-2 infection and after exclusion of other causes, supports diagnosis of neurological Post-COVID Syndrome. | The broader cognitive outcome for which the excerpt notes that detailed longitudinal follow-up data in post-COVID-19 syndrome patients remain limited. | Broad cognitive symptom included in the WHO clinical definition of post COVID-19 condition and described as affecting daily life. | Broad cognitive outcome domain whose possible impairment after COVID-19 is the central focus of the review and meta-analysis. | General cognitive performance domain used as a search term to capture studies evaluating cognitive functioning in patients with persistent COVID-related symptoms. | Broad cognitive-performance domain noted as potentially affected by stress, anxiety disorders, and other psychological problems. | A cognitive outcome explicitly mentioned in Sartori et al. 2012, which discusses the impact of inflammation on cognitive function in older adults. | Broad cognitive impairment outcome associated with COVID-19 in cited neuropsychological studies and described as part of the long-term cognitive repercussions requiring further characterization. | Overall cognitive performance discussed as improving slightly toward normality, although persistent deficits limited broader clinical or quality-of-life gains. | General cognitive performance or functioning, referenced as an outcome or comparator for subjective cognitive complaints in post-COVID syndrome. | Broad cognitive impairment described as a symptom of Long COVID and investigated in relation to task performance and functional brain connectivity. | Cognitive assessment domain evaluated as part of the cognitive and psychological assessment batteries. | Post-infectious cognitive impairment reported after mild COVID-19 and discussed as part of the broader evidence on COVID-19-related brain and cognitive changes. | Broad cognitive outcome assessed in patients after COVID-19 infection in the cited JAMA Network Open article; the excerpt does not specify individual domains or test instruments. | General cognitive-performance construct that the text says can be measured more sensitively and dynamically through detailed time courses of task performance. | Cognitive deficits in multiple sclerosis described in relation to impacts on employment and social functioning. | General cognitive performance construct assessed in the cited multiple-sclerosis neuropsychological literature, including through brief cognitive batteries and orientation tests. | Cognitive function is the broad domain assessed through neuropsychological testing in the study. The excerpt does not specify individual cognitive subdomains or test names. | General cognitive ability discussed in relation to brain volume, with the text noting that brain volume is usually positively correlated with cognitive functioning. | Broad cognitive domain assessed in COVID-19 survivors, with some survivors showing mild cognitive impairment or moderate alteration at follow-up. | Broader cognitive impairment construct discussed in relation to CFQ score; generalized anxiety had the largest standardized association among the reported predictors. | Broad cognitive outcome referenced in the review 'Education and cognitive functioning across the life span.' It represents general cognitive performance rather than a specific neuropsychological test. | Cognitive symptom included in the 38-symptom Long COVID definition used in the survey; reflects broad post-COVID impairment in cognitive functioning. | General cognitive functional domain that may show longer-lasting impairment after ICU treatment. | Broad post-COVID cognitive impairment construct explicitly referenced in the cited study title on cognitive dysfunction, psychiatric distress, and functional decline after COVID-19. | General cognitive abilities supported by hippocampal function. | Broad cognitive outcome referenced in the article title 'Stronger connectivity and higher extraversion protect against stress-related deterioration of cognitive functions.' Specific cognitive subdomains are not identified in the provided text. | General cognitive functioning assessed as part of the Danish Health Study protocol through cognitive tests. The excerpt states that cognitive testing was included but does not name specific cognitive domains or report impairment. | Broad cognitive outcome assessed in patients after COVID-19 infection, as indicated by the Becker et al. JAMA Network Open article title. | A broad impairment-related cognitive outcome referenced in a review of recent evidence in chronic fatigue syndrome. It refers to reduced or abnormal cognitive performance, but the cited reference does not identify a specific neuropsychological test in the provided text. | A broad cognitive outcome mentioned as associated with the inability to identify particular odors in community-dwelling older adults. | General cognitive domain reported to show obvious improvement after transcranial magnetic stimulation in patients with long COVID. | Broad cognitive outcome described as part of central nervous system manifestations in SARS-CoV-2 infection, including problems such as poor concentration and memory impairment. | The broad cognitive ability domain targeted by screening, early intervention, cognitive training, and community management activities. | Cognitive outcomes referenced as key domains affected in MCI and assessed in trials of lifestyle, cognitive, exercise, and technology-based interventions. | Broad cognitive outcome domain reported in previous literature to be significantly affected by mild or non-severe COVID-19 cases. | General cognitive functioning is mentioned in relation to heat-treated Lacticaseibacillus paracasei PS23 enhancing cognition in aging mice. No specific cognitive subdomain, neuropsychological test, effect size, or post-COVID healthcare-worker result is provided. | Overall cognitive performance assessed with validated Chinese versions of standardized neuropsychological tests at baseline and post-intervention to detect change across the trial. | Impaired cognitive performance referenced in an Alzheimer’s disease mouse study assessing probiotic-related prevention of dysfunction. | A cognitive symptom domain explicitly listed among the debilitating manifestations of long COVID and described parenthetically as brain fog. | Broad cognitive outcome described as being worsened in pulmonary patients by chronic hypoxemia, systemic inflammation, oxidative stress, comorbidities, and post-COVID mechanisms. | General cognitive functioning in older adults, described as vulnerable to non-infectious pandemic-related factors such as social isolation, loneliness, loss of social contact, reduced activity, worry, and anxiety. | Broad cognitive outcome referenced in the study title stating that social isolation during COVID-19 lockdown impairs cognitive function. | Neurocognitive abilities that develop across infancy, childhood, and adolescence and may be disrupted by brain injury during these developmental periods. | Overall cognitive profile of each child, presented by the neuropsychologist to therapists before the intervention using baseline assessment data. The presentation identified strengths, weaknesses, and the cognitive abilities to prioritize during rehabilitation. | Cognitive impairment referenced in the cited study of long COVID and myalgic encephalomyelitis/chronic fatigue syndrome, demonstrated using Stroop-task performance and practice effects. | Broad cognitive performance domain reported to correlate with EEG coherence and neural functional connectivity measures. | General cognitive performance assessed in type 2 diabetic patients; the text reports that cognitive scores were associated with metabolic markers and right supramarginal gyrus brain-activity measures. | A broad cognitive domain referenced in the citation title describing repeated assessment of cognitive function in older adults. | General cognitive performance that may be assessed through scalable tools, passive digital biomarkers, and other measurement approaches. | General cognitive ability or performance assessed through study cognitive tasks; no specific cognitive subdomains are named in the provided text. | A broad cognitive impairment construct mentioned in the title of a Parkinson’s disease study assessing cognitive dysfunction using an online tool. | A broad cognitive outcome referenced in the Jaff et al. study title in relation to menopausal symptoms and menopausal stage. | General cognitive performance domain studied in older women in relation to endogenous sex hormones. | A cognitive outcome mentioned in the cited mindfulness-based cognitive therapy article, which reports improvement among individuals with elevated depressive symptoms. | A common cognitive symptom listed in the WHO definition of post-COVID-19 condition; it may affect everyday functioning and may be new after initial recovery or persist from the acute illness. | A broad cognitive outcome targeted by the remote online cognitive training program. The study reports initial evidence that training was feasible and effective in improving cognitive function in schizophrenia. | General cognitive performance assessed at baseline and after training in schizophrenia patient groups. | General cognitive abilities referenced as the target of improvement in schizophrenia-related serious game and cognitive remediation studies. | General cognitive status assessed in older or rural populations in the cited cross-sectional studies. | A broad cognitive impairment construct mentioned in the topic title 'Cognitive Dysfunction Mechanisms in Schizophrenia Models.' | A general cognitive construct mentioned in the topic title 'Cognitive Function and Verbal Fluency in Schizophrenia.' | A broad cognitive-performance construct mentioned across multiple topic titles, including major depressive disorder, psychotic disorders, psychosis risk, and vocational outcomes in schizophrenia. | Listed as a research area for Stephanie Nishi. The text mentions cognitive function in the context of nutritional and chronic disease prevention research, but it does not specify a cognitive subdomain, assessment tool, respiratory infection association, or measured effect. | Cognition was included as a secondary outcome in the trial evaluating protein, resistance exercise, and prebiotic-mediated gut microbiome modulation in older twin participants. | A cognitive outcome domain referenced in the article title on the effects of physical exercise on cognitive functioning and wellbeing. | Broad cognitive outcome that may improve through therapeutic applications of neurofeedback, VR-based training, and memory-focused interventions. | Primary cognitive domain assessed in the clinical study using a cognitive-test battery. | Impaired cognitive function reported in an Alzheimer’s mouse model and linked to neuronal p38α-mediated mechanisms. | Cognitive outcome domain discussed after physiological biomarkers; the available excerpt indicates Forest and Industrial acoustic conditions did not differentially affect cognitive function, though the sentence is truncated. | A cognitive outcome described as potentially improved or supported by exposure to natural environments and ancestral acoustic features. | General cognitive outcome cited in association with indoor dust microbiota in preschool-aged children. | General cognitive performance domains reported as decreased in association with wider retinal venules and related vascular mechanisms. | Cognitive functioning is the outcome domain referenced in prior research and in the study context for which environmental and socio-economic variables were considered as potential confounders or covariates. | A broad cognitive outcome mentioned as being negatively affected by chronic stress and included among the systems strained by chronic stress. | A cognitive outcome/domain explicitly mentioned in the cited work on loneliness and cognitive function. In the broader study context, cognitive function is the domain potentially influenced by psychosocial stressors and extreme environmental conditions. | Cognitive ability or functioning; worse cognitive functioning is reported as consistently associated with higher financial exploitation risk. | Cognitive function is referenced through cited work on defining cognitive function decrement in schizophrenia, indicating relevance as a cognitive outcome domain. | Broad cognitive abilities assessed in neuropsychological testing; represented in the cited CANTAB factor analytic study of normal elderly volunteers. | Cognitive domain represented in the analysis through self-reported memory. | Cognitive performance domain reported to improve with probiotic supplementation in community-dwelling older adults. | A cognitive outcome domain reported as improved in patients with major depression in a cited Lactobacillus plantarum 299v study. | General cognitive performance in the healthy aging population, examined in relation to gut microbiota. | Broad cognitive outcomes disrupted by prefrontal astrocyte activation and linked mechanistically to increased kynurenic acid production. | Overall cognitive performance assessed in participants with recent-onset psychotic disorder, showing heterogeneity across individuals and distinct longitudinal trajectories. | Impaired cognitive functioning identified in one HYDRA-derived patient cluster and described as more closely associated with accelerated brain aging than clinical symptom severity. | General cognitive abilities discussed as potentially related to circadian preference, cortical excitability, brain plasticity, learning, and memory formation. | Cognitive function appears in the article title 'Negative shocks predict change in cognitive function and preferences.' It is presented as a broad cognitive outcome, but the excerpt does not specify particular domains, tests, effect sizes, follow-up intervals, or any association with COVID-19. | A cognitive domain potentially influenced by deviations from normal thyroid hormone levels, thereby affecting mental health status. | Impaired cognitive functioning described as a possible consequence of thyroid hormone imbalance. | A functional outcome reported to improve following MSC-derived exosome treatment after traumatic brain injury. | A cognitive outcome mentioned in the cited UK Biobank study title as being associated with disrupted circadian rhythmicity. It is the primary cognition-related endpoint explicitly present in the excerpt. | The overarching longitudinal cognitive outcome assessed after acute SARS-CoV-2 infection, encompassing attention, working memory, processing speed, executive functioning, language, and memory. | The cognitive domain targeted for profiling and validation in participants with post-COVID-19 condition in the DigiCog Study protocol. | General cognitive outcome domain implied by the use of standard neuropsychological assessment in the DigiCog study. The excerpt does not name specific cognitive subdomains. | A general cognitive impairment outcome mentioned alongside psychiatric distress and functional decline in the truncated citation. | The cognitive outcome assessed during the HCAP substudy. The excerpt refers to overall cognitive abilities or cognitive function rather than naming specific domains such as memory or executive function. | The overall cognitive outcome assessed at baseline and again 3 months after surgery. The study reports objective postoperative decline in this outcome in a subset of participants. | A broader cognitive outcome that the neurofeedback intervention is proposed to enhance in aging individuals and patients at high risk of mild cognitive impairment. | General cognitive performance assessed by neuropsychological instruments, including tools deliverable by telephone. | The overall cognitive outcome that early interventions aim to stabilize or improve during the disease-modification window in mild cognitive impairment. | General cognitive functioning reported in the cited study as associated with timing of menopause and prefrontal cortex aging. | Broad cognitive outcome reported to improve in post-COVID syndrome studies of HBOT. | A main longitudinal outcome domain in PREVENT-AD, represented by baseline and follow-up cognitive data, including 6 additional years of follow-up cognitive data in the second data-acquisition phase. | The excerpt begins mid-word with a reference to a dysfunction mediated by HPA-axis hyperactivity and hippocampal volume reduction. Given the neurobiological mechanisms described, this is treated as cognitive dysfunction, though the exact preceding word is truncated. | A broad cognitive outcome implied by the partially shown reference title mentioning an 'Integrative Cognitive Function Improvement Program.' Although details are incomplete in the provided text, cognitive function is domain-relevant as a general cognitive outcome potentially assessed or targeted in older adults or clinical populations. | Cognitive function is reported to be impaired by both acute total and chronic partial sleep deprivation. | A reported study outcome domain. The text states that large treatment benefits were found for cognitive functioning. | Cognitive outcome examined in relation to sunlight exposure among depressed and non-depressed participants. | Cognitive impairment outcome listed as a major MeSH topic with etiology, rehabilitation, therapy, and virology qualifiers. It is represented as a cognitive outcome/domain because the valid schema does not include a generic disorder/outcome type. | Major MeSH term listed with the qualifier 'etiology,' indicating that cognitive dysfunction is a central cognitive outcome or manifestation considered in relation to COVID-19 complications. | A cognitive impairment outcome repeatedly listed in MeSH with qualifiers including drug therapy, immunology, physiopathology, and virology. Although it is a disorder-level term rather than a single cognitive domain, it is the central cognitive outcome represented in the provided text.</data>
