    <node id="MEMORY">
      <data key="d0">MEMORY</data>
      <data key="d1">COGNITIVE_DOMAIN</data>
      <data key="d2">A cognitive domain evaluated in the study and identified as a major persistent disturbance. Memory deficits were reported at both follow-up assessments. | Cognitive domain described as problematic in the post-acute period after COVID-19, particularly months after hospital discharge and in the context of persistent COVID symptoms. | Cognitive domain reported as altered among individuals with post-acute COVID-19 syndrome assessed with standardized cognitive tools. | Specific cognitive domain reported to be impaired in mild to moderate long COVID. | Cognitive domain reported as affected by some COVID-19 patients as part of persistent post-COVID cognitive complaints. | Specific cognitive domain appearing in the cited study title 'Cognition and memory after Covid-19 in a large community sample.' It is relevant as one of the cognitive functions that may be assessed using digital cognitive tools. | Main cognitive function assessed using Logical Memory and ROCF Recall measures. | A cognitive domain mentioned as one of the signs involved in cognitive failure. | Cognitive domain explicitly described as disrupted in the setting of long COVID and reflected clinically by amnesia. | A cognitive domain referenced in the article title concerning parallel regulation of memory and emotion. | Cognitive domain represented by the Wechsler Memory Scale Logical Memory measure in the cognitive test battery. | Cognitive domain highlighted through self-reported memory symptoms and later amnestic memory deficits during Alzheimer’s disease progression. | Specific cognitive domain described as dysfunctional in long COVID patients and reported to improve after treatment with palmitoylethanolamide plus luteolin. | Specific cognitive domain described as improving alongside strengthened hippocampus-medial prefrontal resting-state connectivity after longitudinal Tai Chi practice. | Cognitive domain assessed by Broadbent's CFQ through self-reported failures in memory and described as part of the stable cognitive resource reflected by CFQ scores. | Specific cognitive domain evaluated in patients with long COVID, including in studies assessing palmitoylethanolamide and luteolin effects on olfaction and memory. | Cognitive domain specifically identified as vulnerable to chronic hypoxemia-related impairment in pulmonary disease. | Specific cognitive domain highlighted as showing particularly steep decline in longitudinal studies of older adults during the pandemic period. | Secondary cognitive domain assessed before and after the 12-week rehabilitation intervention. | A cognitive domain mentioned as one of the higher cognitive functions associated with the precuneus. | A cognitive domain identified in the cited review as showing deficits and potential recovery in chronic alcoholism. | A cognitive domain relevant to the MoCA and its memory-related tasks, particularly in the context of detecting mild cognitive impairment. | A cognitive process explicitly identified as suitable for regular monitoring using gamified cognitive tasks, potentially supported by at-home EEG recording. | Specific cognitive domain referenced in the cited study on cognition and memory after COVID-19 in a large community sample. | A cognitive domain in which underperformance contributed to the global accuracy deficits detected by the online cognitive assessment in Parkinson’s disease and REM sleep behavioural disorder populations. | A cognitive domain referenced through everyday memory lapses, misplacing items, and subjective memory complaints. | School-age cognitive domain associated with white matter development changes. | A cognitive domain described as usually the first affected when cognitive decline becomes evident on formal testing, particularly in amnestic mild cognitive impairment. | A cognitive domain explicitly included among the abilities exercised during the Komori Life-based training intervention. | A cognitive domain mentioned as related to the dorsomedial thalamic nucleus and limbic circuitry. | A cognitive domain relevant to neuropsychological assessment and represented by the Wechsler Memory Scale in the text. | Cognitive domain explicitly mentioned as being affected by chronic stress. | A cognitive domain mentioned as being related to brain regions affected by childhood emotional maltreatment-associated structural and functional alterations. | Cognitive domain assessed by the paired associates learning task. The excerpt reports that the prebiotic group made fewer errors than the placebo group on this memory-related test. | A cognitive domain mentioned alongside verbal learning as showing stress-related deficits associated with prefrontal cortical volume loss. | A fundamental cognitive function that enables animals to store and retrieve information about past experiences and use that information adaptively. | One of the six domains scored by the CDR-SB. | A cognitive domain described as overlapping with imagination and future prediction through shared predictive neural systems. | Internal and external memory strategies were suggested as possible interventions for tracking prior negative incidents or exploitation attempts involving certain individuals. | A cognitive domain explicitly mentioned in the title 'Selective reminding for analysis of memory and learning.' | A cognitive domain discussed through subjective memory ratings and word-recall performance. The text indicates that many middle-aged and older adults perceived their memory as poor and performed below the ideal score on recall tasks. | A cognitive domain associated with human cortico-hippocampal connections in the described stimulation experiments. | A cognitive domain listed as supporting studying and learning materials. The excerpt indicates that memory-dependent educational activities may be hindered at circadian non-preferred times. | Cognitive domain referenced through high-fat diet-dependent memory impairment that may be reversed by neural stem cell-derived exosomes. | A cognitive domain assessed using validated in-person measures. The findings specifically mention improvement in verbal learning and memory through 42 months post-COVID. | Specific cognitive function reported as impaired in the cognitive profile of Post-COVID-19 Condition. | Domain-specific cognitive score assessed at HCAP. Lower respiratory tract infections were specifically associated with poorer memory performance. | Cognitive domain related to retention and recall of verbal information, assessed with Craft Story 21 Recall and the Rey Auditory Verbal Learning Test. | Cognitive domain assessed in the infected participants; the excerpt reports no signs of memory impairment and no functional alteration in memory-related regions. | A cognitive domain discussed as a potential target of neurofeedback-based brain training, with reported potential for enhancement and delayed cognitive decline. | A cognitive domain reported as improved in preliminary human tPBM studies and in AD mouse models following repeated tPBM. | Cognitive domain referenced in the Women's Health Initiative Memory study citation. | Cognitive domain assessed in both WRAP and BrANCH using composite/domain-specific scores calculated by averaging sample-based z-scores from cognitive or neuropsychological tests. | A cognitive domain assessed by the Montreal Cognitive Assessment as part of screening for cognitive impairment. | A cognitive domain listed as part of Everyday Cognition. | Memory is listed among the functions negatively affected by poor sleep quality and sleep deprivation. | Memory was evaluated as a cognitive domain in the post-COVID-19 registry study. The abstract did not report a significant association between prior COVID-19 and memory impairment.</data>
