    <node id="PROCESSING SPEED">
      <data key="d0">PROCESSING SPEED</data>
      <data key="d1">COGNITIVE_DOMAIN</data>
      <data key="d2">Cognitive domain reflecting the speed of mental operations; listed among objective cognitive deficits in hospitalized patients recovering from COVID-19. | A cognitive domain commonly assessed by SDMT and TMT-A, represented among the neuropsychological variables discussed in the principal component analysis context. | Specific cognitive domain reported to be impaired in patients with mild to moderate long COVID. | Cognitive domain reflected by timed or speeded performance, especially Trail Making, where slowing was explicitly reported. | Cognitive domain assessed in hospitalized COVID-19 patients; the passage reports no processing-speed worsening in the cited multimodal study. | Cognitive domain referring to the speed of performing cognitive operations. The fragment mentions processing speed in the context of distinguishing it from motor-speed influence. | Specific cognitive domain highlighted as being affected in prior literature on mild or non-severe COVID-19. | Cognitive performance domain commonly reflected by speeded neuropsychological measures such as Coding and components of Color Trails testing; included to type the role of Coding more accurately. | Specific cognitive domain reported to be lower in general-population studies using online cognitive test batteries during the COVID-19 pandemic period compared with pre-pandemic and early-pandemic levels. | Processing speed is one of the cognitive domains probed by the Test My Brain Digital Neuropsychology Toolkit. | Cognitive domain involving rapid visual scanning, sequencing, and speeded responding. It is particularly relevant to Trail Making, where participants must click items as quickly as possible. | A cognitive domain explicitly identified in the text as being assessed by pre-specified tests within the Neuropsychological Test Battery. | A cognitive domain assessed using the BACS, Symbol Digit Modalities Test, and Trail Making Tests A and B, reflecting the speed of cognitive operations. | A performance-related cognitive domain reflected by reaction time. The excerpt states that reaction time was recorded for AX-CPT target trials. | A specific cognitive domain measured in the post-COVID cohort. Processing speed scores were analyzed longitudinally and improved progressively through 42 months post-COVID. | Cognitive domain reported as impaired after COVID-19 infection and within Post-COVID-19 Condition. | A cognitive domain commonly assessed by timed or symbol-substitution tasks such as the Symbol Digit Modalities Test and Trail Making Test A. | Cognitive domain reflecting speed of mental operations, assessed in both executive-function/processing-speed and attention/processing-speed task groupings. | Cognitive domain reflected by the numeric Trail Making Test Part A task. Increased completion time indicates slower processing speed or worse speeded visual-scanning performance. | A cognitive domain included in the primary endpoint of the tPBM feasibility trial, assessed for change from baseline to post-intervention week 7. | A cognitive domain objectively assessed during functional testing. It significantly improved after HBOT when reassessed 4 weeks post-treatment. | Processing speed was evaluated as a cognitive domain potentially affected after COVID-19. The findings did not report a significant COVID-19 association for this domain. | A cognitive domain objectively reported as impaired in post-acute COVID-19 syndrome, involving the speed of mental operations and information processing. | A cognitive domain assessed in the study. HIV-infected smokers performed significantly worse than HIV-uninfected smokers on processing speed.</data>
      <data key="d3">1.0</data>
      <data key="d4">4b18c63f-2f87-46c1-a3ae-8c4337490271_chunk_27</data>
