    <node id="NINE MONTHS AFTER INFECTION">
      <data key="d0">NINE MONTHS AFTER INFECTION</data>
      <data key="d1">FOLLOW_UP_TIMEPOINT</data>
      <data key="d2">Post-infection follow-up timepoint at which hyposmia was equally common among cases and controls. | Post-infection follow-up interval at which some smell and taste test results improved, with hyposmia reported to normalize. Selective anosmia and selective ageusia did not improve at this interval. | Follow-up interval during which a range of post-vaccination SARS-CoV-2 infection manifestations were evaluated, including myocarditis, myalgia, cerebral hemorrhage, anosmia, and interstitial lung disease. | Follow-up timepoint at which an international long-COVID survey reported persistent gastrointestinal symptoms, including diarrhea and loss of appetite. | Follow-up point at which CT abnormalities including subpleural bands and ground-glass opacity are reported after COVID-19 infection. | Follow-up point at which fibrosis without obvious ground-glass opacities is reported after COVID-19 infection. | Longer-term post-infection point indicating persistence of neuropsychiatric symptoms beyond the acute infection period. | Timepoint after SARS-CoV-2 infection used in the World Health Organization definition of long COVID or PASC. | Post-infection interval at which 26% of people in one cohort still had moderate to severe symptoms. | Post-infection interval in the healthcare-worker cohort at which 15% developed moderate to severe symptoms and functional impairment was assessed. | Post-infection timepoint at which some patients showed radiographic abnormalities and abnormal lung function. | Later post-infection timepoint at which some patients had persistent CT changes suggestive of fibrosis. | Follow-up timepoint at which 47 of 1655 recovered COVID-19 patients, approximately 3%, were reported to have developed a rash. | Follow-up timepoint in the Australian primary-care surveillance study at which 27% of participants were unrecovered. | Follow-up timepoint in the Australian primary-care surveillance study at which 12% of participants were unrecovered and 11% met ME/CFS criteria.</data>
      <data key="d3">0.85</data>
