      <data key="d0">SEVERE ACUTE RESPIRATORY SYNDROME</data>
      <data key="d1">RESPIRATORY_INFECTION</data>
      <data key="d2">Respiratory syndrome term included in the infection-related search block to broaden retrieval of coronavirus-associated human studies. | Pre-COVID-19 coronavirus infection cited as part of evidence on post-recovery neuropsychiatric morbidity during follow-up periods after coronavirus illness. | Earlier coronavirus-related respiratory syndrome cited as precedent for neurological or neuromuscular complications, supporting concern about persistent neuropsychiatric symptoms after COVID-19. | Included as a respiratory-infection-related search term in the systematic review strategy, alongside COVID, coronavirus, and pandemic terms. It is not described as a separately analyzed exposure in the provided passage. | Severe acute respiratory syndrome, referenced through chronic post-SARS syndrome. The cited post-SARS syndrome study reported chronic widespread musculoskeletal pain, fatigue, depression, and disordered sleep, but the excerpt does not provide measured cognitive outcomes. | Severe acute respiratory syndrome, used as a comparator coronavirus infection. SARS survivors are described as having long-term reductions in health status and exercise capacity, impaired lung diffusing capacity, pulmonary interstitial damage, and functional decline. | Prior respiratory infection referenced as evidence that pulmonary injury after severe viral respiratory illness may persist for months to years. | Respiratory infection affecting a cohort of healthcare workers followed for up to 15 years; some individuals continued to show lung CT and lung function abnormalities long after infection. | Respiratory infection referenced through a cited 15-year prospective cohort study describing long-term bone and lung consequences after hospital-acquired SARS. It is used as background for speculating about possible long-term lung damage after COVID-19. | Respiratory infectious disease mentioned in the cited systematic review title as a comparator context for psychological impacts alongside COVID-19. The excerpt itself does not provide SARS-specific cognitive or prevalence results. | Respiratory pandemic infection referenced through survivor research showing long-lasting psychological effects; used as a comparator context for COVID-19-related psychological outcomes. | Mentioned only in a cited reference title as the SARS epidemic, used as a source of lessons for post-traumatic stress disorder prevention after COVID-19. No direct cognitive-outcome evidence, cohort characteristics, severity information, or follow-up data are provided. | Respiratory infection referenced through the SARS epidemic as an example of a prior large-scale stressful event. The text notes prior work associating such stressors with alcohol misuse and problems, without reporting cognitive outcomes. | Severe acute respiratory syndrome from the 2002–2004 epidemic. The passage describes post-acute effects after SARS, including fatigue and neuropsychiatric features such as sleep problems, irritability, depression, anxiety, and memory impairment. | SARS, a severe coronavirus respiratory infection, is referenced in long-term survivor studies reporting mental morbidities, chronic fatigue, musculoskeletal pain, depression, and disordered sleep. | Respiratory infection referenced through a cited quarantine study after potential SARS exposure. The cited study is used to support the point that marital status was not a relevant predictor of mental health status. | Respiratory infection referenced in relation to Kin On Kwok's early work examining nosocomial transmission pathways through mathematical modelling. The text does not link SARS to cognitive domains, neuropsychological testing, or quantified post-infection effects. | Respiratory infection mentioned as a comparator to SARS-CoV-2 and MERS in an article title about molecular feature similarities.</data>
