      <data key="d2">Respiratory infection defining the study case group. The excerpt reports 1706 Covid cases and compares them with 14,398 controls, including historical and contemporary controls. No cognitive domains, neuropsychological tests, effect sizes, disease-severity strata, or specific follow-up cognitive outcomes are reported in this excerpt. | Respiratory infectious disease referenced as the context for prior pandemic-related experience and a previously developed COVID-related olfactory test. The passage does not report cognitive outcomes, disease severity, follow-up cognitive effects, or healthcare-worker-specific findings for COVID. | Acute SARS-CoV-2-related respiratory infection referenced in patients treated with oxygen-ozone autohemotherapy for fatigue and pain. | Respiratory infection context for the study assessments. The text describes COVID-related acute and prolonged symptoms assessed by self-report questionnaire. | Respiratory infectious disease referenced in a COVID Symptom Study Biobank prospective cohort study citation. The provided excerpt does not specify severity categories, occupational cohort details, cognitive outcomes, or effect sizes for COVID. | COVID is mentioned only in the title of a cited reference about risk and resilience factors for mental health in a German representative panel study. The excerpt itself does not report COVID infection status, severity, cognitive outcomes, neuropsychological testing, healthcare-worker data, mechanisms, or effect sizes. | Mentioned only as contextual background in the phrase 'post-COVID research landscape' to justify interest in remote trial designs; no COVID-related outcomes, severity, cohorts, or follow-up assessments are described. | Respiratory infection context for the Predi-COVID cohort and DigiCog study. Participant symptom status during and after COVID infection is used to guide preliminary group allocation. | COVID is referenced through post-COVID ME/CFS and post-COVID syndrome, which provide the clinical context for the cohort. The excerpt does not directly report COVID-related cognitive outcomes, neuropsychological testing, healthcare-worker exposure, or effect sizes. | Respiratory infection referenced in the cited article title. The title states that mild respiratory COVID can cause neural-cell and myelin dysregulation, but the provided text does not report cohort characteristics, cognitive outcomes, or quantitative effect estimates. | COVID is mentioned only as an exclusion criterion for NEJM clinical case records, where cases related to COVID were filtered out. The passage does not provide information about COVID severity, cognitive outcomes, follow-up timepoints, mechanisms, confounders, occupational exposure, or neuropsychological testing.</data>
