      <data key="d1">RESPIRATORY_INFECTION</data>
      <data key="d2">Post-acute persistent manifestations following COVID-19. Although long COVID is not itself an acute respiratory infection, it is retained under the closest available valid type because the text defines it as persistent manifestations after COVID-19 and no post-viral syndrome entity type is available. | Post-acute sequelae of COVID-19 referenced in relation to elevated vascular transformation blood biomarkers and angiogenesis as a possible pathophysiological mechanism. | Post-acute condition described as persistent COVID-19 symptoms in survivors managed in Lagos State, Nigeria. The excerpt does not specify cognitive symptoms, cognitive domains, or neuropsychological testing within Long COVID. | Post-acute condition following SARS-CoV-2 infection, discussed in relation to neurological symptoms and reduced brain metabolism on PET imaging. | Long-COVID is cited as an evolving problem with extensive impact. The passage does not specify symptoms, cognitive domains, neuropsychological tests, effect sizes, or follow-up intervals, but Long-COVID is relevant as a post-acute condition associated with SARS-CoV-2 infection. | Post-acute condition following SARS-CoV-2 infection, described as symptoms persisting beyond the acute phase. The study used the WHO definition of post-COVID-19 condition and evaluated cognitive, symptom, and biomarker outcomes in affected individuals. | A post-acute condition following SARS-CoV-2 infection. In the cited WHO Delphi consensus definition, it involves continuation or development of new symptoms 3 months after initial infection, lasting for at least 2 months with no other explanation. | Post-acute condition following COVID-19 infection, cited as associated with increased absenteeism from work and impacts on school experiences. No specific cognitive-domain outcomes, neuropsychological measures, follow-up timepoints, or effect estimates are provided in the excerpt. | Post-acute condition following SARS-CoV-2 infection, cited in relation to cognitive dysfunction demonstrated with Stroop-task performance and practice effects. | Post-acute condition following COVID-19, described here by persistent symptom burden after at least 3 months of follow-up, with respiratory and neuropsychological symptom patterns. | Post-acute condition following probable or confirmed SARS-CoV-2 infection, defined in the passage using the WHO definition as symptoms lasting for at least 2 months and not explained by an alternative diagnosis. | Post-acute condition following COVID-19 infection, referenced through a cited multicentre study reporting an association with severe cognitive slowing. | Listed as one of Dr. Esther Melamed's research areas and connected to her role as Director of Research for the Post-COVID Program. Although Long COVID is a post-acute sequela of SARS-CoV-2 infection rather than an acute respiratory infection, RESPIRATORY_INFECTION is the closest valid entity type available. The text does not specify cognitive domains, neuropsychological tests, disease severity, effect sizes, mechanisms, confounders, exposures, cohorts, or follow-up timepoints. | Post-acute or persistent syndrome following COVID-19, cited in connection with mitochondrial dysfunction, microglial involvement, and persistent neuroinflammation. | Post-acute condition following COVID-19, framed in the text as carrying a significant and lasting cognitive health burden. | Post-acute condition following COVID-19, cited through a systematic review of prevalence and long-term health effects among hospitalized and non-hospitalized populations. Within the available schema it is retained as part of the COVID-19 respiratory infection context, although it represents a post-infectious syndrome rather than acute infection. | Post-acute condition following SARS-CoV-2 infection, mentioned in the title of a 2024 clinical review on hyperbaric oxygen and long COVID pathophysiology. The excerpt does not provide cognitive outcomes, disease severity strata, follow-up timing, or cohort details. | Post-acute COVID-19-related condition explicitly referenced in connection with cognitive impairment, pathological mechanisms, and rehabilitation interventions. | Post-acute sequelae of SARS-CoV-2 infection referenced in studies of neurocognitive trajectories and neurologic manifestations. Although it is a post-infectious syndrome rather than an acute infection, it is retained under the respiratory infection domain because it follows COVID-19. | Post-acute condition following COVID-19 infection, referenced in review articles concerning children, adults, vulnerable populations, and gut microbial signatures as potential biomarkers or therapeutic targets.</data>
