    <node id="PSYCHOLOGICAL FACTORS">
      <data key="d0">PSYCHOLOGICAL FACTORS</data>
      <data key="d1">CONFOUNDING_FACTOR</data>
      <data key="d2">Suggested non-ovarian explanatory factor for menstrual alterations following SARS-CoV-2 infection. | Psychological variables that the study planned to examine in association with post-COVID outcomes, including PTSD and post-traumatic growth. | Psychological characteristics reported as associated with fatigue in persons with multiple sclerosis and potentially relevant when interpreting fatigue-related outcomes. | Non-cognitive contextual factors mentioned as having differential associations with observed cognitive impairments. A dominant general intelligence factor can make such associations harder to interpret at the process level. | Non-biological factors listed as possible contributors to long COVID and relevant to interpreting persistent subjective symptoms, including neuropsychological complaints. | Broad set of psychological and social variables examined for their relationship with financial exploitation and for interactions with sociodemographic and health factors. | Mentioned as one class of factors related to sleep-change profiles during the COVID-19 pandemic. Psychological factors may confound associations involving sleep, infection exposure, and cognition. | Mental health or psychological variables collected alongside cognitive assessments because they may affect cognitive functioning or perceived cognitive symptoms. | Psychosocial factors are mentioned in relation to sleep quality among prison police officers, but the cited study was excluded due to the wrong patient population.</data>
