      <data key="d7">CHILDHOOD EMOTIONAL MALTREATMENT (CEM)</data>
      <data key="d8">HEALTHY DIETARY BEHAVIOR</data>
      <data key="d9">AFFECTS</data>
      <data key="d10">The text proposes that CEM is associated with healthy dietary behavior. | Hypothesis H2 states that emotional distress may mediate the relationship between CEM and healthy dietary behavior. | CEM is proposed to be negatively associated with healthy dietary behavior. | CEM was significantly negatively correlated with healthy dietary behavior. | The primary model interprets the association between CEM and healthy dietary behavior as mediated through emotional distress and subjective well-being. | Higher CEM was significantly associated with poorer healthy dietary behavior in the final model. | The text indicates indirect pathways through emotional distress variables and SWB linking CEM with healthy dietary behavior. | The relationship between CEM and healthy dietary behavior was partially mediated by depression, anxiety, and SWB.</data>
      <data key="d11">0.95</data>
      <data key="d12">1</data>
