 provide the direction, magnitude, or specific affected cognitive domains. | The cited study title indicates that cognitive function was assessed in patients after COVID-19 infection, supporting COVID-19 as the relevant antecedent condition for post-infection cognitive outcomes. | The COVID-19/PASC follow-up context is described as involving nervous-system effects, including cognitive dysfunction among central nervous system manifestations. | The text references cognitive dysfunction after COVID-19, indicating an association between the antecedent infection and later cognitive symptoms. | A cited study explicitly refers to cognitive dysfunction after COVID-19, indicating post-infectious cognitive effects following the acute illness. | The first peak of the pandemic was concluded to have decreased certain aspects of cognitive functioning to a limited extent. | The text states that the pandemic may have had sustained effects on cognitive functioning, particularly in older adults, although no quantified cognitive deficit is reported. | The passage describes lower cognitive functioning during the COVID-19 pandemic period in the general population. This reflects an association with the pandemic context rather than a confirmed infection-specific causal effect. | The COVID-19 pandemic context was associated with cognitive functioning changes, although the excerpt attributes these mainly to pandemic-related psychosocial factors rather than confirmed infection effects. | The excerpt references a prior survey study examining the impact of the COVID-19 pandemic on wellbeing and cognitive functioning in older adults. This reflects pandemic-context effects rather than confirmed individual infection effects. | The excerpt frames the COVID-19 pandemic as the exposure context whose impact on cognitive functioning in older adults was being investigated. | The cited titles associate the COVID-19 pandemic period with changes in cognitive function. This is title-level evidence and should be interpreted as pandemic-context association rather than proof of direct effects from confirmed SARS-CoV-2 infection. | The cited article title reports cognitive deficits in people who have recovered from COVID-19, supporting a broad association between COVID-19 and impaired cognitive function. | COVID-19 is linked to later cognitive dysfunction as part of post-COVID-19 condition; symptoms may arise after initial recovery or persist from the acute illness. | The study addresses cognitive profiling in post-COVID-19 condition, implying that cognitive functioning is a relevant affected domain after COVID-19. | The truncated citation mentions cognitive dysfunction in the context of post-COVID outcomes, indicating cognitive dysfunction as a relevant post-infectious endpoint. | A cited article explicitly addresses cognitive dysfunction after COVID-19, supporting an association between infection and later cognitive impairment. | The MeSH headings jointly index COVID-19 complications and cognitive dysfunction etiology/virology, supporting an association in which COVID-19 is linked to cognitive dysfunction. No magnitude, timing, severity gradient, or measurement instrument is specified in the excerpt. | The MeSH terms pair COVID-19 complications with cognitive dysfunction etiology, supporting an association between COVID-19 and cognitive dysfunction as a complication or sequela.</data>
