upporting an association between prior infection and cognitive impairment. | COVID-19 is linked to cognitive deficits in people who recovered from infection, with the cited online study comparing recovered individuals with controls. | Post-COVID individuals are described as experiencing cognitive deficits, consistent with cited literature on cognitive complaints and neuropsychological deficits after COVID-19. | Cited studies describe cognitive deficits in COVID-19 cohorts, including hospitalized patients and individuals assessed in long-term follow-up. | COVID-19 is associated with cognitive deficits examined in the cited hospitalized cohort study. | The cited article title explicitly links recovery from COVID-19 with cognitive deficits, indicating an association between the respiratory infection and impaired cognition. | COVID-19 is discussed as being associated with cognitive deficits, including deficits predicted by altered PET-FDG metabolic patterns. | The cited Hampshire et al. article title indicates cognitive deficits in people who have recovered, consistent with an association between COVID-19 and post-acute cognitive deficits, although the excerpt is truncated. | The cited article title explicitly links COVID-19 with cognitive deficits, indicating cognitive impairment among individuals recovering after respiratory assistance. | The cited title explicitly links COVID-19 with cognitive deficits after respiratory assistance, indicating an association between infection and impaired cognitive outcomes. | Related article titles refer to cognitive deficits following COVID-19, including posthospitalization deficits and cognitive deficits targeted by intervention. | The cited article title 'Cognitive deficits in long Covid-19' supports an association between COVID-19-related post-acute illness and cognitive impairment, although no specific cognitive domain or effect size is provided. | COVID-19 survivor status was associated with later cognitive deficits, which were