      <data key="d10">The COVID-19 pandemic context is associated with stress, which is discussed as relevant to cognitive functioning during a pandemic. | The cited literature frames stress as occurring in the time of COVID-19. This relationship reflects contextual association rather than a measured cognitive effect. | The physician survey examines stress during the COVID-19 outbreak in the Iraqi Kurdistan Region. | The cited Iraqi Kurdistan online survey concerns stress among physicians during the COVID-19 outbreak. The citation supports a pandemic-context association, but not a quantified causal effect or cognitive consequence. | Stress is described as an indirect psychosocial consequence of pandemic conditions and may complicate interpretation of health effects attributed to COVID-19 infection itself. | Stress is reported in hospitalized patients with COVID-19 and may act as a confounding psychological factor in studies assessing cognition after infection.</data>
