The citation does not establish a quantified causal effect or link depression to cognitive testing. | Depression is described as associated with pandemic restrictions and reduced social interaction, representing a contextual mental-health factor separate from direct COVID-19 illness. | Depression is discussed in relation to post-COVID-19 syndrome, indicating that COVID-19 and its aftermath are associated with depressive symptoms. | The excerpt cites literature on depression in COVID-19 survivors and hospitalized COVID-19 patients. | The cited studies discuss depression in COVID-19 survivors and in post-COVID-19 syndrome, supporting depression as a post-infectious psychiatric outcome. | Depression is reported in hospitalized patients with COVID-19 and is a relevant potential confounder for interpreting cognitive or neuropsychological findings in COVID-19 populations. | Cited references discuss expected effects of the COVID-19 outbreak on depression incidence in Italy and broader pandemic-related impacts on mental health. | The references include studies on the impact of the COVID-19 pandemic and quarantine on mental health, including depression-related outcomes. | Depression is not presented as a cognitive outcome in the text, but it is a relevant comorbid psychological factor that could confound any downstream analysis of COVID-19-related cognitive complaints or deficits. | A cited meta-analysis examines depression prevalence during the COVID-19 outbreak, indicating an association between the pandemic context and depression. | The adjusted post-COVID cognitive trajectory analysis controlled for depression.</data>
