      <data key="d10">The 10-month study evaluates cognitive features in COVID-19 survivors, indicating investigation of post-COVID cognitive effects. The excerpt does not provide detailed results. | The study addresses cognitive failure after COVID-19 and concludes that it is a multifactorial post-COVID outcome. | The passage identifies cognitive failure after COVID-19 as one of the main long-term issues requiring attention. | The passage frames cognitive failure as a potential long-term outcome in people with COVID-19, while noting that effects of vaccination and booster regimens remain unresolved. | The study investigates the precise impact of COVID-19 on subsequent cognitive failure in a longitudinal Indonesian post-COVID-19 cohort. | The study examines cognitive failure in the context of residual and long-term consequences after COVID-19, while also considering other symptoms that may contribute. | The excerpt frames cognitive failure as a cognitive outcome of interest after COVID-19, although it does not report a directly measured COVID-19-specific cognitive deficit estimate. | The proposed model examines cognitive failure after COVID-19 and assumes that COVID-19 may contribute to later cognitive failure. | The model is explicitly framed as a model of cognitive failure after COVID-19, indicating that COVID-19 is the infection context associated with subsequent cognitive dysfunction. | Cognitive failure was studied in the post-COVID-19 period, making COVID-19 infection the clinical context for the later cognitive outcome. | The study discussion links post-COVID status with cognitive failure, noting that cognitive failure after 6 months should not be underestimated. | COVID-19 is discussed as the clinical context in which cognitive failure was evaluated, with post-COVID symptoms and severity-related factors considered as contributors. | COVID-19 is associated with subsequent cognitive failure, although the passage emphasizes that the relationship is multifactorial and not solely explained by disease severity. | The passage refers to studies evaluating whether contracting COVID-19 affects subjective cognitive failures, including post-COVID-19 cognitive symptoms and cognitive failures at work. | COVID-19 is presented as the antecedent condition for post-infection symptoms that include cognitive failure, motivating the use of CFQ in the post-COVID-19 setting. | The text discusses cognitive failure occurring after COVID-19 among survivors, while clarifying that the outcome is not solely caused by COVID-19. | The title frames cognitive failure as occurring after COVID-19, indicating a studied association between COVID-19 infection and subsequent cognitive problems. | The study framework links COVID-19 to assessment of self-reported cognitive failures, as COVID-19 patients completed the CFQ at T0. The excerpt supports an assessed association rather than a demonstrated causal effect. | The study context links COVID-19 with post-infection assessment of cognitive failures, indicating cognitive functioning was considered a relevant post-COVID outcome in reassessed patients. | Within the COVID-19 cohort, a subset of patients presented cognitive failure as defined by elevated CFQ score. This relationship describes occurrence in the studied COVID-19 population rather than proving causality. | The passage reports that eleven COVID-19 patients complained of cognitive failures at T0, supporting an association between COVID-19 and subjective cognitive problems. | The authors' study reports cognitive failure immediately following recovery from COVID-19. | COVID-19 is associated with cognitive failure, reflected by higher CFQ scores among a greater number of COVID-19 patients at ten months. | The passage connects post-COVID cognitive problems with cognitive failures that may impair daily and occupational functioning. | The cited article title states that contracting COVID-19 affects cognitive failures at work.</data>
