      <data key="d10">Perceived stress is associated with anxiety/depression symptoms and distress during the COVID-19 pandemic and may confound interpretation of COVID-19-related psychological or cognitive associations. | Perceived stress is a pandemic-period psychological factor that can confound interpretation of COVID-19-related affective or cognitive outcomes, particularly when infection-specific effects are not separated from pandemic stress exposure. | Perceived stress was negatively related to mental health during COVID-19 lockdown and differed across trajectory subgroups, making it an important covariate for interpreting outcomes. | The passage frames perceived stress as varying in the context of COVID-19-related mental health trajectories. | The COVID-19 pandemic context and related regulations are associated with changes in perceived stress, including reduction in the recovered group and exacerbation in the vulnerable group after restrictions relaxed. | The passage states that fear or worry about the effects of the COVID-19 outbreak can increase perceived stress, including stress and distress. | The text references stress and perceived stress in patients hospitalized with COVID-19, indicating a link between COVID-19 illness context and perceived stress. | Perceived stress is reported in the COVID-19 hospitalization context and is relevant as a subjective psychological factor that can influence cognitive complaints and performance.</data>
