      <data key="d9">COMPARED_TO</data>
      <data key="d10">The figure compares counts of PCEs and ACEs to identify whether protective or adverse experiences dominate, neutralize each other, or interact. | The analysis compared the influence of PCEs and ACEs, noting that PCEs appeared to overcome ACEs for some outcomes such as anger control, whereas ACEs appeared to dominate for others such as anxiety. | PCEs appeared to offset the adverse impact of ACEs, especially among individuals with higher ACE exposure; as few as 3 PCEs could offset the negative effect of 9 ACEs in the heatmap. | The protective effect of an average PCE was compared with the detrimental effect of an average ACE across total and specific mood challenges. | The protective association of PCEs varied depending on ACE burden; PCE benefits were diminished for anxiety and depression at high ACE levels but remained protective for Total Mood Challenge. | The protective or compensatory association of PCEs was moderated by ACE levels: PCE benefits were stronger at lower ACE levels and attenuated at higher ACE levels.</data>
      <data key="d11">0.9</data>
