    <edge source="SARS-COV-2 INFECTION" target="CONCENTRATION">
      <data key="d7">SARS-COV-2 INFECTION</data>
      <data key="d8">CONCENTRATION</data>
      <data key="d9">AFFECTS</data>
      <data key="d10">After mild to moderate SARS-CoV-2 infection, patients with Post-COVID syndrome reported persistent concentration deficits, which were among the main disturbances at both follow-up assessments.</data>
      <data key="d11">0.96</data>
      <data key="d12">1</data>
      <data key="d13">86f9ab26-f1be-4c98-8916-d978196e27f5_chunk_0</data>
      <data key="d14">86f9ab26-f1be-4c98-8916-d978196e27f5</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="SARS-COV-2 INFECTION" target="MEMORY">
      <data key="d7">SARS-COV-2 INFECTION</data>
      <data key="d8">MEMORY</data>
      <data key="d9">AFFECTS</data>
      <data key="d10">SARS-CoV-2 infection was followed by persistent memory deficits in the Post-COVID syndrome cohort, with memory problems reported at both follow-up time points. | The title 'Changes in memory and cognition during the SARS-CoV-2 human challenge study' indicates that memory changed or was assessed in relation to SARS-CoV-2 infection or exposure. | Subtle memory changes are described as cognitive deficits that may occur following SARS-CoV-2 infection. | Prior SARS-CoV-2 infection is associated with memory-related outcomes in cases compared with controls, though the text explicitly states that the causal nature of this relationship is unclear. | Memory difficulties are reported as part of the cognitive profile following SARS-CoV-2 infection. | The study concerns possible infection-related cognitive consequences, including memory. In this passage, however, memory-related regions did not show functional alteration or post hoc association with selected cognitive tests, so evidence for a direct memory effect here is weak and unresolved. | The text suggests that infection-associated limbic abnormalities may prefigure future vulnerability of the limbic system, including memory, but explicitly states that this remains to be investigated.</data>
      <data key="d11">0.96</data>
      <data key="d12">1</data>
      <data key="d13">86f9ab26-f1be-4c98-8916-d978196e27f5_chunk_0</data>
      <data key="d14">86f9ab26-f1be-4c98-8916-d978196e27f5,e83b96c5-4b27-4c65-81de-ecaece6f2395,41fb90c0-1594-48bd-b974-39b7d20799d4,fe2c70b3-e143-43f7-bbf4-4cdc081212c3,f40f9b83-7586-4b71-869a-2ceb88840fbe</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="SARS-COV-2 INFECTION" target="ATTENTION">
      <data key="d7">SARS-COV-2 INFECTION</data>
      <data key="d8">ATTENTION</data>
      <data key="d9">AFFECTS</data>
      <data key="d10">Attention was assessed as part of the post-infection cognitive test battery. The abstract reports persistent cognitive impairment, although concentration and memory were emphasized more strongly than attention. | Attention difficulties are listed among cognitive problems following SARS-CoV-2 infection.</data>
      <data key="d11">0.9</data>
      <data key="d12">1</data>
      <data key="d13">86f9ab26-f1be-4c98-8916-d978196e27f5_chunk_0</data>
      <data key="d14">86f9ab26-f1be-4c98-8916-d978196e27f5,fe2c70b3-e143-43f7-bbf4-4cdc081212c3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="SARS-COV-2 INFECTION" target="FATIGUE">
      <data key="d7">SARS-COV-2 INFECTION</data>
      <data key="d8">FATIGUE</data>
      <data key="d9">AFFECTS</data>
      <data key="d10">Fatigue was one of the main persistent symptoms reported by patients with Post-COVID syndrome after SARS-CoV-2 infection. | Fatigue is described as a persistent symptom occurring after SARS-CoV-2 infection. | Fatigue was assessed because it may affect cognition or symptom reporting and can complicate interpretation of post-infection outcomes.</data>
      <data key="d11">0.94</data>
      <data key="d12">1</data>
      <data key="d13">86f9ab26-f1be-4c98-8916-d978196e27f5_chunk_0</data>
      <data key="d14">86f9ab26-f1be-4c98-8916-d978196e27f5,41fb90c0-1594-48bd-b974-39b7d20799d4</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="SARS-COV-2 INFECTION" target="COGNITIVE IMPAIRMENT">
      <data key="d7">SARS-COV-2 INFECTION</data>
      <data key="d8">COGNITIVE IMPAIRMENT</data>
      <data key="d9">AFFECTS</data>
      <data key="d10">The text states that significant cognitive impairment persisted after SARS-CoV-2 infection, indicating a post-infectious cognitive effect. | Cognitive impairment was reported after SARS-CoV-2 infection as part of the post-COVID symptom burden assessed in the cited meta-analysis. | Cognitive impairment is discussed within post-acute sequelae of SARS-CoV-2 infection, indicating an association between prior infection and later cognitive dysfunction. | The cited Brain Communications article describes cognitive impairment following SARS-CoV-2 infection. | The introduction states that some recovered patients from acute SARS-CoV-2 infection exhibit cognitive impairment. | SARS-CoV-2 infection is described as contributing to cognitive impairment, including as part of brain fog. | The passage discusses cognitive deficits following SARS-CoV-2 infection, indicating that infection is associated with later cognitive impairment. | Postacute sequelae of SARS-CoV-2 infection are linked to persistent cognitive impairment. | The passage supports monitoring for cognitive impairment after SARS-CoV-2 infection, indicating a post-infectious association with cognitive deficits. | The text links SARS-CoV-2 infection with cognitive impairment observed shortly after infection. | The text describes individuals experiencing cognitive impairment one week after SARS-CoV-2 infection, supporting a post-infectious association. | Cognitive impairment is described as one of the most frequently reported and disabling persistent symptoms following SARS-CoV-2 infection. | The study explored whether SARS-CoV-2 positive status was associated with differences in cognitive-task scores compared with controls. The excerpt does not provide the complete cognitive result or specify affected cognitive subdomains.</data>
      <data key="d11">0.95</data>
      <data key="d12">1</data>
      <data key="d13">86f9ab26-f1be-4c98-8916-d978196e27f5_chunk_1</data>
      <data key="d14">86f9ab26-f1be-4c98-8916-d978196e27f5,5f73e261-6d67-4a51-a17e-d25381a17114,516f7ce3-73d5-40bf-a988-9d3371580712,037d79c5-a241-4f38-8c08-cac6a4c5c0ff,5e787f6f-1547-4d32-aa5f-4a0f4d841129,41fb90c0-1594-48bd-b974-39b7d20799d4,5265c67c-1282-4966-b4a4-87b9ba0eed26,af5ff8eb-8135-49d4-81fd-6de4008d60d8,fe2c70b3-e143-43f7-bbf4-4cdc081212c3,f40f9b83-7586-4b71-869a-2ceb88840fbe</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="SARS-COV-2 INFECTION" target="COGNITIVE DYSFUNCTION">
      <data key="d7">SARS-COV-2 INFECTION</data>
      <data key="d8">COGNITIVE DYSFUNCTION</data>
      <data key="d9">AFFECTS</data>
      <data key="d10">Persistent or newly developed cognitive dysfunction more than three months after SARS-CoV-2 infection is described as part of the clinical presentation relevant to neurological Post-COVID Syndrome. | The inclusion criteria connect confirmed SARS-CoV-2 infection with later evaluation of cognitive function, reflecting the review's interest in infection-associated post-acute cognitive outcomes. The passage does not quantify the effect. | Prior studies cited in the text report cognitive decline and persistent neuropsychological deficits after SARS-CoV-2 infection. | SARS-CoV-2 is presented as the viral exposure for which vaccination-induced antibodies may not be sufficient to prevent cognitive dysfunction. | The passage explicitly lists cognitive dysfunction among central nervous system manifestations observed in patients with SARS-CoV-2 infection. | A history of probable or confirmed SARS-CoV-2 infection is the antecedent infection for post-COVID-19 condition, in which cognitive dysfunction is listed as a common symptom. | DigiCog was developed to investigate cognitive functioning in adults following SARS-CoV-2 infection, indicating the study's focus on possible post-infectious cognitive effects. | Cognitive functioning is assessed following SARS-CoV-2 infection, supporting a post-infectious relationship between infection and later cognitive outcomes.</data>
      <data key="d11">0.9</data>
      <data key="d12">1</data>
      <data key="d13">86f9ab26-f1be-4c98-8916-d978196e27f5_chunk_1</data>
      <data key="d14">86f9ab26-f1be-4c98-8916-d978196e27f5,4b18c63f-2f87-46c1-a3ae-8c4337490271,5f73e261-6d67-4a51-a17e-d25381a17114,b24179f1-ebda-47e4-9ae7-42c367d15e44,c69cfb78-198c-48f8-b6ac-7bf289971093,ed7d7537-d658-4242-92f7-fb95689c31b6,fe2c70b3-e143-43f7-bbf4-4cdc081212c3</data>
    </edge>
