      <data key="d8">AGE</data>
      <data key="d9">CONFOUNDED_BY</data>
      <data key="d10">Scalar scores were computed according to age, indicating that age-related variation in cognitive test performance was controlled in the analysis. | Permutation tests and partial correlations adjusted for age, indicating that age was treated as a covariate when analyzing cognitive and health-score associations. | Age was a potential confounder for cognitive comparisons, but the groups reportedly did not differ in age. | Age is included as a control regressor in augmented models because age differences between hospitalized and non-hospitalized patients could bias comparisons of cognitive outcomes. | Age could influence cognitive performance and biomarker levels; the control group was age-matched to reduce confounding. | Age partially explains systematic differences in cognitive task performance related to device type and operating system. | Age differed significantly across groups and may confound cognitive comparisons. | Age was a potential demographic confounder but was matched across groups. | ANCOVA models comparing PCC and non-PCC participants control for age, indicating that age is treated as a potential confounder of group differences in cognitive performance. | Age was included as an independent variable, consistent with adjustment for its potential confounding influence on cognitive or neurobiological outcomes.</data>
      <data key="d11">0.86</data>
      <data key="d12">1</data>
