      <data key="d9">AFFECTS</data>
      <data key="d10">Participants with higher depressive symptoms showed lower subjective cognitive functioning ratings, with the pattern most prominent among those with the highest depressive symptom levels. | Depressive symptoms bins were associated with T3-Pre subjective cognitive functioning difference scores, with the highest depressive-symptom bin showing more negative change than lower-symptom bins. | Depressive symptoms represent a non-cognitive factor that may explain or contribute to subjective cognitive functioning differences over time. | Depressive symptoms stratified cognitive-functioning change: only participants with the lowest depressive symptoms at T1 showed declines in subjective cognitive functioning, while other groups showed no difference or slight increases.</data>
