      <data key="d7">COGNITIVE IMPAIRMENT</data>
      <data key="d8">FATIGUE</data>
      <data key="d9">CONFOUNDED_BY</data>
      <data key="d10">Fatigue is a persistent post-COVID symptom and may influence cognitive performance or subjective cognitive complaints, making it a potential confounder when interpreting cognitive impairment after COVID-19. | Fatigue is listed alongside cognitive impairment and may influence cognitive symptoms or perceived cognitive dysfunction in post-COVID contexts. The excerpt does not confirm formal confounder adjustment. | Fatigue persisted during follow-up and may influence cognitive performance or cognitive profiles, although cited findings regarding fatigue and cognition were mixed. | Fatigue was prevalent in PACS and evaluated as a possible factor related to cognitive performance, but fatigue-based stratification did not show significant cognitive test differences in the current analysis. | Fatigue may influence cognitive impairment broadly and is described as affecting both cognitive and psychiatric symptom domains. | Fatigue is closely linked to cognitive impairment in post-COVID-19 syndrome and may independently affect measured cognitive performance. | Fatigue is cited alongside cognitive impairment in post-COVID-19 syndrome and may complicate interpretation of cognitive outcomes because fatigue can independently reduce cognitive performance or increase cognitive complaints. | Fatigue is listed as a relevant keyword and may complicate interpretation of cognitive impairment in long-COVID contexts.</data>
