      <data key="d9">CONFOUNDED_BY</data>
      <data key="d10">Depression was examined as a mental health stratification variable, but no significant cognitive test differences were observed for depression categories in the current PACS analysis. | Depression is reported as making a unique contribution to cognitive impairment in post-acute sequelae of SARS-CoV-2 infection, suggesting that cognitive findings may be partly explained or modified by depressive symptoms. | Depression is described as making a unique contribution to cognitive impairment in PASC, meaning depressive symptoms may independently account for part of the observed cognitive deficits. | Depression is a plausible confounder for post-COVID cognitive findings because depressive symptoms can independently impair cognition or increase cognitive complaints. The excerpt supports depression as a relevant neuropsychiatric topic but does not provide a direct post-COVID cognitive association. | Depression co-occurs with cognitive impairment in the cited COVID-19 survivor follow-up study and may contribute to observed cognitive impairment. | The study title links cognitive impairment with depression-related factors in adolescents with suicidal and self-injury behaviors. | Because depression can independently contribute to cognitive symptoms and is associated with dementia risk, it may confound analyses of cognitive impairment. | Depression is common among dementia patients and can affect cognitive performance, making it a potential confounder for cognitive impairment outcomes. | Depression is reported alongside cognitive impairment and may influence cognitive complaints or performance, making it a potential confounding or co-occurring factor rather than a proven causal mechanism in this text.</data>
