      <data key="d9">CONFOUNDED_BY</data>
      <data key="d10">Major depressive disorder is associated with altered connectivity and disrupted correlations with executive function, making it a potential confounder for executive-function outcomes. | Executive-function impairment is described as a key feature of major depressive disorder, so major depressive disorder may confound interpretation of executive-function deficits. | Executive function impairments are studied in patients with major depressive disorder, a condition that may itself affect executive functioning. | Observed executive function deficits in cognitive studies may be confounded by major depressive disorder because the disorder is independently associated with executive dysfunction.</data>
