      <data key="d8">DEPRESSIVE SYMPTOMS</data>
      <data key="d9">AFFECTS</data>
      <data key="d10">Perceived stress was described as a stable risk factor for depressive symptoms in elderly individuals and contributed to depressive symptom prediction in older women. | Perceived stress is proposed as part of a pathway contributing to the prediction of depressive symptoms in the elderly. | Longitudinal studies reported that older adults' perceived stress can predict subsequent depressive symptoms. | The study explored how perceived stress contributes to the prediction of depressive symptoms in older adults. | Existing studies cited in the text link perceived stress with depressive symptoms in older adults. | The primary mediation model tested path b, representing the relationship between perceived stress and depressive symptoms after controlling for regional GMV. | Perceived stress showed a significant positive association with depressive symptoms. | Higher perceived stress was positively associated with greater depressive symptoms. | Perceived stress was reported as a significant predictor of depressive symptoms in older women. | Perceived stress was implicated as a psychological factor contributing to depressive symptoms in older women. | The cited literature reports an association between perceived stress and depressive symptoms, suggesting that perceived stress may influence or co-occur with depressive symptom burden. | As a mediator, perceived stress is implicated in the pathway linking orbitofrontal cortex grey matter volume to depressive symptoms. | Perceived stress is implicated as a pathway through which orbitofrontal cortex grey matter volume is associated with depressive symptom burden. | Perceived stress is described as a stable risk factor that can contribute to depressive symptoms in elderly individuals. | Multiple cross-sectional and longitudinal studies are described as showing that higher perceived stress is positively associated with, and can predict, subsequent depressive symptoms in older people. | Perceived stress is described as a stable risk factor that predicts subsequent depressive symptoms in older people. | Path b in the mediation model represented the relationship between perceived stress and depressive symptoms after controlling for regional GMV. | Higher perceived stress was positively associated with more depressive symptoms. | Perceived stress was positively associated with depressive symptoms and served as the proximal psychological predictor in the proposed pathway. | Perceived stress was positively correlated with depressive symptoms.</data>
      <data key="d11">0.95</data>
      <data key="d12">2</data>
