      <data key="d8">DEMENTIA</data>
      <data key="d9">COMPARED_TO</data>
      <data key="d10">Mild cognitive impairment is described as an intermediate state between normal cognitive aging and dementia. | Intervention studies and meta-analyses compare or jointly examine participants with mild cognitive impairment and dementia. | Mild cognitive impairment and dementia are referenced together as cognitive impairment conditions considered in comparative evidence on exercise interventions. | Prior evidence referenced combined cognitive and physical exercise training in older adults with mild cognitive impairment or dementia. | The systematic review includes both community-dwelling people with mild cognitive impairment and people with dementia when considering medicine self-management interventions. | The cited systematic review considers adults with mild cognitive impairment and adults with dementia as distinct cognitive-status populations in the context of combined exercise and cognitive interventions. | Mild cognitive impairment and dementia are jointly referenced as clinical severity categories in reviews examining cognitive outcomes. | The guideline citation distinguishes mild cognitive impairment within the broader context of dementia and cognitive impairment diagnosis and treatment.</data>
      <data key="d11">0.82</data>
      <data key="d12">1</data>
      <data key="d13">c88820fc-d2e7-4693-97b2-fd50c3d1e971_chunk_2</data>
      <data key="d14">c88820fc-d2e7-4693-97b2-fd50c3d1e971</data>
    </edge>
