      <data key="d8">COGNITIVE FUNCTION</data>
      <data key="d9">AFFECTS</data>
      <data key="d10">Mild cognitive impairment represents a clinically relevant decline affecting cognitive function in older adults. | Mild cognitive impairment is associated with altered cognitive function and is a target condition for interventions aimed at improving cognitive outcomes. | The cited review focuses on cognitive function among older adults with mild cognitive impairment, linking this impairment state to cognitive-function outcomes. | Mild cognitive impairment denotes reduced cognitive function in the older-adult populations examined in the Tai Chi meta-analyses. | Mild cognitive impairment is associated with reduced cognitive function and is a population in which cognitive outcomes are evaluated. | Mild cognitive impairment represents a clinical state involving altered cognitive function and is a target condition for cognitive monitoring. | Mild cognitive impairment involves measurable cognitive decline, and the intervention goal is to stabilize or improve cognitive function during this stage.</data>
      <data key="d11">0.88</data>
      <data key="d12">1</data>
      <data key="d13">c88820fc-d2e7-4693-97b2-fd50c3d1e971_chunk_10</data>
      <data key="d14">c88820fc-d2e7-4693-97b2-fd50c3d1e971,8c67a44e-7846-44b0-8872-5359629554cd,edc67c59-b539-46b8-9064-e280c6f86904</data>
    </edge>
