      <data key="d8">STRESS</data>
      <data key="d9">CONFOUNDED_BY</data>
      <data key="d10">Stress can affect cognitive functioning independently of COVID-19, making it a potential confounder when attributing cognitive deficits to infection. | Stress is referenced through cited pandemic-related cognitive-functioning literature and may independently influence measured cognitive dysfunction. | Cognitive functioning during a pandemic may be influenced by stress, making stress a potential confounder when interpreting cognitive outcomes in the COVID-19 pandemic context. | Stress is discussed in relation to cognitive functioning during the pandemic and may influence cognition independently of respiratory infection, making it a potential confounding factor for cognitive outcomes. | The cited title links stress with cognitive functioning during a pandemic, supporting stress as a plausible confounder of cognitive outcomes in the COVID-19 context. No quantified effect size or healthcare-worker cohort is provided in the excerpt. | Stress may confound cognitive functioning because elevated stress can independently impair cognitive and behavioral performance.</data>
      <data key="d11">0.88</data>
