    <edge source="COGNITIVE DYSFUNCTION" target="ANXIETY">
      <data key="d7">COGNITIVE DYSFUNCTION</data>
      <data key="d8">ANXIETY</data>
      <data key="d9">CONFOUNDED_BY</data>
      <data key="d10">Anxiety is referenced through cited cognition literature and may contribute to or confound cognitive dysfunction attributed to infection. | The cited literature links anxiety with cognition, supporting anxiety as a factor that may independently affect cognitive functioning and confound cognitive outcome assessments. | Persistent anxiety may help explain why slight cognitive improvements did not strongly affect clinical outcomes or quality of life. | Anxiety may influence cognitive performance or subjective cognitive complaints in post-COVID assessments, although the excerpt lists it as part of the broader neuropsychiatric evidence rather than as a confirmed confounder. | Anxiety may influence cognitive performance, but baseline anxiety measures were similar between groups. | Anxiety may confound cognitive performance findings because it is independently associated with cognitive outcomes. | Anxiety was assessed as a factor that may influence post-COVID cognitive scores, since anxiety symptoms can affect attention, working memory, and subjective cognitive complaints. | Anxiety may confound cognitive functioning because anxiety symptoms can independently affect concentration and working memory.</data>
      <data key="d11">0.78</data>
      <data key="d12">2</data>
      <data key="d13">4b18c63f-2f87-46c1-a3ae-8c4337490271_chunk_34</data>
      <data key="d14">4b18c63f-2f87-46c1-a3ae-8c4337490271,5f73e261-6d67-4a51-a17e-d25381a17114,037d79c5-a241-4f38-8c08-cac6a4c5c0ff,338d5c8e-550b-436e-8de2-d7967ecb69eb,9ac1c295-0116-47ef-a0b9-9e8531f02406,3b4ebbd4-4db3-4d69-8990-c98fd16192ff,531dd921-3435-419e-b587-7dfeebb99084</data>
