      <data key="d14">4b18c63f-2f87-46c1-a3ae-8c4337490271</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="COGNITIVE DYSFUNCTION" target="DEPRESSION">
      <data key="d7">COGNITIVE FUNCTIONING</data>
      <data key="d8">DEPRESSION</data>
      <data key="d9">CONFOUNDED_BY</data>
      <data key="d10">Depression is reported as prevalent during the COVID-19 pandemic and can independently affect cognitive functioning, making it a potential confounder of cognitive outcomes. | Depression was assessed as a Long COVID symptom and can independently affect perceived or measured cognitive function. | Depression may influence cognitive performance, but baseline depression measures were similar between groups. | Depression may confound cognitive performance findings because it is independently associated with cognitive outcomes. | Depression was evaluated as a potential influencing factor because depressive symptoms can independently affect cognitive performance and subjective cognitive complaints. | Depression can confound interpretation of cognitive-function outcomes because depressive symptoms may independently affect attention, motivation, processing speed, and subjective cognition. | Depression may confound cognitive functioning because depressive symptoms can independently reduce attention, processing speed, and executive performance.</data>
      <data key="d11">0.78</data>
      <data key="d12">2</data>
      <data key="d13">4b18c63f-2f87-46c1-a3ae-8c4337490271_chunk_57</data>
      <data key="d14">4b18c63f-2f87-46c1-a3ae-8c4337490271,5e787f6f-1547-4d32-aa5f-4a0f4d841129,338d5c8e-550b-436e-8de2-d7967ecb69eb,9ac1c295-0116-47ef-a0b9-9e8531f02406,3b4ebbd4-4db3-4d69-8990-c98fd16192ff,be8e1faa-13e6-4111-8446-ccf9848530c7,531dd921-3435-419e-b587-7dfeebb99084</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="COGNITIVE DYSFUNCTION" target="PSYCHOLOGICAL DISTRESS">
      <data key="d7">COGNITIVE FUNCTIONING</data>
      <data key="d8">PSYCHOLOGICAL DISTRESS</data>
      <data key="d9">CONFOUNDED_BY</data>
      <data key="d10">Psychological distress during the COVID-19 pandemic may contribute to cognitive difficulties independently of respiratory infection status, potentially confounding cognitive findings. | Psychological distress is referenced in a COVID-19-related healthcare worker study and may confound cognitive outcomes because distress can influence attention, memory, and subjective cognitive complaints. The excerpt does not report a direct cognitive effect.</data>
      <data key="d11">0.6</data>
      <data key="d12">2</data>
      <data key="d13">4b18c63f-2f87-46c1-a3ae-8c4337490271_chunk_57</data>
      <data key="d14">4b18c63f-2f87-46c1-a3ae-8c4337490271,b24179f1-ebda-47e4-9ae7-42c367d15e44</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="COGNITIVE DYSFUNCTION" target="COGNITIVE RESERVE">
      <data key="d7">COGNITIVE FUNCTIONING</data>
      <data key="d8">COGNITIVE RESERVE</data>
      <data key="d9">CONFOUNDED_BY</data>
      <data key="d10">Cognitive reserve may influence baseline cognition and resilience to impairment, thereby confounding observed variation in cognitive functioning across individuals. | The study explores cognitive reserve as a factor that may explain interindividual variability in post-infectious cognitive functioning. | Cognitive reserve may alter vulnerability to cognitive impairment, so differences in reserve could confound interpretation of post-COVID cognitive dysfunction.</data>
      <data key="d11">0.81</data>
      <data key="d12">2</data>
      <data key="d13">4b18c63f-2f87-46c1-a3ae-8c4337490271_chunk_57</data>
      <data key="d14">4b18c63f-2f87-46c1-a3ae-8c4337490271,fe2c70b3-e143-43f7-bbf4-4cdc081212c3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="COGNITIVE DYSFUNCTION" target="LONG-TERM">
      <data key="d7">COGNITIVE DYSFUNCTION</data>
      <data key="d8">LONG-TERM</data>
      <data key="d9">PERSISTS_AT</data>
      <data key="d10">Persistent neurologic symptoms and cognitive dysfunction in COVID-19 long-haulers support persistence of cognitive dysfunction into the long-term post-infection period. | Declines or impacts in cognitive functioning are framed as long-term outcomes.</data>
      <data key="d11">0.82</data>
      <data key="d12">1</data>
      <data key="d13">5f73e261-6d67-4a51-a17e-d25381a17114_chunk_3</data>
      <data key="d14">5f73e261-6d67-4a51-a17e-d25381a17114,9ac1c295-0116-47ef-a0b9-9e8531f02406</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="COGNITIVE DYSFUNCTION" target="NEUROPSYCHOLOGICAL TESTS">
      <data key="d7">COGNITIVE FUNCTIONING</data>
      <data key="d8">NEUROPSYCHOLOGICAL TESTS</data>
      <data key="d9">MEASURED_BY</data>
      <data key="d10">Overall cognitive functioning was evaluated through neuropsychological tests, including assessment against normal threshold values. | Cognitive function was assessed using neuropsychological tests, which were available for 191 participants. | Neuropsychological testing is the type of diagnostic cognitive assessment that was not used in this study. Therefore, cognitive functioning was not objectively measured by neuropsychological testing, limiting interpretation of the patient-reported cognitive findings. | The DigiCog study uses neuropsychological testing to assess cognitive functioning in PCC.</data>
      <data key="d11">0.85</data>
      <data key="d12">1</data>
      <data key="d13">5f73e261-6d67-4a51-a17e-d25381a17114_chunk_27</data>
      <data key="d14">5f73e261-6d67-4a51-a17e-d25381a17114,b0310b04-4fe9-4dc6-8bb6-5040028e4f04,63b31c15-b9bb-4a8e-a6dd-082b7c1908fa,fe2c70b3-e143-43f7-bbf4-4cdc081212c3</data>
