      <data key="d8">SOCIAL ISOLATION</data>
      <data key="d9">CONFOUNDED_BY</data>
      <data key="d10">Social isolation can negatively influence cognitive functioning independently of COVID-19 infection, making it a key confounding factor for interpreting pandemic-related cognitive outcomes. | The reported decrease in cognitive functioning was related to social isolation, indicating that reduced social contact may contribute to or partially explain observed cognitive reductions. | Social isolation may account for impaired cognitive function observed during lockdown and should be considered when attributing cognitive changes to the pandemic or infection context. | Because social isolation can independently impair cognition, it may confound interpretations that attribute cognitive changes during the COVID-19 pandemic solely to infection or other pandemic-related factors. | Social isolation is discussed in the COVID-19 older-adult context and could confound cognitive or psychological outcomes, although the excerpt does not provide a direct empirical association with cognitive function. | Social isolation is discussed as having physical and mental health consequences in the COVID-19 context. Although the excerpt does not directly state a cognitive effect, social isolation is a plausible independent psychosocial influence that may confound cognitive outcomes.</data>
      <data key="d11">0.88</data>
      <data key="d12">1</data>
