    <edge source="OBJECTIVE COGNITIVE FUNCTION" target="DEPRESSIVE SYMPTOMS">
      <data key="d7">SUBJECTIVE COGNITIVE FUNCTIONING</data>
      <data key="d8">DEPRESSIVE SYMPTOMS</data>
      <data key="d9">CONFOUNDED_BY</data>
      <data key="d10">The longer-term pandemic impact on subjective cognitive functioning was related to depressive symptoms, which may influence self-reported cognitive complaints. | Depressive symptoms are assessed as potential vulnerability factors and may influence subjective cognitive functioning, making them relevant when interpreting self-reported cognitive outcomes. | Depressive symptoms may contribute to subjective cognitive complaints and are relevant to interpretation of cognitive self-reports. | Depressive symptoms were included as a covariate and potential vulnerability factor because mood symptoms can influence subjective cognitive complaints. | Temporal changes in subjective cognitive functioning varied by depressive symptom level, with the most prominent pattern reported for participants with the highest cognitive failures and depressive symptoms. | Declines in subjective cognitive functioning were accompanied by depressive symptoms. | The association between subjective and objective cognitive functioning is described as mixed, especially among adults with depressive symptoms, indicating that depressive symptoms may complicate interpretation of subjective cognitive ratings.</data>
      <data key="d11">0.9</data>
      <data key="d12">1</data>
      <data key="d13">9ac1c295-0116-47ef-a0b9-9e8531f02406_chunk_0</data>
      <data key="d14">9ac1c295-0116-47ef-a0b9-9e8531f02406</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="OBJECTIVE COGNITIVE FUNCTION" target="ANXIETY SYMPTOMS">
      <data key="d7">SUBJECTIVE COGNITIVE FUNCTIONING</data>
      <data key="d8">ANXIETY SYMPTOMS</data>
      <data key="d9">CONFOUNDED_BY</data>
      <data key="d10">The longer-term pandemic impact on subjective cognitive functioning was related to anxiety symptoms, which may affect perceived cognitive functioning. | Anxiety symptoms were related to greater subjective cognitive difficulties and may confound interpretation of self-reported cognitive functioning during the pandemic. | Anxiety symptoms are assessed as potential vulnerability factors and may influence subjective cognitive complaints independently of the broader pandemic context. | Anxiety symptoms may influence self-reported cognitive complaints independently of infection status. | Anxiety symptoms measured at T3 were included as a covariate and potential vulnerability factor. | Temporal changes in subjective cognitive functioning varied by anxiety symptom level. | Interpretation of pandemic-related cognitive decline may be confounded or modified by anxiety symptoms, because anxiety symptoms were associated with declines in subjective cognitive functioning.</data>
      <data key="d11">0.86</data>
      <data key="d12">1</data>
      <data key="d13">9ac1c295-0116-47ef-a0b9-9e8531f02406_chunk_0</data>
      <data key="d14">9ac1c295-0116-47ef-a0b9-9e8531f02406</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="OBJECTIVE COGNITIVE FUNCTION" target="LONG-TERM FOLLOW-UP">
      <data key="d7">SUBJECTIVE COGNITIVE FUNCTIONING</data>
      <data key="d8">LONGER-TERM FOLLOW-UP</data>
      <data key="d9">PERSISTS_AT</data>
      <data key="d10">The text reports no full recovery from the first wave, indicating that effects on subjective cognitive functioning persisted into the longer-term follow-up period. | Subjective cognitive functioning is considered in the context of the pandemic's 'longer-term impact,' indicating that the outcome was assessed or interpreted at an extended timepoint rather than solely during the immediate acute pandemic period.</data>
      <data key="d11">0.82</data>
      <data key="d12">1</data>
      <data key="d13">9ac1c295-0116-47ef-a0b9-9e8531f02406_chunk_1</data>
      <data key="d14">9ac1c295-0116-47ef-a0b9-9e8531f02406</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="OBJECTIVE COGNITIVE FUNCTION" target="DEPRESSIVE AND ANXIETY SYMPTOMS">
      <data key="d7">SUBJECTIVE COGNITIVE FUNCTIONING</data>
      <data key="d8">DEPRESSIVE AND ANXIETY SYMPTOMS</data>
      <data key="d9">CONFOUNDED_BY</data>
      <data key="d10">Depressive and anxiety symptoms were related to the longer-term impact on subjective cognitive functioning, suggesting that mental health symptoms may partly explain or influence self-reported cognitive difficulties. | Because depressive and anxiety symptoms moderated changes in cognitive functioning at the start of the pandemic, they may have influenced subjective cognitive functioning outcomes. | Depressive and anxiety symptoms are identified as vulnerability factors that may influence subjective cognitive functioning during the pandemic.</data>
      <data key="d11">0.86</data>
      <data key="d12">1</data>
      <data key="d13">9ac1c295-0116-47ef-a0b9-9e8531f02406_chunk_1</data>
      <data key="d14">9ac1c295-0116-47ef-a0b9-9e8531f02406</data>
    </edge>
