      <data key="d8">RESILIENCE</data>
      <data key="d9">CONFOUNDED_BY</data>
      <data key="d10">Resilience is identified as a protective factor that may buffer or explain differences in subjective cognitive functioning during the pandemic. | Resilience is identified as a potential protective factor that may influence subjective cognitive functioning in the pandemic survey context. | Resilience was included as a covariate and potential protective factor in the repeated measures models. | Resilience was included as a covariate and protective factor, meaning variation in resilience was statistically considered in relation to subjective cognitive functioning.</data>
      <data key="d11">0.72</data>
      <data key="d12">2</data>
      <data key="d13">9ac1c295-0116-47ef-a0b9-9e8531f02406_chunk_10</data>
      <data key="d14">9ac1c295-0116-47ef-a0b9-9e8531f02406</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="OBJECTIVE COGNITIVE FUNCTION" target="AGE">
      <data key="d7">SUBJECTIVE COGNITIVE FUNCTIONING</data>
      <data key="d8">AGE</data>
      <data key="d9">CONFOUNDED_BY</data>
      <data key="d10">Age is a demographic variable highlighted by the age-distribution figure and may influence subjective cognitive functioning, so it is relevant as a demographic confounding factor. | Age is a relevant demographic factor for interpreting subjective cognitive functioning. | Age was included as a covariate in repeated measures ANOVAs analyzing changes in subjective cognitive functioning and wellbeing. | Age was included as a covariate in the repeated-measures ANOVA, indicating that age-related variation was statistically controlled when examining subjective cognitive functioning.</data>
      <data key="d11">0.74</data>
      <data key="d12">2</data>
      <data key="d13">9ac1c295-0116-47ef-a0b9-9e8531f02406_chunk_14</data>
