      <data key="d9">MEASURED_BY</data>
      <data key="d10">The Stroop task's incongruent and distracting stimulus conditions require inhibition of competing or irrelevant responses, making it relevant for assessing inhibitory control. | Inhibitory control is assessed by the Stroop task, especially in incongruent trials where participants must overcome the impulse to read the upper word. | The Stroop task engages inhibitory control and is commonly used to assess this cognitive control domain. | The Stroop task engages inhibitory control because participants must ignore conflicting information and focus on the correct response. | The Stroop task engages inhibitory control by requiring suppression of automatic responses, supporting its use for assessing this executive function. | The Stroop task, including congruent and incongruent trials, was used in the EEG/ERP protocol and commonly measures inhibitory control.</data>
