      <data key="d9">AFFECTS</data>
      <data key="d10">Long COVID is presented in the cited literature as a long-term consequence associated with COVID-19. | Long COVID denotes persistent or post-acute sequelae following COVID-19, as implied by the cited epidemiology study title. | Long COVID is defined by symptoms of COVID-19 that persist beyond the acute phase and cannot be attributed to another illness. | Long COVID is discussed as a post-acute condition related to COVID-19 for which further study of ovarian impairment is needed. | Long COVID is presented as a post-acute condition related to prior COVID-19 infection. The passage does not specify a follow-up timepoint. | Long COVID is represented as a post-acute condition persisting after COVID-19, although the excerpt provides no specific follow-up timepoint. | Long COVID is described as post-acute sequelae of COVID-19, indicating that it occurs after the acute COVID-19 phase. | Long COVID is referenced as a post-acute condition related to prior COVID-19 infection; the relationship is represented conservatively because the allowed relationship types do not include 'sequela of'. | Long COVID is presented as persistent symptoms among survivors after COVID-19, linking the post-acute condition to the original respiratory infection. | The UK study compared healthcare impact in a long COVID cohort with a COVID-19 only cohort, reporting higher median annual healthcare impact for long COVID. | Long COVID is discussed as a chronic condition stemming from COVID-19 and as part of the long-term economic impact of the infection. No cognitive comparison group or effect estimate is provided. | Long COVID is described as a condition occurring after COVID-19 infection; the excerpt links it conceptually to prior COVID-19 rather than reporting a direct cognitive outcome comparison. | Long-COVID is presented as a post-acute condition following COVID-19 infection. The excerpt does not provide a specific follow-up timepoint or duration threshold. | The cited references distinguish economic and productivity impacts attributed to Long COVID from costs associated with acute COVID-19 care, including inpatient COVID-19 costs and chronic illness stemming from COVID-19.</data>
