      <data key="d10">The study examines whether exposure to school-related stress is associated with an increased tendency to develop PTSD symptoms. | School-related stress exposure was included in moderation models examining maladaptive outcomes, including PTSD symptom severity. | Higher levels of school-related stress exposure were associated with higher PTSD symptom severity. | The regression model testing school-related stress exposure, cognitive flexibility, and their interaction as predictors of PTSD symptoms had R2 = 0.13, F(5, 143) = 4.42, p &lt; 0.001. | The association between school-related stress exposure and PTSD symptoms was statistically adjusted for age. | The association between school-related stress exposure and PTSD symptoms was statistically adjusted for gender. | The current study found a significant positive correlation between school-related stress exposure and PTSD symptom severity. | Greater school-related stress exposure was associated with increased PTSD symptom severity, specifically among educators with low cognitive flexibility.</data>
      <data key="d11">0.95</data>
      <data key="d12">1</data>
      <data key="d13">b1a08202-3b7d-4086-b35d-61754b0def57_chunk_15</data>
